{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi1e6rWbDGCxDW18b8L3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016x8UVJ1NZ3gxgNVQVMzj3F","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-070229__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","offset":400,"limit":450},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14786,"cache_read_input_tokens":22623,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14786},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1931e3d-b97b-4f9d-8b9d-c7660f7740b5","uuid":"d4ba29af-dd36-45e9-b3bb-93862ee02690","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:02:40.308Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi1e6Z9H874nhdu7byiP","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_016x8UVJ1NZ3gxgNVQVMzj3F":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-070229__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","offset":400,"limit":450}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016x8UVJ1NZ3gxgNVQVMzj3F","type":"tool_result","content":"400\t### 5.4 Floor painting (`floorTex(x, y, tag)`)\n401\t- Grass in mown stripes 5 yards wide, alternating two greens per stadium, with per-pixel noise (`E.hash2`,\n402\t  `E.noise2`), like the SPORTS kit's floor.\n403\t- White paint for yard lines (a band 1.2 units wide), hashes (0.8 yd long), and the sidelines and end lines (2 units).\n404\t  Numbers are the 5×7 font's digit bitmaps scaled to 2.5 yd tall, sampled per world pixel. End-zone letters and the\n405\t  midfield logo work the same way from their bitmaps (§12.8).\n406\t- Mud (`mud` tag and noise blotches) is brown and wet-dark. Ice is pale blue with streaks. Divots are decals added at\n407\t  runtime (§11.5).\n408\t- Night stadiums: `[r, g, b, glow]` texels make the Afterglow Arena's yard lines glow. A wear pass darkens the grass\n409\t  between the hashes.\n410\t- The floor bakes once per view (engine behavior). View changes (Kick Cam, replays) rebake. That's fine: cut, don't\n411\t  rotate continuously (§11.1).\n412\t\n413\t### 5.5 Dressing per stadium\n414\tEach stadium (§18.2) lists its props: shapes and `r.prop`s on the far sideline (benches, coolers, the Gatorade-style\n415\tcooler table, TV cameras, light towers at the 4 corners), the near sideline (team benches, the cheerleader squad, the\n416\tmascot, the chain gang with down markers that move to the line of scrimmage and the line to gain, photographers),\n417\tbehind the end zones (pyro cannons, banners, a parked pickup or an airboat), and the backdrop layers. Aim for 3–8\n418\tprops on any screen (the API's look target). Light towers carry `lights: [...]` that light the field at night.\n419\t\n420\t### 5.6 Views\n421\t| Id | Made as | Used for |\n422\t|---|---|---|\n423\t| `broadcast` | `new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 50, 1, 1)` | the default for play |\n424\t| `sky` | `new E.View('sky', 'Sky Cam', 0, 65, 1, 1)` | option (V cycles; Settings, Camera) |\n425\t| `blimp` | `E.VIEWS.topdown` | option |\n426\t| `kickcam` | `new E.View('kickcam', 'Kick Cam', ±90, 32, 1, 1)`, turned so the kick goes up the screen | FGs and PATs (and kickoffs from behind the kicker for 1.2 s) |\n427\t| `field` | `new E.View('field', 'Field Level', 0, 25, 1, 1)` (the brawler pitch) | replays, celebrations, cutscenes on the field |\n428\t| `iso` | `E.VIEWS.iso` | replays |\n429\t| `reverse` | `new E.View('reverse', 'Reverse Angle', 180, 45, 1, 1)` | replays (the far stands cut low) |\n430\t\n431\tSteer with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`, so pushing up always means up the screen in every view, including\n432\tthe Kick Cam. `E.charView` draws rigs from a lower angle in pitch 40–89 views automatically, and that's wanted.\n433\t\n434\t---\n435\t\n436\t## 6. Football rules and the match flow (`30-sim.js`)\n437\t\n438\t### 6.1 The match state machine\n439\t```\n440\tCOIN_TOSS ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP ─▶ KICK_LIVE ─▶ (DEAD) ─▶ PLAYCALL ─▶ PRESNAP ─▶ LIVE ─▶ DEAD ─▶ ...\n441\tDEAD branches: score ─▶ CELEBRATE ─▶ [REPLAY] ─▶ PAT_CALL ─▶ PAT ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP\n442\t               turnover / downs ─▶ PLAYCALL (other side)    quarter end ─▶ QUARTER_BREAK\n443\t               halftime ─▶ HALFTIME ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP        end of regulation, tied ─▶ OVERTIME ─▶ ...\n444\t               final ─▶ FINAL (results)\n445\t```\n446\t- **COIN_TOSS** (1.5 s): the home mascot flips a giant coin (cosmetic). The winner is `FF.rng.sim`'s, or the\n447\t  scenario's. The winner receives.\n448\t- **PLAYCALL**: both sides choose on the play-call UI (§14.5). The CPU chooses after a human-like 0.6–1.4 s delay.\n449\t  The timer is `T.playcallTime` (12 s). When it runs out, the COACH SAYS play is picked. Kicking situations show the\n450\t  special cards.\n451\t- **PRESNAP**: athletes jog to their formation spots (1.2 s), then the line sets. The offense hikes with b1. The CPU\n452\t  hikes after 0.5–2.0 s. A human gets an auto-hike when `T.autoHike` (8 s) runs out, with a visible play clock from\n453\t  5 s. The defense's human may move their defender behind the line. The QB barks his cadence (§13.4).\n454\t- **LIVE**: the play. It ends with a **dead ball**: a carrier down (tackled, or a knee or body touching the ground\n455\t  after a dive), out of bounds, an incomplete pass, a score, a pile-up resolved, a kick dead (touchback, out of\n456\t  bounds, downed), or a kneel.\n457\t- **DEAD** (1.2 s, or longer for presentation): refs signal, the chain gang moves, late hits are possible for\n458\t  `T.lateHitWindow` (1.2 s), then the spot and downs update.\n459\t- **CELEBRATE** (after a TD, 3.5 s, skippable with b1): the scorer's dance (§10.6), pyro, the crowd, the announcer.\n460\t  Then **REPLAY** if the Replays setting allows (§11.2).\n461\t- **PAT_CALL**: KICK XP (1 point, the snap from the 15) or GO FOR 2 (a normal play from the 3).\n462\t- **QUARTER_BREAK** (1.5 s, a card \"END OF THE 1ST\"). Teams switch ends only at halftime.\n463\t- **HALFTIME**: story mode plays the locker-room cutscene with an adjustment choice. Exhibition shows the halftime\n464\t  show (cheerleaders, band, mascot; 6 s, skippable) and 4 stat tiles. A hub session gets a 3 s card.\n465\t- **OVERTIME**: tied after Q4. A coin toss, then sudden death: the first score wins, with a period of\n466\t  `T.quarterLength`. Still tied after it: exhibition ends in a TIE; story chapters say \"That's a FIASCO!\" and offer a\n467\t  rematch; a hub round finishes tied (every player in one standings array).\n468\t- **FINAL**: the whistle, the winners cheer (`pose: 'cheer'`) and the losers kneel. Then the results screen (§14.8).\n469\t\n470\t### 6.2 Downs, distance and spotting\n471\t- First and **20** (`T.yardsToGo`, 10–30). Gain the distance within 4 downs for a new set. Otherwise it's a turnover\n472\t  on downs at the spot.\n473\t- Forward progress is the ball's x when the play dies. A dive gains to where the ball lands.\n474\t- An incomplete pass puts the ball back at the previous spot.\n475\t- A loss behind the line counts against the distance. \"2ND & 27\" is legal and funny.\n476\t- Goal to go: when the line to gain is past the goal line, show \"& GOAL\".\n477\t- The first-down line (yellow) and the line of scrimmage (blue) are drawn on the field as decals, broadcast style.\n478\t\n479\t### 6.3 Scoring\n480\t| Score | Points | After |\n481\t|---|---|---|\n482\t| Touchdown: the ball carried over the goal line (the ball's x), or caught in the end zone with both feet in (simplified: the catch point inside it) | 6 | CELEBRATE, then PAT_CALL |\n483\t| Extra point kick | 1 | kickoff |\n484\t| Two-point conversion (any play from the 3) | 2 | kickoff |\n485\t| Field goal (between the uprights and over the bar, §7.8) | 3 | kickoff by the scoring team |\n486\t| Safety (the carrier downed in his own end zone) | 2 | the team that gave up the safety kicks off from its own 20 |\n487\t| Defensive TD on an INT or fumble return | 6 | as a touchdown |\n488\t\n489\t### 6.4 Kicks\n490\t- **Kickoff** from the kicking team's 30. Choices: DEEP (meter power 0.6–1.0 gives 50–70 yd), SQUIB (a low bouncer of\n491\t  30–40 yd that is hard to field), ONSIDE (10–14 yd, high bounce; recovery is a scramble, §7.7).\n492\t- **Receiving:** the returner auto-catches when under the ball within 1.2 yd. A ball caught in the end zone becomes a\n493\t  **touchback** at the 25 if the returner presses b1 (take a knee) or stays in the end zone for 1.5 s. A kick out of\n494\t  bounds puts the ball at the receiving team's 40.\n495\t- **Punt:** a long snap to the punter 12 yd deep, then the meter. A return, or a fair catch: the returner presses b1\n496\t  before the catch. A ball into the end zone is a touchback at the 20.\n497\t- **FG / PAT:** a snap to the holder 7 yd deep, a 0.35 s plant, the kick. The Kick Cam (§11.1). Rushers can block a\n498\t  kick if one reaches within 1.2 yd of the kick point before contact (`T.blockChance` 0.6).\n499\t\n500\t### 6.5 The clock\n501\t- Quarters of `T.quarterLength`: 2:00 in exhibition (a setting: 1:00, 1:30, 2:00, 3:00, 5:00), set per chapter in\n502\t  story mode, 1:30 in hub rounds.\n503\t- The clock runs during live plays. After an in-bounds play that wasn't a score, an incompletion or a turnover, it\n504\t  runs `T.runoff` (10 s) more (the huddle), but not in the last 30 s of a half.\n505\t- It stops for the play-call screen and every presentation beat. The play-call timer is separate (12 s).\n506\t- Hurry-up: in the last minute of a half the music tempo rises 8% (`audio.tempo`) and the announcer counts down.\n507\t- The clock never goes below 0. A play that starts at 0:01 is played out. At 0:00 the quarter ends when the ball is\n508\t  dead.\n509\t\n510\t### 6.6 Turnovers and possession changes\n511\t- **Interception:** the defender has the ball live and can return it. Control flips (§9.4).\n512\t- **Fumble:** the ball goes loose (§7.7). Whoever recovers has possession. A recovery by the defense is live (can be\n513\t  returned). A recovery by the offense is live too (arcade).\n514\t- **Lateral or backward pass:** legal any time. A bad lateral that hits the ground is a loose ball (a fumble).\n515\t- **Turnover on downs:** the other side takes the ball at the spot.\n516\t- After a change of possession, the line of scrimmage, `dir` and the down reset for the new offense.\n517\t\n518\t---\n519\t\n520\t## 7. Athletes, movement and actions\n521\t\n522\t### 7.1 Ratings\n523\tEach athlete has ratings 1–10: `SPD` speed, `ACC` acceleration and agility (cuts, spins), `STR` strength (blocking,\n524\tbreaking tackles), `HND` hands (catching, ball security), `TCK` tackling, `ARM` throw power, `AIM` throw accuracy,\n525\t`AWR` awareness (AI reaction and reads), `KCK` kicking (power and steadiness). Team ratings on the team-select screen\n526\tare 4 bars averaged from the roster: PASS, RUN, DEFENSE, SPECIAL.\n527\t\n528\t### 7.2 Movement\n529\t- Top speed in units/s = `T.speedBase` (92) + SPD × `T.speedPerSpd` (7), so SPD 2 is 106 (about 6.6 yd/s, a lineman)\n530\t  and SPD 10 is 162 (10.1 yd/s, Zap Zapata).\n531\t- Carrying the ball costs −4%. Backpedaling (moving against `facing` by more than 100°) costs −35%.\n532\t- Acceleration = `T.accelBase` (520) + ACC × 30 units/s². Braking is 900. Turn rate is 9 rad/s at speed and 16 rad/s\n533\t  slow (`E.approachAng`).\n534\t- **Turbo** (the `turbo` action, held): × `T.turboMult` (1.25) speed and × 1.3 acceleration. It drains `T.turboDrain`\n535\t  (0.45/s of a full bar) and regenerates `T.turboRegen` (0.22/s) when not held. Each dead ball refills +35%.\n536\t  Big Hits need turbo ≥ 0.15 and cost 0.15. Every athlete has a bar; the HUD shows the bars of human-held athletes.\n537\t- Surfaces: `mud` × 0.85 speed and × 0.7 acceleration, dives slide 1.5× farther. `ice` × 0.7 grip: velocity\n538\t  approaches the desired one at half the rate (slides), and cuts are wider. Snow weather × 0.95 speed. These come from\n539\t  the floor tag under the athlete (`map.floorAt`).\n540\t- Bodies are `E.Body` (r 6; linemen r 7.5; gravity 700) with `body.update(dt, map)` for z, jumps and the stands.\n541\t  The sim sets `vx, vy` from intents each step.\n542\t\n543\t### 7.3 Collisions between athletes\n544\t- Circle against circle every step for every pair (91 pairs at 14 athletes; no hash needed). Separate along the\n545\t  normal in proportion to the inverse of mass (mass = 1 + STR × 0.15; linemen get +0.6).\n546\t- Teammates push gently. Opponents push hard: no overlap.\n547\t- An overlap with the ball carrier during LIVE is a **contact** and may start a tackle (§7.5). An overlap between a\n548\t  blocker and a rusher starts an **engagement** (§7.9).\n549\t- Refs and sideline cast don't collide with the sim. Refs are pushed aside cosmetically and fall comically if a play\n550\t  runs through them (§10.8).\n551\t\n552\t### 7.4 The action system\n553\tEach athlete has at most one action at a time: `{ id, t, dur, phase, data }`. Actions lock movement as their table\n554\tsays, set the athlete's **pose code** (Appendix C) for the presentation, and resolve at their key moment.\n555\t\n556\t| Action | Who | Input | Duration | Key moment and effect |\n557\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n558\t| `hike` | C | the offense's b1 in PRESNAP | 0.25 s | the ball to the QB (shotgun: an arc in 0.3 s; under center: in hand) |\n559\t| `handoff` | QB → RB | automatic on run plays at the play's `handoffT` | 0.35 s | the ball changes hands when within 1.2 yd; a pitch or toss is a short arc |\n560\t| `throw` | QB | a receiver button, or a click on a receiver | 0.28 s wind + release | the ball's flight (§7.6); with turbo held, a Rocket Pass |\n561\t| `dive` | carrier | b1 | 0.45 s air + 0.6 s down | a lunge of 2.5 yd at 1.4× speed; the ball spot is where he lands; the play ends (down) unless he's in the end zone, which is a TD |\n562\t| `spin` | carrier | b3 | 0.32 s | evade window 0.0–0.22 s: a contact from a tackler that isn't behind him (angle > 110° from his facing) misses; speed × 0.8 during the spin; cooldown 0.8 s |\n563\t| `stiffArm` | carrier | b2 | 0.4 s | a tackler in front (within 60° of facing, within 1.8 yd) gets the break chance + `T.stiffBonus` (0.35); on a break the tackler stumbles (0.6 s down) |\n564\t| `hurdle` | carrier | b4 | 0.55 s (`body.jump(260)`) | airborne: low dives (`dive`, `tackleDive`) pass under him; a standing tackler isn't evaded |\n565\t| `lateral` | carrier | lb or rb | 0.25 s | pitch to the nearest teammate behind or beside him (within 100° of the backward direction, ≤ 9 yd), a 0.35–0.6 s arc; a bad lateral (no teammate in range) is thrown to the nearest spot and is a loose ball |\n566\t| `tackleDive` | defender | b1 (the AI decides when, §8.4) | 0.45 s lunge + 0.7 s down on a miss | contact with the carrier during the lunge resolves a tackle (§7.5) |\n567\t| `bigHit` | defender | turbo + b1 | 0.35 s lunge at 1.6× speed | as a tackle with `T.bigHitBonus` (+0.12) and a fumble roll × 3; a miss is 1.0 s down |\n568\t| `strip` | defender | b3 | 0.3 s | within 1.2 yd of the carrier: fumble chance `T.stripChance` (0.12) − HND × 0.01; otherwise a whiff |\n569\t| `jump` | any | b4 | 0.5 s | a jump for a catch, an INT or a swat: +16 units reach height and +6 catch radius for the ball arriving during 0.12–0.42 s |\n570\t| `diveCatch` | receiver | b1 while the ball is in the air | 0.45 s | +10 catch radius for low balls (arrival z < 12); he ends on the ground |\n571\t| `kick` | K or P | the meter (§7.8) | 0.6 s | the ball's flight from the meter's power and accuracy |\n572\t| `lateHit` | anyone | b1 within `T.lateHitWindow` after the whistle, an opponent within 2 yd | 0.5 s | cosmetic knockdown, crowd boos and cheers, the announcer's outrage, the \"Late hits\" stat |\n573\t| `celebrate` | scorer | auto or b2/b3/b4 | 3.5 s | dance (§10.6) |\n574\t| `getUp` | anyone downed | auto | 0.6–1.0 s | back on his feet |\n575\t\n576\tInputs are buffered for 0.15 s (`input.buffered` + `consume`) so a press slightly early still happens.\n577\t\n578\t### 7.5 Tackle resolution\n579\tWhen a tackler's lunge, or an engaged contact, touches the carrier (centers within r1 + r2 + 4):\n580\t```\n581\tp = T.tackleBase (0.62) + 0.03*TCK(t) - 0.015*STR(c) - 0.012*ACC(c)\n582\t  + (fromBehind ? 0.15 : 0) + (bigHit ? T.bigHitBonus : 0) + (carrier diving or hurdling ? +0.25 : 0)\n583\t  + bus.mod('tackle.p', ctx)    // cards, Hot Streak, Dinged, specials, difficulty\n584\tp = clamp(p, 0.15, 0.97)\n585\t```\n586\t- Evasions are checked first: a spin's window, a stiff arm's frontal break, a hurdle against a low dive.\n587\t- Success: the carrier goes down (pose `TACKLED`, falling the way the tackle pushed him), the ball is dead where it\n588\t  is, and the tackler lands on him. On a **Big Hit**, the carrier is launched along the hit direction at\n589\t  `T.bigHitLaunch` (180 u/s) and up at 150 u/s (pose `FLY`). Hit-stop 0.08 s, shake 4, a comic \"BOOM!\" (§11.3).\n590\t  The fumble roll is `T.fumbleBase` (0.03) × (bigHit 3) × (rain or snow 1.6) × (1 − HND × 0.05) + mods. A fumbled\n591\t  ball comes loose at the moment of the hit (§7.7).\n592\t- Failure: the tackler's lunge carries on and he lands (0.7 s down; 1.0 after a Big Hit). The carrier loses 25% of\n593\t  his speed for 0.3 s (a stumble) and gets a \"BROKEN TACKLE!\" pop when he was going ≥ 60% of top speed.\n594\t- **Gang tackles:** 2+ tacklers in contact within 0.3 s get +0.18 each beyond the first.\n595\t\n596\t### 7.6 The ball (`28-ball.js`)\n597\tStates: `held` (by an athlete), `snap`, `pass`, `kick`, `loose`, `dead`.\n598\t- **Held:** at the holder's carry point (sim: 6 units in front of the hip at z 14). The presentation draws it at the\n599\t  rig's hand through the gear hook (§12.2).\n600\t- **Pass:** analytic flight. At the throw, pick the target point P = the receiver's predicted position at the arrival\n601\t  time (iterate twice: t = d / speed). Speed by type: lob `T.lobSpeed` (240) + ARM × 8 u/s along the ground, bullet\n602\t  (Rocket Pass) `T.bulletSpeed` (400) + ARM × 10. Height at release is 30, and the ball aims to arrive at chest\n603\t  height (z 20): `E.arc({ x, y, z: 30 }, { x: P.x, y: P.y, z: 20 }, T.passGravity (300), { time: t })` gives the\n604\t  velocity. Passes use their own gravity, 300 (loose balls use 600), so the arcs read well. A 30-yd bullet (about\n605\t  1.2 s) peaks about 3 yd up, and a lob (about 1.6 s) about 6 yd up, a rainbow over the defender.\n606\t  - **Accuracy:** P gets a gaussian offset with σ = `T.aimError` (0.035) × distance × (1.3 − AIM × 0.06) × (pressure:\n607\t    a rusher within 2 yd 1.6; throwing on the run 1.25) + mods.\n608\t  - **Max range:** `T.maxPass` (55 yd) + ARM × 1.5 yd. Beyond it the ball is short.\n609\t  - **In flight:** the ball spins (draw the spiral, §12.7). A marker shows the landing point on the ground for the\n610\t    receiving team's human and the defending human (an X in team color, with a ring that shrinks as it nears).\n611\t  - **Arrival** (the ball at z ≤ 34 near P): resolve the catch (§7.7). If no one is in range, or the ball hits the\n612\t    ground, the pass is incomplete (a bounce: a short random-direction `loose` bounce for 0.6 s, cosmetic, dead).\n613\t- **Kick:** analytic flight to the landing point the meter gives, by `E.arc(from, landing, T.kickGravity (260),\n614\t  { apex })`, with apex `T.kickApex` (punts 260, kickoffs 220, FGs and PATs 150, squibs 20, onside 60). The hang time\n615\t  follows: a punt is about 2.8 s. A high kick may leave the top of the screen. The KICK shot zooms out to 0.75, and\n616\t  the shadow and landing marker keep the ball readable. On landing it goes `loose`: the `E.Body` ball, bounce 0.45,\n617\t  with friction. A football bounces erratically, so on each bounce add a random ±25° turn and ±30% speed from\n618\t  `FF.rng.sim`.\n619\t- **Loose:** an `E.Body` (r 3, bounce 0.45, gravity 600) with erratic bounces as above. Players dive at it (§7.7).\n620\t- Out of bounds (y outside the sidelines, or past an end line) kills the ball at the crossing point. A kick out of\n621\t  bounds follows §6.4.\n622\t\n623\t### 7.7 Catches, interceptions, deflections, fumbles and pile-ups\n624\t**Catch resolution** at arrival. Candidates are athletes whose reach covers the ball. The horizontal distance must be\n625\t≤ `T.catchRadius` (14) (+6 jumping in window, +10 diving), and the arrival height must be ≤ reach (34 standing, 50\n626\tjumping). For each candidate:\n627\t```\n628\toffense: pc = T.catchBase (0.66) + 0.03*HND - 0.22*(defenders within 14) - (bullet ? 0.10 : 0)\n629\t             + (jump in window ? 0.08 : 0) + bus.mod('catch.p')        // Glue Gloves, Butterfingers, Hot Streak\n630\tdefense: pi = 0.10 + 0.035*HND + (jump in window ? 0.12 : 0) + (in front of receiver ? 0.10 : 0) + bus.mod('int.p')\n631\t```\n632\t- Order the candidates by distance to the ball. Each nearest defender rolls first: INT with `pi`, else a\n633\t  **deflection** with 0.35 (the ball pops up to z 20–40 with a random velocity and can be caught again).\n634\t- Then the receiver rolls `pc`: a catch, or a **drop** (\"DROPPED!\", the ball bounces dead).\n635\t- Cheats and specials can force outcomes: `forceCatch`, `forceINT`, Golden Spiral (no INT).\n636\t- A catch gives the receiver the ball live with his velocity kept (catch and run). Control follows (§9.4).\n637\t\n638\t**Fumbles and loose balls.** The ball pops out with a velocity away from the hit plus up 60–110 u/s. Every athlete\n639\twithin `T.pileRadius` (5 yd) of the ball gets the brain behavior `recover` (dive at the ball). Humans dive with b1.\n640\t- The first athlete whose dive or feet touch the ball while z ≤ 6 gets a **recovery**. Rolled, not instant: if 2+\n641\t  athletes are within 1.5 yd at that moment, a **pile-up** starts.\n642\t- **Pile-up** (1.6 s): every athlete within 2.5 yd of the ball dives in (poses `PILE`, stacked by z offsets), a dust\n643\t  or mud cloud, the camera punches in, the crowd \"OOOH\". Then the ref \"digs\" (comic) and signals possession, a roll\n644\t  weighted by each piler's distance to the ball when it started (closer is more likely) and STR. \"WHO'S GOT IT?! ...\n645\t  THE FIASCOS HAVE IT!\"\n646\t- A loose ball that goes out of bounds belongs to the team that last had it.\n647\t\n648\t### 7.8 Kicking and the kick meter\n649\tA 3-press meter in screen space near the kicker (§14.7):\n650\t1. b1 starts the needle sweeping a power bar. It rises at `T.meterSpeed` (1.4 sweeps/s), then falls back.\n651\t2. b1 again sets the **power** (0–1). Past 0.95 is the red zone: +5% distance but the accuracy needle runs 1.5×\n652\t   faster.\n653\t3. The accuracy needle sweeps across a center band. b1 sets **accuracy** (−1..1). |acc| ≤ the band\n654\t   (`T.accBand` 0.12; Super Foot ×2.5) is straight; otherwise the kick hooks by acc × 9° (FG) or acc × 14° (punt\n655\t   and kickoff).\n656\t- Before step 1 the stick aims: ±15° for punts and kickoffs, ±4° for FGs (the Kick Cam shows the aim line).\n657\t- Distance: FG = 25 + power × (25 + KCK × 1.6) yd, so KCK 7 at full power is about 61 yd, before wind. Punt: 30–55\n658\t  yd. Kickoff: as §6.4.\n659\t- The FG test: when the ball reaches the end line's x, it's good if y is between the uprights (±49.5) and z > 53.\n660\t  A ball off an upright (within 3 units) bounces \"DOINK!\" and is no good (or good if it falls inside: roll it).\n661\t- CPU kickers: power and accuracy from KCK and difficulty, plus gaussian noise (σ 0.08 / 0.10).\n662\t- A mouse left click, b1 and Space all press the meter.\n663\t\n664\t### 7.9 Blocking\n665\t- **Engagement:** a blocker and a rusher overlap, or come within 1.5 yd with the blocker facing him, which locks them\n666\t  as a pair: they hold 0.8 yd apart, facing each other.\n667\t- **Contest:** each step the pair moves together with velocity `T.blockPush` (18 u/s) × (STR_r − STR_b +\n668\t  rushMoveBonus + (turbo ? 2 : 0)) toward the rusher's goal (the QB or the carrier) when positive, or the blocker's\n669\t  drive direction when negative. Run blocks add the blocker's drive (+1.5).\n670\t- **Shed:** the rusher sheds when his shed timer runs out. `shedTime = T.shedBase (1.9 s) × (STR_b / STR_r) ×\n671\t  rand(0.7, 1.3)`, or ∞ under Brick Wall. On a shed the rusher gets a 0.3 s burst (×1.2) toward his target, and the\n672\t  blocker staggers 0.3 s. A human rusher can mash b3 (□) to cut his timer by 0.12 s per press (a swim move; max 6).\n673\t- **Pancake:** when STR_b − STR_r ≥ 3, a 15% roll on engagement knocks the rusher down for 1.2 s. \"PANCAKE!\"\n674\t- Pass blocks mirror the most dangerous unblocked rusher in the blocker's zone (left, middle, right). Run blocks\n675\t  follow the play's assignment (§8.2).\n676\t- A human-controlled lineman (co-op, or by switching) moves freely. Engagement then happens on contact.\n677\t\n678\t---\n679\t\n680\t## 8. The AI (`33-ai.js`, `34-ai-coach.js`)\n681\t\n682\t### 8.1 Brains and behaviors\n683\tEvery athlete not held by a human (or by a remote player) has a brain. A brain picks a **behavior** from its play\n684\tassignment and the phase, re-thinks at 20 Hz (staggered: `(step + index) % 6 === 0`), and every step turns the current\n685\tbehavior into an intent (a move vector, turbo, presses). Reaction delays come from `AWR` and difficulty: an AI sees a\n686\t**delayed** world. It reads other athletes' positions from a 0.1–0.4 s history buffer, which makes cutbacks, jukes\n687\tand play fakes work against it.\n688\t\n689\t| Behavior | Role | What it does |\n690\t|---|---|---|\n691\t| `formation` | all, PRESNAP | jog to the formation spot, face the line, take the stance |\n692\t| `passBlock(zone)` | OL, RB on some plays | hold a spot 1.5–2.5 yd in front of the QB in his zone, mirror the most dangerous rusher there, engage |\n693\t| `runBlock(target, drive)` | OL, WR, TE | engage the assigned defender and drive him along `drive`, or climb to the 2nd level |\n694\t| `pull(path, then)` | G on sweeps and counters | run the path, then lead-block the first defender in front of the carrier |\n695\t| `route(points, after)` | WR, RB | run the waypoints (cut sharply: brake to 60% at a break, 0.12 s), then `after`: `settle` (find the nearest hole in the zones, face the QB), `scramble` (work toward the sideline the QB scrambles to) or `block` |\n696\t| `carry(path)` | the carrier | follow the play's path while it's open, then **free running**: score 16 directions each 0.1 s, `forward·w1 − Σ threat(defender) − sideline penalty − crowding`, where threat = closing speed / distance² for defenders within 8 yd; pick the best with hysteresis; turbo when no defender is within 4 yd or one is closing from behind; evasive moves, below |\n697\t| `qbPass(drop, reads)` | QB | drop back to the play's spot. From `readT` (0.6–1.0 s) read the receivers in order. **Openness** = the distance from the nearest defender to the receiver's arrival point, minus 8 per defender within 3 yd of the pass lane. Throw to the first receiver with openness > `T.aiOpen` (3 yd; by difficulty) whose route is ≥ 40% done. At 2.8 s, or with a rusher within 2 yd, throw to the best, scramble if the best is < 1 yd open, or throw it away out of bounds (Pro and Fiasco difficulty). Scramble: `carry`, and still look to throw while behind the line. |\n698\t| `qbRun` | QB on sneaks and the run fakes of draws | his part of the play script |\n699\t| `rush(gap)` | DL, blitzers | attack the gap toward the QB, engage blockers, try to shed; once free, `pursue` the QB; when the QB throws, `pursue` the ball |\n700\t| `manCover(target)` | DB, LB | keep a cushion of `T.cushion` (2 yd, smaller near the goal line) on the goal side of the target, matching his speed with the reaction delay; when the ball is in the air, break to the arrival point after the reaction delay and jump when within 2 yd as it arrives |\n701\t| `zoneCover(zone)` | DB, LB | drop to the zone center; pick the most threatening receiver inside (zone radius + 3 yd), weighted by depth and how open he is; shadow him; release him when he leaves; the ball in the air is as above |\n702\t| `spy(QB)` | LB | mirror the QB at 5 yd; when the QB crosses the line, `pursue` |\n703\t| `pursue(carrier)` | all defenders, once there is a carrier past the line, a scramble, or a return | head for the intercept point: the smallest t with `|c + v_c·t − d| ≤ speed_d·t`, solved in 0.05 s steps up to 3 s, with the angle limited by the sideline. Tackle decision below |\n704\t| `kickCover(lane)` | the kicking team | sprint downfield in lanes 6 yd apart, then `pursue` the returner |\n705\t| `returnBlock` | the receiving team | form a wedge of 3 in front of the returner's lane; engage the nearest coverage man |\n706\t| `recover` | everyone near a loose ball | dive at the ball (§7.7) |\n707\t| `celebrate`, `dejected`, `idle` | after the play | poses (§10.6) |\n708\t\n709\t**Evasive moves for an AI carrier.** When a tackler is within 1.6 yd and closing: against a diving tackler, hurdle\n710\twith chance 0.25 + ACC × 0.03; against a frontal one, stiff arm with 0.2 + STR × 0.03; against one from the side,\n711\tspin with 0.2 + ACC × 0.03. One move per 0.8 s. Difficulty scales these.\n712\t\n713\t**The tackle decision (AI tacklers).** With a reachable carrier, dive when the distance is under 1.8 yd and the angle\n714\tto his predicted position is under 35°. Use a Big Hit when turbo ≥ 0.15 and a roll of 0.2 + TCK × 0.03 passes (×1.5\n715\twhen Hot). Otherwise keep pursuing to contact (a wrap-up on contact counts as a tackle attempt with −0.08).\n716\t\n717\t### 8.2 Play assignments (data to brain)\n718\t`FF.data.plays[id].assign[slot]` (Appendix A.2) is read when PRESNAP ends:\n719\t- Offense: `{ block: 'pass' | 'run', zone, target, drive }`, `{ pull: [[x, y], ...], then: 'lead' }`,\n720\t  `{ route: [[x, y], ...], after: 'settle', read: n }`, `{ carry: [[x, y], ...], handoffT }`, and the QB's\n721\t  `{ drop: [x, y], reads: ['WR2', 'WR1', 'RB'], readT, fake }`.\n722\t- Defense: `{ rush: 'A' | 'B' | 'C' | 'contain' }`, `{ man: 'WR1' | 'WR2' | 'RB' | 'C' }`,\n723\t  `{ zone: { at: [x, y], r } }`, `{ spy: 'QB' }`.\n724\t- Play coordinates are in **yards** from the ball spot: x downfield (+ is the offense's attacking direction), y\n725\t  across (+ is the offense's right). Mirroring (FLIP) negates y. Defense coordinates use the same frame (x is the\n726\t  distance past the line toward the defense's own goal). The sim converts with `dir`.\n727\t- Targets in defense assignments point at offensive slots. If the offense's formation lacks a slot, fall back to\n728\t  zone `[8, 0], r 7`.\n729\t\n730\t### 8.3 Special teams brains\n731\tKick coverage, wedge returns, the punt shield (3 blockers in front of the punter for 1.5 s), FG protection\n732\t(pass-block in place) and the FG rush (all-out `rush`; 2 jumpers in the middle `jump` at the kick).\n733\t\n734\t### 8.4 Difficulty\n735\t| | Rookie | Pro | Fiasco |\n736\t|---|---|---|---|\n737\t| CPU reaction delay | 0.38 s | 0.24 s | 0.13 s |\n738\t| CPU tackle p × | 0.85 | 1.0 | 1.12 |\n739\t| CPU QB openness threshold | 4.5 yd | 3 yd | 2 yd |\n740\t| CPU kick noise × | 1.5 | 1.0 | 0.6 |\n741\t| CPU coverage cushion × | 1.3 | 1.0 | 0.85 |\n742\t| Catch-up assist (`T.rubberBand`): when the human trails by ≥ 14, his team gets +0.05 catch, +0.04 tackle and −0.04 to the CPU's | on | off | off |\n743\tStory chapters set a difficulty per chapter (§15.1), scaled by the Difficulty setting.\n744\t\n745\t### 8.5 The coach AI (play calling, cards, decisions)\n746\t- **Situation features:** down, distance, field position, score difference, time left, quarter, and the opponent's\n747\t  last 5 play types (Fiasco difficulty only).\n748\t- **Offense:** start from the team's tendency weights (`ai.run`, `ai.pass`, `ai.trick`, §18.1), then apply the\n749\t  situation rules in order:\n750\t  - 3rd/4th & 10+ means pass deep or mid (×2.5).\n751\t  - Less than 3 to go means run, sneak or quick passes (×2).\n752\t  - Goal to go inside the 5 means GOAL LINE runs, slants and fades.\n753\t  - In the last 1:00 while trailing: sideline routes (Quick Outs, Out & Up) ×3, and get out of bounds.\n754\t  - Leading by 9+ in the last 2:00: runs and kneels.\n755\t  - 4th down: field goal if the kick is ≤ `T.cpuFgRange` (45 yd) + KCK. Go for it if the distance is ≤ 3 and the ball\n756\t    is past midfield, or if trailing late. Punt otherwise. A 5% fake punt roll on 4th & ≤ 5.\n757\t- **Defense:** counter the offense's tendency (blitz on 3rd & long ×1.8, goal line inside the 3, prevent while\n758\t  leading late), with 20% randomness so the CPU can be read only roughly.\n759\t- **Cards:** the CPU uses a card when its trigger matches (§10.2) and it holds one. At most one card per 3 plays.\n760\t- **PAT:** go for 2 when down by 2, 5, 9, 10 or 12 after the TD in the second half. Otherwise kick.\n761\t- **COACH SAYS:** run the offense logic for the human's side, and mark the top pick with a star badge on the\n762\t  play-call screen. If the human doesn't choose, it plays when the timer runs out.\n763\t\n764\t### 8.6 The Play Lab (an AI quality tool and test)\n765\t`FF.dev.playLab(offPlay, defPlay, n, { seed, teams, difficulty })` runs n plays headless and fast (presentation off)\n766\tfrom midfield on 1st & 20, both sides CPU. It returns `{ avgYards, completion, sacks, ints, fumbles, tds, avgTime,\n767\tmaxTime, stuck }`, where `stuck` counts plays over 25 s or an athlete idle more than 3 s while LIVE.\n768\t**The bands it must stay in** (every offensive play against each of the 8 non-kick defenses, n = 30, Pro, Fiascos vs\n769\tMudhogs):\n770\t- Nothing is `stuck`, and every play ends within 25 s.\n771\t- A pass play's completion, averaged over the 8 defenses, is 35–85% (HAIL MARY 5–40%), and its sack rate is under\n772\t  25%.\n773\t- A run play averages 1.5–9 yd over the 8 defenses.\n774\t- No play averages over 25 yd over the 8 defenses (no unstoppable play), and no defense holds every play under 1 yd\n775\t  (no unbeatable defense).\n776\tIn the browser the Dev menu runs the same lab on screen with debug overlays (§17).\n777\t\n778\t---\n779\t\n780\t## 9. Controls and human control (`35-control.js`)\n781\t\n782\t### 9.1 The input map (`FF.KEYS`)\n783\tA custom preset (scene `input: FF.KEYS`). The order matters: `Input.halfKeys` gives a shared-keyboard half its 6\n784\tbuttons in this order. The engine's standard `confirm`/`cancel` are redefined so J and K don't mean menu\n785\tconfirm/cancel.\n786\t```js\n787\tFF.KEYS = {\n788\t  up: E.Input.DEFAULT.up, down: E.Input.DEFAULT.down, left: E.Input.DEFAULT.left, right: E.Input.DEFAULT.right,\n789\t  start: ['Enter', 'NumpadEnter', 'Pad9'], pause: ['Escape', 'KeyP', 'Pad9'],\n790\t  confirm: ['Enter', 'Space', 'NumpadEnter', 'Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape', 'Backspace', 'Pad1'],\n791\t  b1: ['Space', 'KeyK', 'Pad0'],   // ✕ / A    hike, dive, tackle, kick meter, pick\n792\t  b2: ['KeyL', 'Pad1'],            // ○ / B    stiff arm, switch player, back\n793\t  b3: ['KeyJ', 'Pad2'],            // □ / X    spin, strip, flip play\n794\t  b4: ['KeyI', 'Pad3'],            // △ / Y    hurdle, jump, catch, Fiasco Card\n795\t  turbo: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight', 'Pad7'],   // R2 / RT\n796\t  lb: ['KeyQ', 'Pad4'],            // L1 / LB  lateral, previous tab\n797\t  rb: ['KeyE', 'Pad5'],            // R1 / RB  lateral, next tab\n798\t  r1: ['Digit1'], r2: ['Digit2'], r3: ['Digit3'], r4: ['Digit4']   // keyboard: throw to receiver 1-4\n799\t};\n800\t```\n801\tCheck `E.Input.DEFAULT` for the exact move codes (WASD, the arrows, the d-pad, `PadAxis*`). The Controls screen names\n802\tthem with `menu.controls: { b1: 'Hike · Dive · Tackle · Kick', b2: 'Stiff arm · Switch player', b3: 'Spin · Strip ·\n803\tFlip play', b4: 'Hurdle · Jump · Catch · Card', turbo: 'Turbo', lb: 'Lateral · Tab left', rb: 'Lateral · Tab right',\n804\tr1: 'Throw to receiver 1', ... }`.\n805\t\n806\t### 9.2 The control table by context\n807\t| Context | Action | PlayStation | Xbox | Keyboard | Mouse |\n808\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n809\t| Menus | move the focus | d-pad / left stick | d-pad / left stick | arrows / WASD | hover moves the focus |\n810\t| | select | ✕ | A | Enter / Space | left click |\n811\t| | back | ○ | B | Esc / Backspace | the on-screen BACK button |\n812\t| | tabs | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Q / E | click a tab |\n813\t| Play call | pick the play | ✕ | A | Enter / Space | click a card |\n814\t| | flip it | □ | X | J | click FLIP |\n815\t| | Fiasco Card | △ (opens the card row) | Y | I | click a card slot |\n816\t| Pre-snap, offense | hike | ✕ | A | Space | click the QB |\n817\t| Pre-snap, defense | move your defender | stick | stick | WASD | — |\n818\t| | switch defender | ○ | B | L | click a defender |\n819\t| QB behind the line | throw to receiver ✕ ○ □ △ | ✕ ○ □ △ | A B X Y | 1 2 3 4 (or K L J I) | click a receiver |\n820\t| | Rocket Pass | hold R2 + a receiver | RT + | Shift + | Shift + click |\n821\t| | scramble | stick (+ R2) | stick (+ RT) | WASD (+ Shift) | — |\n822\t| Ball carrier | turbo | R2 | RT | Shift | — |\n823\t| | dive | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n824\t| | stiff arm | ○ | B | L | — |\n825\t| | spin | □ | X | J | — |\n826\t| | hurdle | △ | Y | I | — |\n827\t| | lateral | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Q / E | — |\n828\t| Ball in the air, offense | jump catch | △ | Y | I | — |\n829\t| | diving catch | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n830\t| Defense, live | switch to the defender nearest the ball | ○ | B | L | click a defender |\n831\t| | tackle (dive) | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n832\t| | Big Hit | R2 + ✕ | RT + A | Shift + Space | — |\n833\t| | jump, swat, intercept | △ | Y | I | — |\n834\t| | strip | □ | X | J | — |\n835\t| | swim move (while blocked) | □ repeatedly | X | J | — |\n836\t| After the whistle | late hit | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n837\t| Kicking | aim | stick ← → | stick | A / D, ← / → | — |\n838\t| | the meter (3 presses) | ✕ | A | Space | left click |\n839\t| Kick return in the end zone | take a knee | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n840\t| TD celebration | pick a dance | ○ □ △ | B X Y | L J I | — |\n841\t| Holding a boss star | his special (§10.5) | L1 + R1 together | LB + RB | Q + E | — |\n842\t| Anywhere | pause | Options | Menu | Esc / P | the corner menu button |\n843\t| Anywhere | change the camera view | — | — | V | — |\n844\tThe mouse wheel zooms the camera within the scene's range (engine standard, `camera: { zoom: [.7, 1.4] }`).\n845\t\n846\t### 9.3 Prompts that follow the device\n847\t- `FF.glyph.chip(g, x, y, action, { player, scale, device })` draws a button chip for that player's last device\n848\t  (`input.lastDevice`):\n849\t  - **Gamepad, PlayStation** (the default): a dark disc with the symbol drawn as pixels in its classic color: ✕ blue"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1931e3d-b97b-4f9d-8b9d-c7660f7740b5","uuid":"19d0946e-f159-4711-afcf-aeb37a43de1c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:02:40.472Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-070229__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","content":"### 5.4 Floor painting (`floorTex(x, y, tag)`)\n- Grass in mown stripes 5 yards wide, alternating two greens per stadium, with per-pixel noise (`E.hash2`,\n  `E.noise2`), like the SPORTS kit's floor.\n- White paint for yard lines (a band 1.2 units wide), hashes (0.8 yd long), and the sidelines and end lines (2 units).\n  Numbers are the 5×7 font's digit bitmaps scaled to 2.5 yd tall, sampled per world pixel. End-zone letters and the\n  midfield logo work the same way from their bitmaps (§12.8).\n- Mud (`mud` tag and noise blotches) is brown and wet-dark. Ice is pale blue with streaks. Divots are decals added at\n  runtime (§11.5).\n- Night stadiums: `[r, g, b, glow]` texels make the Afterglow Arena's yard lines glow. A wear pass darkens the grass\n  between the hashes.\n- The floor bakes once per view (engine behavior). View changes (Kick Cam, replays) rebake. That's fine: cut, don't\n  rotate continuously (§11.1).\n\n### 5.5 Dressing per stadium\nEach stadium (§18.2) lists its props: shapes and `r.prop`s on the far sideline (benches, coolers, the Gatorade-style\ncooler table, TV cameras, light towers at the 4 corners), the near sideline (team benches, the cheerleader squad, the\nmascot, the chain gang with down markers that move to the line of scrimmage and the line to gain, photographers),\nbehind the end zones (pyro cannons, banners, a parked pickup or an airboat), and the backdrop layers. Aim for 3–8\nprops on any screen (the API's look target). Light towers carry `lights: [...]` that light the field at night.\n\n### 5.6 Views\n| Id | Made as | Used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `broadcast` | `new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 50, 1, 1)` | the default for play |\n| `sky` | `new E.View('sky', 'Sky Cam', 0, 65, 1, 1)` | option (V cycles; Settings, Camera) |\n| `blimp` | `E.VIEWS.topdown` | option |\n| `kickcam` | `new E.View('kickcam', 'Kick Cam', ±90, 32, 1, 1)`, turned so the kick goes up the screen | FGs and PATs (and kickoffs from behind the kicker for 1.2 s) |\n| `field` | `new E.View('field', 'Field Level', 0, 25, 1, 1)` (the brawler pitch) | replays, celebrations, cutscenes on the field |\n| `iso` | `E.VIEWS.iso` | replays |\n| `reverse` | `new E.View('reverse', 'Reverse Angle', 180, 45, 1, 1)` | replays (the far stands cut low) |\n\nSteer with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`, so pushing up always means up the screen in every view, including\nthe Kick Cam. `E.charView` draws rigs from a lower angle in pitch 40–89 views automatically, and that's wanted.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Football rules and the match flow (`30-sim.js`)\n\n### 6.1 The match state machine\n```\nCOIN_TOSS ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP ─▶ KICK_LIVE ─▶ (DEAD) ─▶ PLAYCALL ─▶ PRESNAP ─▶ LIVE ─▶ DEAD ─▶ ...\nDEAD branches: score ─▶ CELEBRATE ─▶ [REPLAY] ─▶ PAT_CALL ─▶ PAT ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP\n               turnover / downs ─▶ PLAYCALL (other side)    quarter end ─▶ QUARTER_BREAK\n               halftime ─▶ HALFTIME ─▶ KICKOFF_SETUP        end of regulation, tied ─▶ OVERTIME ─▶ ...\n               final ─▶ FINAL (results)\n```\n- **COIN_TOSS** (1.5 s): the home mascot flips a giant coin (cosmetic). The winner is `FF.rng.sim`'s, or the\n  scenario's. The winner receives.\n- **PLAYCALL**: both sides choose on the play-call UI (§14.5). The CPU chooses after a human-like 0.6–1.4 s delay.\n  The timer is `T.playcallTime` (12 s). When it runs out, the COACH SAYS play is picked. Kicking situations show the\n  special cards.\n- **PRESNAP**: athletes jog to their formation spots (1.2 s), then the line sets. The offense hikes with b1. The CPU\n  hikes after 0.5–2.0 s. A human gets an auto-hike when `T.autoHike` (8 s) runs out, with a visible play clock from\n  5 s. The defense's human may move their defender behind the line. The QB barks his cadence (§13.4).\n- **LIVE**: the play. It ends with a **dead ball**: a carrier down (tackled, or a knee or body touching the ground\n  after a dive), out of bounds, an incomplete pass, a score, a pile-up resolved, a kick dead (touchback, out of\n  bounds, downed), or a kneel.\n- **DEAD** (1.2 s, or longer for presentation): refs signal, the chain gang moves, late hits are possible for\n  `T.lateHitWindow` (1.2 s), then the spot and downs update.\n- **CELEBRATE** (after a TD, 3.5 s, skippable with b1): the scorer's dance (§10.6), pyro, the crowd, the announcer.\n  Then **REPLAY** if the Replays setting allows (§11.2).\n- **PAT_CALL**: KICK XP (1 point, the snap from the 15) or GO FOR 2 (a normal play from the 3).\n- **QUARTER_BREAK** (1.5 s, a card \"END OF THE 1ST\"). Teams switch ends only at halftime.\n- **HALFTIME**: story mode plays the locker-room cutscene with an adjustment choice. Exhibition shows the halftime\n  show (cheerleaders, band, mascot; 6 s, skippable) and 4 stat tiles. A hub session gets a 3 s card.\n- **OVERTIME**: tied after Q4. A coin toss, then sudden death: the first score wins, with a period of\n  `T.quarterLength`. Still tied after it: exhibition ends in a TIE; story chapters say \"That's a FIASCO!\" and offer a\n  rematch; a hub round finishes tied (every player in one standings array).\n- **FINAL**: the whistle, the winners cheer (`pose: 'cheer'`) and the losers kneel. Then the results screen (§14.8).\n\n### 6.2 Downs, distance and spotting\n- First and **20** (`T.yardsToGo`, 10–30). Gain the distance within 4 downs for a new set. Otherwise it's a turnover\n  on downs at the spot.\n- Forward progress is the ball's x when the play dies. A dive gains to where the ball lands.\n- An incomplete pass puts the ball back at the previous spot.\n- A loss behind the line counts against the distance. \"2ND & 27\" is legal and funny.\n- Goal to go: when the line to gain is past the goal line, show \"& GOAL\".\n- The first-down line (yellow) and the line of scrimmage (blue) are drawn on the field as decals, broadcast style.\n\n### 6.3 Scoring\n| Score | Points | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| Touchdown: the ball carried over the goal line (the ball's x), or caught in the end zone with both feet in (simplified: the catch point inside it) | 6 | CELEBRATE, then PAT_CALL |\n| Extra point kick | 1 | kickoff |\n| Two-point conversion (any play from the 3) | 2 | kickoff |\n| Field goal (between the uprights and over the bar, §7.8) | 3 | kickoff by the scoring team |\n| Safety (the carrier downed in his own end zone) | 2 | the team that gave up the safety kicks off from its own 20 |\n| Defensive TD on an INT or fumble return | 6 | as a touchdown |\n\n### 6.4 Kicks\n- **Kickoff** from the kicking team's 30. Choices: DEEP (meter power 0.6–1.0 gives 50–70 yd), SQUIB (a low bouncer of\n  30–40 yd that is hard to field), ONSIDE (10–14 yd, high bounce; recovery is a scramble, §7.7).\n- **Receiving:** the returner auto-catches when under the ball within 1.2 yd. A ball caught in the end zone becomes a\n  **touchback** at the 25 if the returner presses b1 (take a knee) or stays in the end zone for 1.5 s. A kick out of\n  bounds puts the ball at the receiving team's 40.\n- **Punt:** a long snap to the punter 12 yd deep, then the meter. A return, or a fair catch: the returner presses b1\n  before the catch. A ball into the end zone is a touchback at the 20.\n- **FG / PAT:** a snap to the holder 7 yd deep, a 0.35 s plant, the kick. The Kick Cam (§11.1). Rushers can block a\n  kick if one reaches within 1.2 yd of the kick point before contact (`T.blockChance` 0.6).\n\n### 6.5 The clock\n- Quarters of `T.quarterLength`: 2:00 in exhibition (a setting: 1:00, 1:30, 2:00, 3:00, 5:00), set per chapter in\n  story mode, 1:30 in hub rounds.\n- The clock runs during live plays. After an in-bounds play that wasn't a score, an incompletion or a turnover, it\n  runs `T.runoff` (10 s) more (the huddle), but not in the last 30 s of a half.\n- It stops for the play-call screen and every presentation beat. The play-call timer is separate (12 s).\n- Hurry-up: in the last minute of a half the music tempo rises 8% (`audio.tempo`) and the announcer counts down.\n- The clock never goes below 0. A play that starts at 0:01 is played out. At 0:00 the quarter ends when the ball is\n  dead.\n\n### 6.6 Turnovers and possession changes\n- **Interception:** the defender has the ball live and can return it. Control flips (§9.4).\n- **Fumble:** the ball goes loose (§7.7). Whoever recovers has possession. A recovery by the defense is live (can be\n  returned). A recovery by the offense is live too (arcade).\n- **Lateral or backward pass:** legal any time. A bad lateral that hits the ground is a loose ball (a fumble).\n- **Turnover on downs:** the other side takes the ball at the spot.\n- After a change of possession, the line of scrimmage, `dir` and the down reset for the new offense.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Athletes, movement and actions\n\n### 7.1 Ratings\nEach athlete has ratings 1–10: `SPD` speed, `ACC` acceleration and agility (cuts, spins), `STR` strength (blocking,\nbreaking tackles), `HND` hands (catching, ball security), `TCK` tackling, `ARM` throw power, `AIM` throw accuracy,\n`AWR` awareness (AI reaction and reads), `KCK` kicking (power and steadiness). Team ratings on the team-select screen\nare 4 bars averaged from the roster: PASS, RUN, DEFENSE, SPECIAL.\n\n### 7.2 Movement\n- Top speed in units/s = `T.speedBase` (92) + SPD × `T.speedPerSpd` (7), so SPD 2 is 106 (about 6.6 yd/s, a lineman)\n  and SPD 10 is 162 (10.1 yd/s, Zap Zapata).\n- Carrying the ball costs −4%. Backpedaling (moving against `facing` by more than 100°) costs −35%.\n- Acceleration = `T.accelBase` (520) + ACC × 30 units/s². Braking is 900. Turn rate is 9 rad/s at speed and 16 rad/s\n  slow (`E.approachAng`).\n- **Turbo** (the `turbo` action, held): × `T.turboMult` (1.25) speed and × 1.3 acceleration. It drains `T.turboDrain`\n  (0.45/s of a full bar) and regenerates `T.turboRegen` (0.22/s) when not held. Each dead ball refills +35%.\n  Big Hits need turbo ≥ 0.15 and cost 0.15. Every athlete has a bar; the HUD shows the bars of human-held athletes.\n- Surfaces: `mud` × 0.85 speed and × 0.7 acceleration, dives slide 1.5× farther. `ice` × 0.7 grip: velocity\n  approaches the desired one at half the rate (slides), and cuts are wider. Snow weather × 0.95 speed. These come from\n  the floor tag under the athlete (`map.floorAt`).\n- Bodies are `E.Body` (r 6; linemen r 7.5; gravity 700) with `body.update(dt, map)` for z, jumps and the stands.\n  The sim sets `vx, vy` from intents each step.\n\n### 7.3 Collisions between athletes\n- Circle against circle every step for every pair (91 pairs at 14 athletes; no hash needed). Separate along the\n  normal in proportion to the inverse of mass (mass = 1 + STR × 0.15; linemen get +0.6).\n- Teammates push gently. Opponents push hard: no overlap.\n- An overlap with the ball carrier during LIVE is a **contact** and may start a tackle (§7.5). An overlap between a\n  blocker and a rusher starts an **engagement** (§7.9).\n- Refs and sideline cast don't collide with the sim. Refs are pushed aside cosmetically and fall comically if a play\n  runs through them (§10.8).\n\n### 7.4 The action system\nEach athlete has at most one action at a time: `{ id, t, dur, phase, data }`. Actions lock movement as their table\nsays, set the athlete's **pose code** (Appendix C) for the presentation, and resolve at their key moment.\n\n| Action | Who | Input | Duration | Key moment and effect |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `hike` | C | the offense's b1 in PRESNAP | 0.25 s | the ball to the QB (shotgun: an arc in 0.3 s; under center: in hand) |\n| `handoff` | QB → RB | automatic on run plays at the play's `handoffT` | 0.35 s | the ball changes hands when within 1.2 yd; a pitch or toss is a short arc |\n| `throw` | QB | a receiver button, or a click on a receiver | 0.28 s wind + release | the ball's flight (§7.6); with turbo held, a Rocket Pass |\n| `dive` | carrier | b1 | 0.45 s air + 0.6 s down | a lunge of 2.5 yd at 1.4× speed; the ball spot is where he lands; the play ends (down) unless he's in the end zone, which is a TD |\n| `spin` | carrier | b3 | 0.32 s | evade window 0.0–0.22 s: a contact from a tackler that isn't behind him (angle > 110° from his facing) misses; speed × 0.8 during the spin; cooldown 0.8 s |\n| `stiffArm` | carrier | b2 | 0.4 s | a tackler in front (within 60° of facing, within 1.8 yd) gets the break chance + `T.stiffBonus` (0.35); on a break the tackler stumbles (0.6 s down) |\n| `hurdle` | carrier | b4 | 0.55 s (`body.jump(260)`) | airborne: low dives (`dive`, `tackleDive`) pass under him; a standing tackler isn't evaded |\n| `lateral` | carrier | lb or rb | 0.25 s | pitch to the nearest teammate behind or beside him (within 100° of the backward direction, ≤ 9 yd), a 0.35–0.6 s arc; a bad lateral (no teammate in range) is thrown to the nearest spot and is a loose ball |\n| `tackleDive` | defender | b1 (the AI decides when, §8.4) | 0.45 s lunge + 0.7 s down on a miss | contact with the carrier during the lunge resolves a tackle (§7.5) |\n| `bigHit` | defender | turbo + b1 | 0.35 s lunge at 1.6× speed | as a tackle with `T.bigHitBonus` (+0.12) and a fumble roll × 3; a miss is 1.0 s down |\n| `strip` | defender | b3 | 0.3 s | within 1.2 yd of the carrier: fumble chance `T.stripChance` (0.12) − HND × 0.01; otherwise a whiff |\n| `jump` | any | b4 | 0.5 s | a jump for a catch, an INT or a swat: +16 units reach height and +6 catch radius for the ball arriving during 0.12–0.42 s |\n| `diveCatch` | receiver | b1 while the ball is in the air | 0.45 s | +10 catch radius for low balls (arrival z < 12); he ends on the ground |\n| `kick` | K or P | the meter (§7.8) | 0.6 s | the ball's flight from the meter's power and accuracy |\n| `lateHit` | anyone | b1 within `T.lateHitWindow` after the whistle, an opponent within 2 yd | 0.5 s | cosmetic knockdown, crowd boos and cheers, the announcer's outrage, the \"Late hits\" stat |\n| `celebrate` | scorer | auto or b2/b3/b4 | 3.5 s | dance (§10.6) |\n| `getUp` | anyone downed | auto | 0.6–1.0 s | back on his feet |\n\nInputs are buffered for 0.15 s (`input.buffered` + `consume`) so a press slightly early still happens.\n\n### 7.5 Tackle resolution\nWhen a tackler's lunge, or an engaged contact, touches the carrier (centers within r1 + r2 + 4):\n```\np = T.tackleBase (0.62) + 0.03*TCK(t) - 0.015*STR(c) - 0.012*ACC(c)\n  + (fromBehind ? 0.15 : 0) + (bigHit ? T.bigHitBonus : 0) + (carrier diving or hurdling ? +0.25 : 0)\n  + bus.mod('tackle.p', ctx)    // cards, Hot Streak, Dinged, specials, difficulty\np = clamp(p, 0.15, 0.97)\n```\n- Evasions are checked first: a spin's window, a stiff arm's frontal break, a hurdle against a low dive.\n- Success: the carrier goes down (pose `TACKLED`, falling the way the tackle pushed him), the ball is dead where it\n  is, and the tackler lands on him. On a **Big Hit**, the carrier is launched along the hit direction at\n  `T.bigHitLaunch` (180 u/s) and up at 150 u/s (pose `FLY`). Hit-stop 0.08 s, shake 4, a comic \"BOOM!\" (§11.3).\n  The fumble roll is `T.fumbleBase` (0.03) × (bigHit 3) × (rain or snow 1.6) × (1 − HND × 0.05) + mods. A fumbled\n  ball comes loose at the moment of the hit (§7.7).\n- Failure: the tackler's lunge carries on and he lands (0.7 s down; 1.0 after a Big Hit). The carrier loses 25% of\n  his speed for 0.3 s (a stumble) and gets a \"BROKEN TACKLE!\" pop when he was going ≥ 60% of top speed.\n- **Gang tackles:** 2+ tacklers in contact within 0.3 s get +0.18 each beyond the first.\n\n### 7.6 The ball (`28-ball.js`)\nStates: `held` (by an athlete), `snap`, `pass`, `kick`, `loose`, `dead`.\n- **Held:** at the holder's carry point (sim: 6 units in front of the hip at z 14). The presentation draws it at the\n  rig's hand through the gear hook (§12.2).\n- **Pass:** analytic flight. At the throw, pick the target point P = the receiver's predicted position at the arrival\n  time (iterate twice: t = d / speed). Speed by type: lob `T.lobSpeed` (240) + ARM × 8 u/s along the ground, bullet\n  (Rocket Pass) `T.bulletSpeed` (400) + ARM × 10. Height at release is 30, and the ball aims to arrive at chest\n  height (z 20): `E.arc({ x, y, z: 30 }, { x: P.x, y: P.y, z: 20 }, T.passGravity (300), { time: t })` gives the\n  velocity. Passes use their own gravity, 300 (loose balls use 600), so the arcs read well. A 30-yd bullet (about\n  1.2 s) peaks about 3 yd up, and a lob (about 1.6 s) about 6 yd up, a rainbow over the defender.\n  - **Accuracy:** P gets a gaussian offset with σ = `T.aimError` (0.035) × distance × (1.3 − AIM × 0.06) × (pressure:\n    a rusher within 2 yd 1.6; throwing on the run 1.25) + mods.\n  - **Max range:** `T.maxPass` (55 yd) + ARM × 1.5 yd. Beyond it the ball is short.\n  - **In flight:** the ball spins (draw the spiral, §12.7). A marker shows the landing point on the ground for the\n    receiving team's human and the defending human (an X in team color, with a ring that shrinks as it nears).\n  - **Arrival** (the ball at z ≤ 34 near P): resolve the catch (§7.7). If no one is in range, or the ball hits the\n    ground, the pass is incomplete (a bounce: a short random-direction `loose` bounce for 0.6 s, cosmetic, dead).\n- **Kick:** analytic flight to the landing point the meter gives, by `E.arc(from, landing, T.kickGravity (260),\n  { apex })`, with apex `T.kickApex` (punts 260, kickoffs 220, FGs and PATs 150, squibs 20, onside 60). The hang time\n  follows: a punt is about 2.8 s. A high kick may leave the top of the screen. The KICK shot zooms out to 0.75, and\n  the shadow and landing marker keep the ball readable. On landing it goes `loose`: the `E.Body` ball, bounce 0.45,\n  with friction. A football bounces erratically, so on each bounce add a random ±25° turn and ±30% speed from\n  `FF.rng.sim`.\n- **Loose:** an `E.Body` (r 3, bounce 0.45, gravity 600) with erratic bounces as above. Players dive at it (§7.7).\n- Out of bounds (y outside the sidelines, or past an end line) kills the ball at the crossing point. A kick out of\n  bounds follows §6.4.\n\n### 7.7 Catches, interceptions, deflections, fumbles and pile-ups\n**Catch resolution** at arrival. Candidates are athletes whose reach covers the ball. The horizontal distance must be\n≤ `T.catchRadius` (14) (+6 jumping in window, +10 diving), and the arrival height must be ≤ reach (34 standing, 50\njumping). For each candidate:\n```\noffense: pc = T.catchBase (0.66) + 0.03*HND - 0.22*(defenders within 14) - (bullet ? 0.10 : 0)\n             + (jump in window ? 0.08 : 0) + bus.mod('catch.p')        // Glue Gloves, Butterfingers, Hot Streak\ndefense: pi = 0.10 + 0.035*HND + (jump in window ? 0.12 : 0) + (in front of receiver ? 0.10 : 0) + bus.mod('int.p')\n```\n- Order the candidates by distance to the ball. Each nearest defender rolls first: INT with `pi`, else a\n  **deflection** with 0.35 (the ball pops up to z 20–40 with a random velocity and can be caught again).\n- Then the receiver rolls `pc`: a catch, or a **drop** (\"DROPPED!\", the ball bounces dead).\n- Cheats and specials can force outcomes: `forceCatch`, `forceINT`, Golden Spiral (no INT).\n- A catch gives the receiver the ball live with his velocity kept (catch and run). Control follows (§9.4).\n\n**Fumbles and loose balls.** The ball pops out with a velocity away from the hit plus up 60–110 u/s. Every athlete\nwithin `T.pileRadius` (5 yd) of the ball gets the brain behavior `recover` (dive at the ball). Humans dive with b1.\n- The first athlete whose dive or feet touch the ball while z ≤ 6 gets a **recovery**. Rolled, not instant: if 2+\n  athletes are within 1.5 yd at that moment, a **pile-up** starts.\n- **Pile-up** (1.6 s): every athlete within 2.5 yd of the ball dives in (poses `PILE`, stacked by z offsets), a dust\n  or mud cloud, the camera punches in, the crowd \"OOOH\". Then the ref \"digs\" (comic) and signals possession, a roll\n  weighted by each piler's distance to the ball when it started (closer is more likely) and STR. \"WHO'S GOT IT?! ...\n  THE FIASCOS HAVE IT!\"\n- A loose ball that goes out of bounds belongs to the team that last had it.\n\n### 7.8 Kicking and the kick meter\nA 3-press meter in screen space near the kicker (§14.7):\n1. b1 starts the needle sweeping a power bar. It rises at `T.meterSpeed` (1.4 sweeps/s), then falls back.\n2. b1 again sets the **power** (0–1). Past 0.95 is the red zone: +5% distance but the accuracy needle runs 1.5×\n   faster.\n3. The accuracy needle sweeps across a center band. b1 sets **accuracy** (−1..1). |acc| ≤ the band\n   (`T.accBand` 0.12; Super Foot ×2.5) is straight; otherwise the kick hooks by acc × 9° (FG) or acc × 14° (punt\n   and kickoff).\n- Before step 1 the stick aims: ±15° for punts and kickoffs, ±4° for FGs (the Kick Cam shows the aim line).\n- Distance: FG = 25 + power × (25 + KCK × 1.6) yd, so KCK 7 at full power is about 61 yd, before wind. Punt: 30–55\n  yd. Kickoff: as §6.4.\n- The FG test: when the ball reaches the end line's x, it's good if y is between the uprights (±49.5) and z > 53.\n  A ball off an upright (within 3 units) bounces \"DOINK!\" and is no good (or good if it falls inside: roll it).\n- CPU kickers: power and accuracy from KCK and difficulty, plus gaussian noise (σ 0.08 / 0.10).\n- A mouse left click, b1 and Space all press the meter.\n\n### 7.9 Blocking\n- **Engagement:** a blocker and a rusher overlap, or come within 1.5 yd with the blocker facing him, which locks them\n  as a pair: they hold 0.8 yd apart, facing each other.\n- **Contest:** each step the pair moves together with velocity `T.blockPush` (18 u/s) × (STR_r − STR_b +\n  rushMoveBonus + (turbo ? 2 : 0)) toward the rusher's goal (the QB or the carrier) when positive, or the blocker's\n  drive direction when negative. Run blocks add the blocker's drive (+1.5).\n- **Shed:** the rusher sheds when his shed timer runs out. `shedTime = T.shedBase (1.9 s) × (STR_b / STR_r) ×\n  rand(0.7, 1.3)`, or ∞ under Brick Wall. On a shed the rusher gets a 0.3 s burst (×1.2) toward his target, and the\n  blocker staggers 0.3 s. A human rusher can mash b3 (□) to cut his timer by 0.12 s per press (a swim move; max 6).\n- **Pancake:** when STR_b − STR_r ≥ 3, a 15% roll on engagement knocks the rusher down for 1.2 s. \"PANCAKE!\"\n- Pass blocks mirror the most dangerous unblocked rusher in the blocker's zone (left, middle, right). Run blocks\n  follow the play's assignment (§8.2).\n- A human-controlled lineman (co-op, or by switching) moves freely. Engagement then happens on contact.\n\n---\n\n## 8. The AI (`33-ai.js`, `34-ai-coach.js`)\n\n### 8.1 Brains and behaviors\nEvery athlete not held by a human (or by a remote player) has a brain. A brain picks a **behavior** from its play\nassignment and the phase, re-thinks at 20 Hz (staggered: `(step + index) % 6 === 0`), and every step turns the current\nbehavior into an intent (a move vector, turbo, presses). Reaction delays come from `AWR` and difficulty: an AI sees a\n**delayed** world. It reads other athletes' positions from a 0.1–0.4 s history buffer, which makes cutbacks, jukes\nand play fakes work against it.\n\n| Behavior | Role | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `formation` | all, PRESNAP | jog to the formation spot, face the line, take the stance |\n| `passBlock(zone)` | OL, RB on some plays | hold a spot 1.5–2.5 yd in front of the QB in his zone, mirror the most dangerous rusher there, engage |\n| `runBlock(target, drive)` | OL, WR, TE | engage the assigned defender and drive him along `drive`, or climb to the 2nd level |\n| `pull(path, then)` | G on sweeps and counters | run the path, then lead-block the first defender in front of the carrier |\n| `route(points, after)` | WR, RB | run the waypoints (cut sharply: brake to 60% at a break, 0.12 s), then `after`: `settle` (find the nearest hole in the zones, face the QB), `scramble` (work toward the sideline the QB scrambles to) or `block` |\n| `carry(path)` | the carrier | follow the play's path while it's open, then **free running**: score 16 directions each 0.1 s, `forward·w1 − Σ threat(defender) − sideline penalty − crowding`, where threat = closing speed / distance² for defenders within 8 yd; pick the best with hysteresis; turbo when no defender is within 4 yd or one is closing from behind; evasive moves, below |\n| `qbPass(drop, reads)` | QB | drop back to the play's spot. From `readT` (0.6–1.0 s) read the receivers in order. **Openness** = the distance from the nearest defender to the receiver's arrival point, minus 8 per defender within 3 yd of the pass lane. Throw to the first receiver with openness > `T.aiOpen` (3 yd; by difficulty) whose route is ≥ 40% done. At 2.8 s, or with a rusher within 2 yd, throw to the best, scramble if the best is < 1 yd open, or throw it away out of bounds (Pro and Fiasco difficulty). Scramble: `carry`, and still look to throw while behind the line. |\n| `qbRun` | QB on sneaks and the run fakes of draws | his part of the play script |\n| `rush(gap)` | DL, blitzers | attack the gap toward the QB, engage blockers, try to shed; once free, `pursue` the QB; when the QB throws, `pursue` the ball |\n| `manCover(target)` | DB, LB | keep a cushion of `T.cushion` (2 yd, smaller near the goal line) on the goal side of the target, matching his speed with the reaction delay; when the ball is in the air, break to the arrival point after the reaction delay and jump when within 2 yd as it arrives |\n| `zoneCover(zone)` | DB, LB | drop to the zone center; pick the most threatening receiver inside (zone radius + 3 yd), weighted by depth and how open he is; shadow him; release him when he leaves; the ball in the air is as above |\n| `spy(QB)` | LB | mirror the QB at 5 yd; when the QB crosses the line, `pursue` |\n| `pursue(carrier)` | all defenders, once there is a carrier past the line, a scramble, or a return | head for the intercept point: the smallest t with `|c + v_c·t − d| ≤ speed_d·t`, solved in 0.05 s steps up to 3 s, with the angle limited by the sideline. Tackle decision below |\n| `kickCover(lane)` | the kicking team | sprint downfield in lanes 6 yd apart, then `pursue` the returner |\n| `returnBlock` | the receiving team | form a wedge of 3 in front of the returner's lane; engage the nearest coverage man |\n| `recover` | everyone near a loose ball | dive at the ball (§7.7) |\n| `celebrate`, `dejected`, `idle` | after the play | poses (§10.6) |\n\n**Evasive moves for an AI carrier.** When a tackler is within 1.6 yd and closing: against a diving tackler, hurdle\nwith chance 0.25 + ACC × 0.03; against a frontal one, stiff arm with 0.2 + STR × 0.03; against one from the side,\nspin with 0.2 + ACC × 0.03. One move per 0.8 s. Difficulty scales these.\n\n**The tackle decision (AI tacklers).** With a reachable carrier, dive when the distance is under 1.8 yd and the angle\nto his predicted position is under 35°. Use a Big Hit when turbo ≥ 0.15 and a roll of 0.2 + TCK × 0.03 passes (×1.5\nwhen Hot). Otherwise keep pursuing to contact (a wrap-up on contact counts as a tackle attempt with −0.08).\n\n### 8.2 Play assignments (data to brain)\n`FF.data.plays[id].assign[slot]` (Appendix A.2) is read when PRESNAP ends:\n- Offense: `{ block: 'pass' | 'run', zone, target, drive }`, `{ pull: [[x, y], ...], then: 'lead' }`,\n  `{ route: [[x, y], ...], after: 'settle', read: n }`, `{ carry: [[x, y], ...], handoffT }`, and the QB's\n  `{ drop: [x, y], reads: ['WR2', 'WR1', 'RB'], readT, fake }`.\n- Defense: `{ rush: 'A' | 'B' | 'C' | 'contain' }`, `{ man: 'WR1' | 'WR2' | 'RB' | 'C' }`,\n  `{ zone: { at: [x, y], r } }`, `{ spy: 'QB' }`.\n- Play coordinates are in **yards** from the ball spot: x downfield (+ is the offense's attacking direction), y\n  across (+ is the offense's right). Mirroring (FLIP) negates y. Defense coordinates use the same frame (x is the\n  distance past the line toward the defense's own goal). The sim converts with `dir`.\n- Targets in defense assignments point at offensive slots. If the offense's formation lacks a slot, fall back to\n  zone `[8, 0], r 7`.\n\n### 8.3 Special teams brains\nKick coverage, wedge returns, the punt shield (3 blockers in front of the punter for 1.5 s), FG protection\n(pass-block in place) and the FG rush (all-out `rush`; 2 jumpers in the middle `jump` at the kick).\n\n### 8.4 Difficulty\n| | Rookie | Pro | Fiasco |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CPU reaction delay | 0.38 s | 0.24 s | 0.13 s |\n| CPU tackle p × | 0.85 | 1.0 | 1.12 |\n| CPU QB openness threshold | 4.5 yd | 3 yd | 2 yd |\n| CPU kick noise × | 1.5 | 1.0 | 0.6 |\n| CPU coverage cushion × | 1.3 | 1.0 | 0.85 |\n| Catch-up assist (`T.rubberBand`): when the human trails by ≥ 14, his team gets +0.05 catch, +0.04 tackle and −0.04 to the CPU's | on | off | off |\nStory chapters set a difficulty per chapter (§15.1), scaled by the Difficulty setting.\n\n### 8.5 The coach AI (play calling, cards, decisions)\n- **Situation features:** down, distance, field position, score difference, time left, quarter, and the opponent's\n  last 5 play types (Fiasco difficulty only).\n- **Offense:** start from the team's tendency weights (`ai.run`, `ai.pass`, `ai.trick`, §18.1), then apply the\n  situation rules in order:\n  - 3rd/4th & 10+ means pass deep or mid (×2.5).\n  - Less than 3 to go means run, sneak or quick passes (×2).\n  - Goal to go inside the 5 means GOAL LINE runs, slants and fades.\n  - In the last 1:00 while trailing: sideline routes (Quick Outs, Out & Up) ×3, and get out of bounds.\n  - Leading by 9+ in the last 2:00: runs and kneels.\n  - 4th down: field goal if the kick is ≤ `T.cpuFgRange` (45 yd) + KCK. Go for it if the distance is ≤ 3 and the ball\n    is past midfield, or if trailing late. Punt otherwise. A 5% fake punt roll on 4th & ≤ 5.\n- **Defense:** counter the offense's tendency (blitz on 3rd & long ×1.8, goal line inside the 3, prevent while\n  leading late), with 20% randomness so the CPU can be read only roughly.\n- **Cards:** the CPU uses a card when its trigger matches (§10.2) and it holds one. At most one card per 3 plays.\n- **PAT:** go for 2 when down by 2, 5, 9, 10 or 12 after the TD in the second half. Otherwise kick.\n- **COACH SAYS:** run the offense logic for the human's side, and mark the top pick with a star badge on the\n  play-call screen. If the human doesn't choose, it plays when the timer runs out.\n\n### 8.6 The Play Lab (an AI quality tool and test)\n`FF.dev.playLab(offPlay, defPlay, n, { seed, teams, difficulty })` runs n plays headless and fast (presentation off)\nfrom midfield on 1st & 20, both sides CPU. It returns `{ avgYards, completion, sacks, ints, fumbles, tds, avgTime,\nmaxTime, stuck }`, where `stuck` counts plays over 25 s or an athlete idle more than 3 s while LIVE.\n**The bands it must stay in** (every offensive play against each of the 8 non-kick defenses, n = 30, Pro, Fiascos vs\nMudhogs):\n- Nothing is `stuck`, and every play ends within 25 s.\n- A pass play's completion, averaged over the 8 defenses, is 35–85% (HAIL MARY 5–40%), and its sack rate is under\n  25%.\n- A run play averages 1.5–9 yd over the 8 defenses.\n- No play averages over 25 yd over the 8 defenses (no unstoppable play), and no defense holds every play under 1 yd\n  (no unbeatable defense).\nIn the browser the Dev menu runs the same lab on screen with debug overlays (§17).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Controls and human control (`35-control.js`)\n\n### 9.1 The input map (`FF.KEYS`)\nA custom preset (scene `input: FF.KEYS`). The order matters: `Input.halfKeys` gives a shared-keyboard half its 6\nbuttons in this order. The engine's standard `confirm`/`cancel` are redefined so J and K don't mean menu\nconfirm/cancel.\n```js\nFF.KEYS = {\n  up: E.Input.DEFAULT.up, down: E.Input.DEFAULT.down, left: E.Input.DEFAULT.left, right: E.Input.DEFAULT.right,\n  start: ['Enter', 'NumpadEnter', 'Pad9'], pause: ['Escape', 'KeyP', 'Pad9'],\n  confirm: ['Enter', 'Space', 'NumpadEnter', 'Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape', 'Backspace', 'Pad1'],\n  b1: ['Space', 'KeyK', 'Pad0'],   // ✕ / A    hike, dive, tackle, kick meter, pick\n  b2: ['KeyL', 'Pad1'],            // ○ / B    stiff arm, switch player, back\n  b3: ['KeyJ', 'Pad2'],            // □ / X    spin, strip, flip play\n  b4: ['KeyI', 'Pad3'],            // △ / Y    hurdle, jump, catch, Fiasco Card\n  turbo: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight', 'Pad7'],   // R2 / RT\n  lb: ['KeyQ', 'Pad4'],            // L1 / LB  lateral, previous tab\n  rb: ['KeyE', 'Pad5'],            // R1 / RB  lateral, next tab\n  r1: ['Digit1'], r2: ['Digit2'], r3: ['Digit3'], r4: ['Digit4']   // keyboard: throw to receiver 1-4\n};\n```\nCheck `E.Input.DEFAULT` for the exact move codes (WASD, the arrows, the d-pad, `PadAxis*`). The Controls screen names\nthem with `menu.controls: { b1: 'Hike · Dive · Tackle · Kick', b2: 'Stiff arm · Switch player', b3: 'Spin · Strip ·\nFlip play', b4: 'Hurdle · Jump · Catch · Card', turbo: 'Turbo', lb: 'Lateral · Tab left', rb: 'Lateral · Tab right',\nr1: 'Throw to receiver 1', ... }`.\n\n### 9.2 The control table by context\n| Context | Action | PlayStation | Xbox | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Menus | move the focus | d-pad / left stick | d-pad / left stick | arrows / WASD | hover moves the focus |\n| | select | ✕ | A | Enter / Space | left click |\n| | back | ○ | B | Esc / Backspace | the on-screen BACK button |\n| | tabs | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Q / E | click a tab |\n| Play call | pick the play | ✕ | A | Enter / Space | click a card |\n| | flip it | □ | X | J | click FLIP |\n| | Fiasco Card | △ (opens the card row) | Y | I | click a card slot |\n| Pre-snap, offense | hike | ✕ | A | Space | click the QB |\n| Pre-snap, defense | move your defender | stick | stick | WASD | — |\n| | switch defender | ○ | B | L | click a defender |\n| QB behind the line | throw to receiver ✕ ○ □ △ | ✕ ○ □ △ | A B X Y | 1 2 3 4 (or K L J I) | click a receiver |\n| | Rocket Pass | hold R2 + a receiver | RT + | Shift + | Shift + click |\n| | scramble | stick (+ R2) | stick (+ RT) | WASD (+ Shift) | — |\n| Ball carrier | turbo | R2 | RT | Shift | — |\n| | dive | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n| | stiff arm | ○ | B | L | — |\n| | spin | □ | X | J | — |\n| | hurdle | △ | Y | I | — |\n| | lateral | L1 / R1 | LB / RB | Q / E | — |\n| Ball in the air, offense | jump catch | △ | Y | I | — |\n| | diving catch | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n| Defense, live | switch to the defender nearest the ball | ○ | B | L | click a defender |\n| | tackle (dive) | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n| | Big Hit | R2 + ✕ | RT + A | Shift + Space | — |\n| | jump, swat, intercept | △ | Y | I | — |\n| | strip | □ | X | J | — |\n| | swim move (while blocked) | □ repeatedly | X | J | — |\n| After the whistle | late hit | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n| Kicking | aim | stick ← → | stick | A / D, ← / → | — |\n| | the meter (3 presses) | ✕ | A | Space | left click |\n| Kick return in the end zone | take a knee | ✕ | A | Space | — |\n| TD celebration | pick a dance | ○ □ △ | B X Y | L J I | — |\n| Holding a boss star | his special (§10.5) | L1 + R1 together | LB + RB | Q + E | — |\n| Anywhere | pause | Options | Menu | Esc / P | the corner menu button |\n| Anywhere | change the camera view | — | — | V | — |\nThe mouse wheel zooms the camera within the scene's range (engine standard, `camera: { zoom: [.7, 1.4] }`).\n\n### 9.3 Prompts that follow the device\n- `FF.glyph.chip(g, x, y, action, { player, scale, device })` draws a button chip for that player's last device\n  (`input.lastDevice`):\n  - **Gamepad, PlayStation** (the default): a dark disc with the symbol drawn as pixels in its classic color: ✕ blue","numLines":450,"startLine":400,"totalLines":2515}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016x8UVJ1NZ3gxgNVQVMzj3F","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi1eJQYEkD7zFyRCjMEz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011gHpUwRUqP6ScL6Uc999yE","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-070229__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","offset":850,"limit":450},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16990,"cache_read_input_tokens":37409,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16990},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1931e3d-b97b-4f9d-8b9d-c7660f7740b5","uuid":"9d3fe943-0407-4a37-ba06-f6262e029848","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:02:42.556Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi1eJ6gSmMkKX7Xcrp9P","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_011gHpUwRUqP6ScL6Uc999yE":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-070229__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","offset":850,"limit":450}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011gHpUwRUqP6ScL6Uc999yE","type":"tool_result","content":"850\t    `#7aa8ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#5ae0a0`.\n851\t  - **Gamepad, Xbox** (Settings, Controller, Button symbols): colored discs with letters: A green, B red, X blue,\n852\t    Y yellow.\n853\t  - Shoulders and triggers are rounded rectangles labeled L1, R1, R2 or LB, RB, RT. Options/Menu are small pills.\n854\t  - **Keyboard:** a keycap (a light rectangle with a darker bottom edge) with `input.label(action, 'keyboard')`. Wide\n855\t    caps for SPACE and SHIFT.\n856\t  - **Mouse:** a mouse icon with the left or right button lit, used where the mouse does that context's job (clicking\n857\t    a receiver, a card, the kick meter). Elsewhere the keyboard chip.\n858\t  - Labels come from `input.label(action, device)` after rebinding. Never hard-code a key. Map the engine's label to\n859\t    a chip style: glyph labels (`✕ ○ □ △ A B X Y L1 ...`) are pad chips, everything else is keycaps.\n860\t- The receiver chips over receivers' heads show b1–b4 on a pad, r1–r4 (1–4) on a keyboard (b1–b4 on a shared-keyboard\n861\t  half), and a mouse cursor badge on the hovered receiver.\n862\t- **The prompt strip** (bottom right, 1–5 chips with words, §14.6) shows the current context's actions only: \"✕ HIKE\",\n863\t  then \"✕ ○ □ THROW · R2 ROCKET\", then \"□ SPIN ○ STIFF △ HURDLE ✕ DIVE R2 TURBO\". It changes the moment the context\n864\t  changes. Each local player's strip sits in their own corner.\n865\t\n866\t### 9.4 Who controls whom\n867\t- **Offense, one human:** the QB through the snap. After a handoff or pitch he controls the carrier. After a throw he\n868\t  controls the **target receiver** (camera and control follow the ball). After a catch, the catcher.\n869\t- **Offense, two humans (co-op):** player A has the QB, then the ball. Player B picks a receiver at the play call\n870\t  (□ cycles the highlighted receiver on his panel; default WR1) and runs it himself; the route line shows as a guide.\n871\t  If B's receiver catches the ball, B has the ball and A takes the nearest teammate (to block). Otherwise B keeps his\n872\t  receiver (to block downfield).\n873\t- **Defense:** each human controls one defender. At PRESNAP the first human gets the S (a safety sees the whole play\n874\t  and can roam) and the second gets LB1, both of which can be moved before the snap. During the play, b2 or a click\n875\t  switches to the free defender nearest the ball (on a pass in the air: the one nearest the landing point). With the **Auto-switch** setting (on by default), a\n876\t  pass in the air or a carrier past the line switches each defensive human to the best unheld defender, with 0.5 s\n877\t  hysteresis.\n878\t- **Turnovers:** at an INT or a fumble recovery, the humans of the side that gained the ball take the carrier (the\n879\t  first human) and the nearest teammates. The other side's humans take their nearest defenders.\n880\t- **Kicks:** the kicking human runs the meter. The receiving human controls the returner.\n881\t- **Hub sessions:** the same rules, keyed by seat (§16.4).\n882\t- A ring under each held athlete in the player's color (local: `E.PLAYER_COLORS`; sessions: the seat color), and a\n883\t  tag over him (\"P1\", or the hub name) during PRESNAP and for 1 s after each switch.\n884\t\n885\t### 9.5 The mouse on the field\n886\t- Every athlete is drawn with `r.actor(..., { pick: athleteId })`. Each step, `r.pick(...input.pointer())` gives the\n887\t  hovered athlete. Hovering a receiver while QB highlights his chip, and a left click (`input.clicked()`) throws to\n888\t  him (Shift held: a Rocket Pass). Hovering a defender on defense outlines him, and a click switches to him.\n889\t- Pre-snap on offense, a click on the QB or the ball hikes.\n890\t- During the kick meter, a click anywhere presses it.\n891\t- Clicking open ground does nothing in play. A click is never bound to b1, which avoids accidental hikes and dives.\n892\t- The pointer hides after 2 s without mouse movement. `input.lastDevice` switches back to the keyboard on the next\n893\t  key.\n894\t\n895\t---\n896\t\n897\t## 10. The over-the-top systems (`36-ots.js`)\n898\t\n899\t### 10.1 Hot Streak (\"BLAZING!\")\n900\t- **Big plays** are: a TD, a catch of 20+ yd, a run of 15+ yd, a sack, an INT, a forced fumble, and a Big Hit that\n901\t  tackles. Each athlete keeps a heat count. +1 per big play by him. It resets when the opponents make a big play\n902\t  against him (a sack of that QB, a tackle for a loss on that carrier, a pick of that QB) or when they score.\n903\t- At heat 3 he goes **BLAZING** for `T.hotPlays` (6 plays), or until a reset event happens:\n904\t  - unlimited turbo, +12% speed;\n905\t  - offense: tackle p −0.15 against him;\n906\t  - defense: tackle p +0.15, Big Hit fumble ×2;\n907\t  - a QB: aim error ×0.6 and fire-trail passes;\n908\t  - visuals: fire particles at his feet and shoulders (`particles.fire` every 0.05 s, emissive), a flaming ball while\n909\t    he has it, an orange light on him at night;\n910\t  - audio: the music's `hot` layer comes in (`audio.layer('hot', 1, .5)`), the crowd chants, the announcer's\n911\t    `hotStreakOn` lines.\n912\t- A team can have 2 blazing athletes. The HUD shows a flame icon by his name and a heat pip count (1–3) on held\n913\t  athletes' tags.\n914\t\n915\t### 10.2 The Fiasco Meter and the Fiasco Cards (the items)\n916\t**The Fiasco Meter** is the title's mechanic: every disaster is an opportunity. Each team's meter (0–100) fills when\n917\tthings go wrong for it:\n918\t\n919\t| Event | Fill |\n920\t|---|---|\n921\t| fumble lost | 35 |\n922\t| INT thrown | 35 |\n923\t| safety allowed | 40 |\n924\t| sacked | 20 |\n925\t| turnover on downs | 25 |\n926\t| opponent's TD | 20 |\n927\t| dropped pass | 10 |\n928\t| Big Hit received | 10 |\n929\t| late hit received | 5 |\n930\t\n931\tWhen it fills, the team **draws a Fiasco Card** at random from its pool (story: the cards you've unlocked;\n932\texhibition: all 8), up to 3 held. Then the meter resets. A HUD flourish plays: a card flips in, \"FIASCO BREAK!\".\n933\tEach team also starts a game with cards: story, a loadout of up to 3 picked on the chapter screen; exhibition, 1\n934\trandom card each; hub rounds, 1 each, dealt from `AIHub.rng('cards')`.\n935\t\n936\t**Using a card:** on the play-call screen press b4 (△) to open the card row and pick one (or click a slot). One card\n937\tper play. A card on offense needs an offensive card and so on; a card that doesn't fit the side is greyed out. Its\n938\teffect lasts as its text says. Cards are data: `{ id, name, side: 'off' | 'def' | 'any' | 'kick', text, art,\n939\tapply(ctx) }`. `apply` registers bus modifiers for the play or for N plays. The CPU trigger is in §8.5.\n940\t\n941\t| Card | Side | Effect | CPU uses it when |\n942\t|---|---|---|---|\n943\t| ROCKET CLEATS | off | your carrier and receivers +20% speed this play | 3rd & 10+, or trailing in the last 2:00 |\n944\t| GLUE GLOVES | off | your receivers can't drop the ball; contested catches +30% this play | a pass play on 3rd or 4th down |\n945\t| BRICK WALL | off | your blockers can't be shed for 3.5 s | a pass play on 3rd & long |\n946\t| HOT SAUCE | any | the held athlete (CPU: its best player) goes BLAZING for 3 plays | first chance in the 2nd half |\n947\t| MEGA HIT | def | this play's first tackle is a Mega Hit: launch × 1.6, fumble chance 60%, the slow-mo cam | 3rd down defense, or the opponent in the red zone |\n948\t| BUTTERFINGERS | def | opposing receivers' catch chance −35% this play | 3rd & long defense |\n949\t| MASCOT MAYHEM | any | your mascot runs on as a 12th player for the play: he blocks the nearest defender on offense, rushes the passer on defense, with `noClip` through blockers. The announcers lose their minds. | trailing by 7+ |\n950\t| SUPER FOOT | kick | the next kick: +15 yd, an accuracy band ×2.5, the power needle ×0.7 speed | a FG of 45+ yd, or a PAT in the last minute |\n951\t\n952\t### 10.3 Big Hits, Mega Hits, Dinged and the flying helmet\n953\t- **Big Hit:** turbo + b1 (§7.4–7.5). Hit-stop 0.08 s, `game.shake(4)`, `game.hitFx` with an impact star, grass, mud\n954\t  or snow bits, a camera punch-in to 1.25× for 0.35 s, slow motion (`timeScale` 0.4 for 0.3 s, honoring reduce\n955\t  motion), a comic \"BOOM!\" / \"WHAM!\" / \"KER-RUNCH!\" burst over the hit, and the crowd \"OOOOH\".\n956\t- **Mega Hit** (the card, or a 1-in-6 roll of a Blazing Big Hit): launch × 1.6, the victim spins in the air (PoseFX\n957\t  spin), hit-stop 0.12, the slow-mo cam (`timeScale` 0.25 for 0.6 s), a white flash (`game.flash`, honoring the\n958\t  flashes setting).\n959\t- **Dinged:** the victim of a Mega Hit, or of 2 Big Hits in one drive, is Dinged for 2 plays: −10% speed and little\n960\t  cartoon stars orbiting his helmet (3 tiny glints circling, emissive).\n961\t- **The flying helmet:** on a Mega Hit (and 25% of Big Hits) the victim's helmet pops off as a separate bouncing\n962\t  prop: a shape `helmet` in team colors, an `E.Body` with bounce 0.5. His hair shows until the next PRESNAP, when he\n963\t  picks it up (cosmetic). The announcer says \"HIS HELMET IS IN A DIFFERENT AREA CODE!\"\n964\t\n965\t### 10.4 Late hits\n966\tAfter the whistle, for 1.2 s, b1 near an opponent does a late hit (cosmetic knockdown). The refs throw their caps\n967\t(Fiasco rules: no flags). A crowd reaction, the announcer's outrage lines, a stat. AI athletes late-hit too, by team\n968\tpersonality (the Mudhogs often, the Dynasty sneakily).\n969\t\n970\t### 10.5 Boss specials (story bosses; also in exhibition)\n971\tEach special is used by the AI, or by a human holding that star (in exhibition or a hub round), who presses **lb and\n972\trb together** (L1 + R1, LB + RB, Q + E) within 0.1 s while its conditions hold. A single shoulder press waits those\n973\t0.1 s before it becomes a lateral, so the chord never laterals by accident. For the Golden Spiral, press the chord,\n974\tthen a receiver button within 1 s. A badge shows \"SPECIAL READY\" on the star's tag. NIGHT VISION is passive.\n975\t\n976\t| Star | Special | Rules |\n977\t|---|---|---|\n978\t| BIG BERTHA BOUDREAUX (Mudhogs RB #44) | **MUDSLIDE** | once per drive: for 1.2 s she lowers her shoulder; the first tackler in front is bowled over (down 1.2 s) unless it's a Big Hit from the front; mud spray FX |\n979\t| LUMEN LOPEZ (Owls QB #1) | **NIGHT VISION** | always on: his reads see 1 extra yard of openness, and his aim error ×0.8 |\n980\t| ZAP ZAPATA (Owls WR #11) | **AFTERBURNER** | once per play: a 1.0 s burst at ×1.35 speed that leaves neon light trails (`ghost` afterimages in cyan) |\n981\t| AVALANCHE (Yetis DE #99) | **SNOWPLOW** | once per drive: his engagement drives the blocker straight back at 3× push for 1.5 s; a snow spray |\n982\t| BROCK STERLING III (Dynasty QB #12) | **GOLDEN SPIRAL** | once per drive: a perfect pass, no aim error, it can't be intercepted (defenders may still deflect it with 0.15), gold sparkle trail |\n983\t| THE MOUNTAIN, MALU FALEOLO (Dynasty LB #55) | **SEISMIC SACK** | once per half, when within 3 yd of the carrier: he leaps (0.6 s) and slams down; everyone within 2.5 yd is knocked down; a shockwave ring (`particles.ring`), shake 7, \"SEISMIC!\" |\n984\tThe commentary has lines for each, and the HUD shows a banner with the star's portrait for 1.2 s when one fires.\n985\t\n986\t### 10.6 Celebrations\n987\tAfter a TD the scorer celebrates. Locally the scorer's human picks one: b2 the Charleston, b3 the Body Roll, b4 the\n988\tHelicopter (breakdance). Otherwise it's random by personality, from the list below. In hub sessions it's always\n989\trandom (no player-triggered emotes, §16.6).\n990\t\n991\t| Celebration | How it's animated |\n992\t|---|---|\n993\t| Spike | a throw-down attack spec, the ball bounces high |\n994\t| Charleston, Body Roll | mocap `Dance Charleston` / `Dance Body Roll` |\n995\t| Helicopter | mocap `Breakdance_Helicopter` |\n996\t| Backflip | mocap `Backflip` |\n997\t| Fist pump | mocap `Victory Fist Pump` |\n998\t| Stands Dive | he leaps into the end zone stands: `body.jump`, the crowd lifts him, bodies bob |\n999\t| Group Huddle Dance | 3 nearest teammates do `Dance_Loop` in a ring |\n1000\t\n1001\tTeammates near him do `Cheering_Two_Hands`. Defenders kneel (`pose: 'kneel'`) or put their hands on their helmets\n1002\t(PoseFX). Pyro cannons fire, confetti falls (`particles.bits` in team colors from z 160), the crowd goes wild.\n1003\t\n1004\t### 10.7 Mascots\n1005\tEach team has a mascot on the near sideline: a Humanoid in team colors with a big costume head drawn by the gear\n1006\thook (§12.2). They react: dance on scores, slump on turnovers, and do a backflip on a Big Hit. They flip the coin, and\n1007\trun on with Mascot Mayhem (`noClip`, a cartoon dust cloud, a squeaky sfx). Fumbles the Flaming Tumbleweed sets\n1008\thimself on fire when the Fiascos score (fire particles), and trips over the power cable in chapter 3 (§15.1).\n1009\t\n1010\t### 10.8 Refs\n1011\tTwo refs on the field (an umpire 6 yd behind the defense, a back judge 20 yd deep) and a line judge on the near\n1012\tsideline. They're cosmetic Humanoids in striped shirts (the gear hook draws the stripes), with poses for signals:\n1013\tTOUCHDOWN (both arms up), FIRST DOWN (an arm pointing at the offense's goal), INCOMPLETE (arms crossing, a wave),\n1014\tFUMBLE / POSSESSION (pointing), and \"Sure, why not\" (a shrug) after a late hit. A play running through a ref knocks\n1015\thim over comically (the announcer: \"AND THE REF GOES DOWN! HE'S FINE! HE'S... MOSTLY FINE!\").\n1016\t\n1017\t### 10.9 LIGHTS OUT (chapter 3's event; also a Dev toggle)\n1018\tFor 3 plays: `lights.ambient` 0.12, `darkness` 0.9, the light towers off. Every uniform's trim, the Owls' neon, the\n1019\tball, the yard lines and the Fiascos' glow paint (their trim turns `#7aff6a` with glow) stay bright as emissive (the\n1020\tgear hook draws trims with `px.glow` and queues them `emissive: true`). Camera flashes in the crowd sparkle.\n1021\tCommentary and the crowd play along, then the lights come back with a thunk and a flicker.\n1022\t\n1023\t---\n1024\t\n1025\t## 11. Presentation\n1026\t\n1027\t### 11.1 The camera director (`37-camera.js`)\n1028\tOur own smoothing (`E.smoothDamp` on x, y and zoom), `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update, `game.setZoom(z)`, and\n1029\t`cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)`. The player's own zoom (wheel, `-` `=`) multiplies ours.\n1030\t\n1031\t| Shot | When | Focus and zoom |\n1032\t|---|---|---|\n1033\t| PRESNAP | the formation | x = LOS + dir × 5 yd; y = the ball's y × 0.6 + MID_Y × 0.4; zoom 1.0 |\n1034\t| RUN | a carrier | the carrier + v × `T.camLead` (0.35 s); zoom 1.05 |\n1035\t| PASS_PRE | QB with the ball on a pass play | `cam.frame([qb, ...receivers within 30 yd], { margin: 40, zoom: [.62, 1] })` |\n1036\t| BALL_AIR | a pass in the air | between the ball's ground point and its landing point, zoomed to fit both (min .62) |\n1037\t| KICK | kickoffs and punts | follow the ball, zoom .75; on the catch, RUN on the returner |\n1038\t| KICKCAM | FG and PAT | cut (`{ fade: 0 }` style, 0.15 s dissolve) to the `kickcam` view behind the kicker; follow the ball to the posts; hold 1.2 s on the result; cut back |\n1039\t| PUNCH | a Big Hit or Mega Hit | zoom × 1.25 over 0.12 s, hold 0.25 s, ease back over 0.4 s |\n1040\t| CELEBRATE | after a TD | the scorer, zoom 1.35 |\n1041\t| CUTAWAY | after the celebration (50% of TDs) | pan up to the stands at the end zone for 1.2 s, zoom 1.2: \"the crowd goes wild\" |\n1042\t| REPLAY | §11.2 | `field`, `iso`, `reverse` views at 0.5× and 0.35× |\n1043\t| CUTSCENE | §15.1 | scripted tweens (`game.tween` on a camera target object) |\n1044\tViews are fixed per shot. Never animate the yaw continuously: each new yaw rebakes the floor.\n1045\t\n1046\t### 11.2 Instant replay and the highlight reel (`38-replay.js`)\n1047\t- **Recording:** from the snap to 1 s after the whistle, at 60 Hz (every 2nd step), record a frame per entity:\n1048\t  athletes `[x, y, z, facing, poseCode, poseU, flags]` (flags: carrying, hot, dinged, helmet off), the ball\n1049\t  `[x, y, z, state, spin]`, refs and the mascot. Record presentation events with their time: hit FX, particles\n1050\t  bursts, comic pops, sfx names, card banners. A 12 s ring buffer in `Float32Array`s.\n1051\t- **Playback** is a REPLAY sub-state of the match scene. The sim is frozen and untouched. Rigs are driven from the\n1052\t  frames (positions interpolated, velocity derived for the gait), the recorded events re-fire at their times, and\n1053\t  the audio plays the sfx (the commentary doesn't repeat; the crowd bed stays). Shots: 1) the `field` view following\n1054\t  the ball at 0.5× over the 3 s around the key moment; 2) the `iso` or `reverse` view at 0.35× for the impact. Then\n1055\t  back to the live view.\n1056\t- **The overlay:** a REPLAY badge with a blinking red dot, a VCR timecode, letterbox bars, \"✕ SKIP\" (b1 or a click\n1057\t  skips). The music ducks.\n1058\t- **Triggers:** a TD (when Replays is All big plays or Touchdowns only), a turnover, a Mega Hit, a Big Hit fumble, a\n1059\t  play of 40+ yd, a FG of 50+ yd, a Seismic Sack, a Mascot Mayhem play. Setting: Replays: All big plays (default) /\n1060\t  Touchdowns only / Off. In hub sessions: off.\n1061\t- **Highlight reel:** the 5 most exciting clips of the game, by an excitement score (event priority × yards), are\n1062\t  kept (8 s each). The results screen offers WATCH HIGHLIGHTS, which plays them back to back with captions\n1063\t  (\"Q2 3:12 — DOC DOBSON 48-YD TD RUN\").\n1064\t- **Invariant (tested):** the sim's state hash is the same before and after a replay.\n1065\t\n1066\t### 11.3 Commentary and on-field text (`39-commentary.js`)\n1067\t- **Two announcers:** CHET THUNDERBUNS (play-by-play: loud, excitable) and LOU \"THE TOE\" DIGGS (color: an ex-kicker,\n1068\t  folksy, deadpan).\n1069\t- **Events** have priorities (Appendix D): TD 10, turnover 9, Mega Hit 9, special 8, Big Hit 8, sack 8, long play 7,\n1070\t  Hot Streak 7, mascot 7, FG result 7, late hit 6, card 6, drop 5, first down 4, incomplete 3, weather 2, idle\n1071\t  chatter 1.\n1072\t- **Rules:** at most one line per 2.5 s. A higher priority interrupts a lower one. On events of priority ≥ 7, CHET\n1073\t  speaks and LOU answers 1.2 s later with chance 0.4. No line repeats within the last 8 for that event. Lines take\n1074\t  variables (`{player}`, `{team}`, `{opp}`, `{yards}`, `{score}`).\n1075\t- **The caption:** a non-modal box at the bottom left (keep clear of the prompt strip and the score bug). The\n1076\t  speaker's portrait (`rig.drawPortrait` of the announcer rig: a headset and a blazer) and name, the text typed at\n1077\t  45 chars/s with the babble voice (§13.4). It hides after its length / 15 + 1.5 s. The Commentary setting: On /\n1078\t  Subtitles only (no voice) / Off.\n1079\t- **On-field pops:** comic bursts in world space over the event: BOOM!, WHAM!, KER-RUNCH!, SACKED!, PICKED OFF!,\n1080\t  FUMBLE!, PANCAKE!, BROKEN TACKLE!, DOINK!, BLAZING!, FIASCO BREAK!, SEISMIC!, DROPPED!. `E.font.title` with an\n1081\t  outline over a jagged polygon burst (`px.poly`, 12 points with radii alternating 1 and 0.6). It scales from 0.4 to\n1082\t  1.1 to 1.0 (`outBack`) and lasts 0.9 s. Anchor it with `r.w(x, y, z + 40)`.\n1083\t- **Center text** for game events: TOUCHDOWN! (title scale 4 in the scoring team's colors with a shine sweep), FIRST\n1084\t  DOWN!, TURNOVER!, SAFETY!, IT'S GOOD! / NO GOOD!, TOUCHBACK, 4TH DOWN, RED ZONE, TWO-MINUTE WARNING (if quarters\n1085\t  are 2:00 or longer), LIGHTS OUT!\n1086\t\n1087\t### 11.4 The crowd (`26-crowd.js`)\n1088\t- **Fans** sit on the stand tiers: 2–3 per tile per tier (about 2,400 at the Mega Dome). Each is drawn like the SPORTS\n1089\t  kit's fans (`r.queue` with 2–3 `px.rect`s: shirt, head, an optional hat), with the team colors by section (home\n1090\t  sections, plus an away section by one end zone), and skin and hat variety from a seeded hash. 8% hold signs: a\n1091\t  6×4 rectangle with a tiny-font word (\"FIASCO!\", \"D!\", \"HOGS!\", \"HOOT!\", \"BRRR!\", \"$$$\"). 5% have foam fingers.\n1092\t- **Excitement E** (0–1): big events set it up, `T.crowdDecay` (0.15/s) brings it down. Above 0.6 the fans stand and\n1093\t  bob. On a TD the scoring side's fans jump (+5 z). Below 0.2 they sway.\n1094\t- **The Wave** rolls around the stands every 40–70 s when E is moderate and the home team leads: a sine wave of\n1095\t  stand-ups moving 10 tiles/s.\n1096\t- **Density** by stadium. Tumbleweed Field is 35% full until the story's ending, when it's 100%.\n1097\t- **Camera flashes** at night: random 1-px white dots with tiny lights, more on big plays.\n1098\t- **Audio:** the crowd bed and reactions (§13.3).\n1099\t\n1100\t### 11.5 FX (`40-fx.js`)\n1101\t- **Hits:** `game.hitFx(x, y, z, { power, angle })` with no damage number (leave `damage` out and check that none\n1102\t  shows), `particles.impact`, surface bits (grass `#4a8a3a`/`#2e5a22`, mud `#5a3a1a`, snow `#f0f4ff`), dust, and white\n1103\t  `particles.sparks` on helmet-to-helmet hits.\n1104\t- **Divots:** on Big Hits and dives, a persistent decal of torn turf at the spot, up to 40 per game (the oldest fade).\n1105\t- **Footprints** in snow and mud: small decals behind runners every 0.15 s, fading over 6 s, up to 200.\n1106\t- **Breath puffs** at Frostbite: `particles.smoke` at each head every 1.2 s, on the cosmetic RNG stream.\n1107\t- **Weather:** `game.weather = { kind: 'rain', amount: .6, wind: .2, lightning: .1 }` at the Hog Wallow;\n1108\t  `{ kind: 'snow', amount: .7, wind: .3 }` at Frostbite; set in the match's `enter` (weather ends with the scene).\n1109\t- **Celebrations:** confetti (`particles.bits` in team colors from z 160), fireworks in the sky\n1110\t  (`particles.explosion(..., { debris: false, shake: 0 })` and colored rings at z 300+ over the end zone), pyro columns\n1111\t  from the cannons (`particles.fire` + a light), streamers.\n1112\t- **Trails:** a Hot Streak's fire on the ball and athlete, Afterburner's neon ghosts (`r.actor(..., { ghost })`), the\n1113\t  Golden Spiral's gold glints.\n1114\t- **Lights** (`game.lights.enabled = true` in every stadium):\n1115\t  - Tumbleweed: dusk, `ambient` 0.62 warm, a low sun light from the west, string lights over the bleachers.\n1116\t  - Hog Wallow: overcast, 0.55, lightning flashes from the weather.\n1117\t  - Afterglow: night, 0.35, 4 light towers (radius 420, intensity 0.9) and colored neon lights.\n1118\t  - Frostbite: 0.5, the northern lights tint the sky, towers.\n1119\t  - Mega Dome: indoor 0.55, 6 spotlights sweeping on tweens, pyro lights.\n1120\t  - Try GPU lighting (`game.enableGPU({ map })`, ground views) for soft shadows from the stands. The canvas fallback\n1121\t    must look right too: set both ambients.\n1122\t- **Shadows:** `r.shadow` under every athlete, and under the ball with a radius and alpha that shrink with its\n1123\t  height. The ball's shadow is how players read a lob.\n1124\t\n1125\t---\n1126\t\n1127\t## 12. Art and animation\n1128\t\n1129\t### 12.1 The look target (from API.md: use every step)\n1130\t`res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true` (480×270 at 1080p, 427×240 at 1440p and 4K). Dressed HD characters 40–60 px tall\n1131\t(sizes 1.2–1.45; bosses 1.6–1.8). A backdrop behind the stands. A textured field and stands. 3–8 props on screen.\n1132\tEffects on every hit. Colors shaded with `E.tones`. Titles with `E.font.title`. Lights in every scene. Portraits in\n1133\tevery dialog. Compare screenshots with Metal Slug and Symphony of the Night, not NES games. `node tools/check.mjs`\n1134\tgives look notes: act on every one.\n1135\t\n1136\t### 12.2 Characters (`27-rigs.js`)\n1137\t**Rig recipes by position** (`new E.Humanoid({...})`, `weapon: null`, `sleeves: 'short'`, `outfit: 'shirt'`,\n1138\t`hat: { style: 'football', color, stripe, logo }` (E4)):\n1139\t\n1140\t| Position | build | size | Notes |\n1141\t|---|---|---|---|\n1142\t| OL, DL | bulky | 1.38–1.48 | `shoulderHalf` +0.6, `torsoW` +0.6, a lean of 0.1 |\n1143\t| LB | bulky or heroic | 1.3 | |\n1144\t| RB | heroic | 1.22 | stocky: `limbW` +0.3 |\n1145\t| WR, CB, S | heroic | 1.2 | lean: `limbW` −0.2 |\n1146\t| QB | heroic | 1.25 | |\n1147\t| K | heroic | 1.02 | the tiny kicker (a joke the announcer makes) |\n1148\t| Bosses: Bertha 1.6, The Mountain 1.7, Avalanche 1.8 | bulky | | Avalanche has a beard (gear hook) and `eyeGlow` off |\n1149\t| Refs | heroic | 1.15 | a cap (`hat: 'cap'`, white); stripes from the gear hook |\n1150\t| Coaches | bulky or heroic | 1.2 | a cap and a headset (gear hook), a polo |\n1151\t| Cheerleaders | heroic | 1.1 | `hair: 'ponytail'`, pom-poms (gear hook) |\n1152\t| Mascots | bulky | 1.3 | a costume head (gear hook), team colors |\n1153\t| Announcers (portraits only) | heroic | 1.2 | a headset, a blazer (`outfit: 'coat'`) |\n1154\t| Story cast (Mama Pearl, Sterling, ...) | per character | 1.2 | §18.1 |\n1155\t\n1156\t**The uniform from team colors:** `colors.cloth` is the jersey, `trim` the jersey trim (sleeve bands), `pants`,\n1157\t`belt`, `boot` the cleats, `glove` the gloves, the hat color the helmet. Away teams wear white jerseys with\n1158\tteam-color trim, and home teams wear their color. Skin tones (`#f0c8a8 #e0b090 #c89070 #a87050 #6a4028 #4a2c18`) and\n1159\thair styles vary per player (data). Faces show: `face: { eyes: 'big' }` on story cast, default on athletes, and eye\n1160\tblack on athletes (gear hook).\n1161\t\n1162\t**The gear hook (E3)** draws, depth-sorted inside the rig:\n1163\t- **Shoulder pads:** a wide disc over each shoulder joint in the jersey color, with a darker rim. They sit over the\n1164\t  torso, so the silhouette reads as football at a glance.\n1165\t- **The jersey number:** 3×5 digits (`font: 'tiny'`, scale by `u`) in the trim color, on the chest when the torso\n1166\t  faces the camera (`dot(facing, view.fx/fy) > .3`), on the back when it faces away (< −.3), hidden edge-on. Placed at\n1167\t  `Sp(J.shC, ±torsoDepth, 0, -3)`.\n1168\t- **The carried ball:** at the carrying hand, a brown ellipse with white laces (below), at the hand's depth + 0.02.\n1169\t  The sim sets `rig.carry = 'R' | 'L' | null`.\n1170\t- **Eye black,** a headset on coaches, pom-poms on cheerleaders (discs at the hands, team colors), ref stripes (black\n1171\t  vertical lines over the torso).\n1172\t- **Mascot heads:** a big disc 2.2× the head with features. Fumbles is a twig ball (brown line scribbles) with googly\n1173\t  eyes. Hambone is a pink hog with a snout and ears. Hooty is an owl with neon eyes (glow). Abominable Al is a white\n1174\t  fur ball with horns. Goldie is a gold lion with a mane.\n1175\t- **Big Heads cheat:** `rig.o.headR × 1.9` live. Check it re-solves; if not, rebuild the rigs.\n1176\t- **The Fiascos' glow paint** in LIGHTS OUT: the trims drawn with `px.glow(g, 1)` and an emissive queue.\n1177\t\n1178\t### 12.3 Pose codes to animation (`FF.Rigs.drive(rig, athlete, dt)`)\n1179\tThe sim sets `pose` and `poseU` (Appendix C). The presentation maps each to rig inputs, from four sources: the\n1180\tengine's procedural rig (velocity gait, `pose`, `expr`, `air`, `down`, `stance`, `run`, `flip`, `land`), `E.MOVES`\n1181\tand attack specs, mocap clips (§12.4), and PoseFX (§12.5).\n1182\t\n1183\t| Pose | Drive |\n1184\t|---|---|\n1185\t| IDLE, RUN | `rig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing })`, the velocity gait; `sprint: true` with turbo |\n1186\t| STANCE3 (linemen) | mocap `Land_Three_Point` held at its last key (a hand on the ground), `mocapW` 1 |\n1187\t| STANCE2 (LB, WR, DB) | `pose: 'crouch'` blended 0.5, a lean of 0.25 |\n1188\t| QB_UC / QB_SG | `pose: 'crouch'` 0.35 with the hands forward (an attack spec held), or upright with the hands at the chest |\n1189\t| SNAP | a two-handed spec from low forward to between the legs, 0.25 s |\n1190\t| DROPBACK | the gait backward (verify the backpedal in a filmstrip; if it looks wrong, turn `facing` 25° off the motion) |\n1191\t| THROW | mocap `OverhandThrow`, `mocapMask: 'upper'` while moving (throwing on the run), time-scaled so the release lands on the sim's release frame |\n1192\t| HANDOFF give / take | specs: the arm extends / both arms form a pocket |\n1193\t| CARRY | the gait plus PoseFX `tuck` (the carrying hand to the chest side, the elbow in) |\n1194\t| CATCH_READY, CATCH | a two-handed spec reaching to the ball's height (`z1` from the arrival height); `air: true` with a jump |\n1195\t| DIVE, TACKLE_DIVE | PoseFX `tilt` forward 0→75° over 0.15 s, the arms forward (a two-handed `thrust`), `air: true`; on landing `down: 1` |\n1196\t| SPIN | the `facing` passed to `rig.update` turned through a full circle over the action (`facing + 2π·u`, eased); don't write `rig.spin` (the rig's own field for attack spins) |\n1197\t| STIFFARM | the `jab` move with the free hand, held 70% of the action |\n1198\t| HURDLE | mocap `Run_Jump_Over`, time-scaled to the jump |\n1199\t| BLOCK | mocap `Push_Loop` (`mocapMask: 'upper'`) + a lean of 0.2; feet from the gait |\n1200\t| SHED / SWIM | `E.MOVES.hook` (an arm over) |\n1201\t| BIGHIT | `E.MOVES.bash` + PoseFX `tilt` 25° |\n1202\t| TACKLED | mocap `Fall_On_Face` (tackled from behind) or `Hit_Knockback` (from the front), then DOWN |\n1203\t| FLY (a Big Hit victim) | PoseFX `tilt` back 70° + `roll` spin (Mega Hit), `air: true`, then DOWN |\n1204\t| DOWN | `down: 1` |\n1205\t| GETUP | mocap `Get_Up_Face_Down` or `Get_Up_From_Back`, by how he fell |\n1206\t| PILE | `down: 1` + PoseFX random `tilt`/`roll` and a z offset (stacked) |\n1207\t| KICK | `E.MOVES.kick` after a 3-step run-up |\n1208\t| HOLD | `pose: 'kneel'` + a hand low forward |\n1209\t| CELEBRATE(k) | §10.6 |\n1210\t| CHEER | mocap `Cheering_Two_Hands` (teammates), `pose: 'cheer'` (refs and coaches) |\n1211\t| DEJECTED | `pose: 'kneel'`, or PoseFX `handsOnHelmet` |\n1212\t| REF_TD, REF_FD, REF_INC, REF_SHRUG | specs: both arms up, an arm pointing, arms crossing back and forth, a shrug |\n1213\t| CHEERLEADER | mocap `Jumping Jacks` and `Cheering_Two_Hands`, alternating |\n1214\t| COACH_YELL | `expr: 'shout'`, `pose: 'hips'`, the headset |\n1215\tFaces act: `expr: 'shout'` on hits and celebrations, `'wince'` when hurt, `'smile'` when Blazing. Enemy specials\n1216\ttelegraph with a 0.25–0.35 s wind-up and a flash.\n1217\t\n1218\t### 12.4 Motion clips (the FIASCO set)\n1219\tCut once, from the engine repository (§3.2):\n1220\t```sh\n1221\tnode .cache/engine/tools/anim-set.mjs .cache/engine/src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js .cache/engine/src/mocap/sets/mesh2motion.js \\\n1222\t  .cache/engine/src/mocap/sets/cmu.js \\\n1223\t  --clips \"QUATERNIUS:OverhandThrow,QUATERNIUS:Push_Loop,QUATERNIUS:Dance_Loop,Dance Charleston,Dance Body Roll,Victory Fist Pump,Cheering_Two_Hands,Breakdance_Helicopter,Run_Jump_Over,Dive_Roll,Fall_On_Face,Get_Up_Face_Down,Get_Up_From_Back,QUATERNIUS:Hit_Knockback,MESH2MOTION:Land_Three_Point,Jumping Jacks,Front_Kick,MESH2MOTION:Backflip\" \\\n1224\t  --name FIASCO --credit \"Quaternius (CC0), Mesh2Motion (CC0), CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses)\" \\\n1225\t  --out vendor/anim/fiasco-anim.js\n1226\t```\n1227\tThis was run during planning. It writes 18 clips, 736 key poses, 222 KB. In the set the clips are named without the\n1228\t`SET:` prefix (`lib.clip('OverhandThrow')`). A headless probe confirmed that each drives an HD Humanoid (bulky, 1.3) on\n1229\tthe handoff engine: `Land_Three_Point` ends with the hand at z ≈ 1 (a three-point stance), and `OverhandThrow` raises\n1230\tthe hand to z ≈ 28 and comes through. Use:\n1231\t```js\n1232\tconst lib = Mocap.load(window.MOCAP.FIASCO);  Mocap.drive(rig, lib);           // once per rig\n1233\trig.mocap = lib.sample(lib.clip('OverhandThrow'), t); rig.mocapW = w; rig.mocapMask = 'upper';  // each step, before rig.update\n1234\t```\n1235\tFade clips in and out with `mocapW` over 0.1 s. Never snap.\n1236\t\n1237\t### 12.5 PoseFX (new animation by joint post-processing)\n1238\tWrap each athlete rig's `solvePose` (the API allows it): call the original, then apply the active transforms to\n1239\t`rig.J` (all joints are in the rig's own frame: forward, right, up):\n1240\t- `tilt(angle)` rotates every joint about the hip center around the right axis (forward dives, backward falls).\n1241\t- `roll(angle)` rotates about the forward axis.\n1242\t- `lift(dz)` raises everything.\n1243\t- `tuck(side)` moves the hand and elbow of that side to fixed points by the chest (ball security).\n1244\t- `handsOnHelmet` puts both hands at the head's sides.\n1245\tTransforms blend with weights so they ease in and out. This is how Emberdeep makes its dodge roll (AI_GUIDE, Building\n1246\ta big game).\n1247\t\n1248\t### 12.6 New shapes (`24-shapes.js`) and store props\n1249\tWrite these as `E.SHAPES` entries (docs/ASSETS.md format; 16 units = 1 m; face south). Look at each with\n1250\t`node .cache/engine/dist/handoff/tools/asset-sheet.mjs $PWD/src/24-shapes.js#<id>` and fix its lint notes:\n1251\t`pylon` (orange foam 2×2×7), `cone` (an orange traffic cone on a base), `dummy` (a red tackling dummy, r 4, h 22),\n1252\t`sled` (a blocking sled with two pads), `cooler` (an orange sports-drink cooler with a white lid and spigot), `cups`\n1253\t(a stacked paper cup tower), `teamBench` (a long bench, `params: { len }`, a team-color material slot), `lightTower` (a\n1254\tlattice tower h 200, a lamp bank in a glow material, `lights`), `oldScoreboard` (Tumbleweed's hand-flipped board;\n1255\tdigits drawn by code), `jumbotron` (a big dark screen on a pylon; the content drawn by code into its projected quad:\n1256\tthe score and \"MAKE SOME NOISE!\"), `downMarker` (an orange pole with a flip card; the down drawn by code),\n1257\t`chainPole`, `tvCamera` (a camera on a tripod), `podium`, `micStand`, `locker` (a metal locker 10×6×30 with vents and a\n1258\tteam-color door), `chalkboard` (on an easel; X's and O's by code), `trophy` (the Fiasco Cup: a gold lathe cup with\n1259\thandles and a football on top), `pickupTruck`, `waterTower` (\"TUMBLEWEED JCT\" by code), `airboat`, `neonSign` (glow\n1260\tletters by code), `snowbank` (snowy rock lumps), `pyroCannon`, `blimp` (a flattened ball with fins, drawn at z 300+),\n1261\t`stage` (a ceremony stage with bunting), `cake` (a tiered victory cake), `bulldozer` (yellow, blade, tracks),\n1262\t`goldCart` (a gold golf cart), `grill` (a tailgate grill; smoke from particles), `helmet` (a loose football helmet:\n1263\ta shell ball and facemask bars, mats per team), `inflatableHelmet` (the giant team-entrance tunnel helmet), `bassDrum`\n1264\t(held by band members through the gear hook).\n1265\t\n1266\tFrom the core library, no store needed: `bench`, `table`, `barrel`, `crate`, `lamppost`, `fence`, `pine`, `tree`,\n1267\t`bush`, `grass`, `rock`. `r.prop` built-ins: `banner`, `sign`, `lamp`, `streetlamp`, `awning`, `cactus`, `palm`,\n1268\t`lantern`, `trashcan`, `firedrum`, `vines`. Cut the few store entries into one set:\n1269\t```sh\n1270\tS=.cache/engine/dist/handoff/src/assets/sets\n1271\tnode .cache/engine/dist/handoff/tools/asset-set.mjs $S/kenney-survival.js $S/kenney-graveyard.js $S/kenney-pirate.js $S/kenney-nature.js $S/kenney-furniture.js \\\n1272\t  --ids kenney-survival:bucket,kenney-graveyard:hay-bale,kenney-pirate:flag,kenney-nature:tent-small-closed,kenney-furniture:speaker \\\n1273\t  --name fiasco-store --out vendor/assets/fiasco-store.js\n1274\t```\n1275\tThis was run during planning: 5 shapes, 10 materials, 1,799 tokens. Load it with\n1276\t`E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['fiasco-store'])`, which names the entries `fiasco-store:<id>`. The bucket is the sports-drink bucket dump. Use\n1277\t`asset-find.mjs` for anything else. The store sets are CC0: credit Kenney on the About screen.\n1278\t\n1279\t### 12.7 The football\n1280\tDrawn in `r.queue` at the ball's world position, or by the gear hook while carried. It is a prolate ellipse (a\n1281\t10-point polygon), 7×4 px at zoom 1, brown `#8a4a22` with an `E.tones` shade and highlight, a white lace stripe of 3\n1282\tstitches, and a white band at each tip. Its long axis follows the projected velocity\n1283\t(`r.w(p)` → `r.w(p + v·dt)`). In flight it spins: alternate the lace position each 1/30 s so it reads as a spiral.\n1284\tKicks tumble end over end (the axis rotates in screen space at 10 rad/s). Shadow below. A flame trail when Blazing, a\n1285\tgold trail on the Golden Spiral, white speed lines on Rocket Passes.\n1286\t\n1287\t### 12.8 Logos, field art and UI art\n1288\t- **Team logos** are string sprites (`E.sprite`), 16×16, with a 4–6 color palette per team: a flaming tumbleweed, a\n1289\t  hog head with tusks, neon owl eyes, a yeti footprint, a crown on a football, and a grey \"P\" on a whistle for the\n1290\t  Scrubs. Use them on the score bug (×1), menus (×3), the helmet ear dot (a 1–2 px dot of the main color), the\n1291\t  midfield paint (sampled as a bitmap at 1 sprite px = 0.5 yd) and the end-zone walls.\n1292\t- **The title logo:** `AMERICAN FOOTBALL` in `E.font.title` scale 3 (white, cream, orange, deep orange, extruded\n1293\t  purple), with `FIASCO` beneath in scale 5, colors fire yellow to orange to red, a crack line through it (`px.line`\n1294\t  in dark), and a cartoon football with motion lines bouncing off the \"O\". A short intro animation: the football hits\n1295\t  the word, which cracks and shakes.\n1296\t- **Play cards:** `E.ui.box` with a team-color gradient, a mini field strip (green with yard lines) where routes are\n1297\t  `px.line` with arrow heads in white and the team color, blocks as short T bars, and zones as dithered circles\n1298\t  (`px.polyDither`). The play name is font scale 2, plus a RUN/PASS/TRICK/KICK badge and the star badge for COACH\n1299\t  SAYS."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1931e3d-b97b-4f9d-8b9d-c7660f7740b5","uuid":"eb17eb04-cd32-4adf-be1a-bcba58138b78","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:02:42.693Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-070229__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","content":"    `#7aa8ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8`, △ green `#5ae0a0`.\n  - **Gamepad, Xbox** (Settings, Controller, Button symbols): colored discs with letters: A green, B red, X blue,\n    Y yellow.\n  - Shoulders and triggers are rounded rectangles labeled L1, R1, R2 or LB, RB, RT. Options/Menu are small pills.\n  - **Keyboard:** a keycap (a light rectangle with a darker bottom edge) with `input.label(action, 'keyboard')`. Wide\n    caps for SPACE and SHIFT.\n  - **Mouse:** a mouse icon with the left or right button lit, used where the mouse does that context's job (clicking\n    a receiver, a card, the kick meter). Elsewhere the keyboard chip.\n  - Labels come from `input.label(action, device)` after rebinding. Never hard-code a key. Map the engine's label to\n    a chip style: glyph labels (`✕ ○ □ △ A B X Y L1 ...`) are pad chips, everything else is keycaps.\n- The receiver chips over receivers' heads show b1–b4 on a pad, r1–r4 (1–4) on a keyboard (b1–b4 on a shared-keyboard\n  half), and a mouse cursor badge on the hovered receiver.\n- **The prompt strip** (bottom right, 1–5 chips with words, §14.6) shows the current context's actions only: \"✕ HIKE\",\n  then \"✕ ○ □ THROW · R2 ROCKET\", then \"□ SPIN ○ STIFF △ HURDLE ✕ DIVE R2 TURBO\". It changes the moment the context\n  changes. Each local player's strip sits in their own corner.\n\n### 9.4 Who controls whom\n- **Offense, one human:** the QB through the snap. After a handoff or pitch he controls the carrier. After a throw he\n  controls the **target receiver** (camera and control follow the ball). After a catch, the catcher.\n- **Offense, two humans (co-op):** player A has the QB, then the ball. Player B picks a receiver at the play call\n  (□ cycles the highlighted receiver on his panel; default WR1) and runs it himself; the route line shows as a guide.\n  If B's receiver catches the ball, B has the ball and A takes the nearest teammate (to block). Otherwise B keeps his\n  receiver (to block downfield).\n- **Defense:** each human controls one defender. At PRESNAP the first human gets the S (a safety sees the whole play\n  and can roam) and the second gets LB1, both of which can be moved before the snap. During the play, b2 or a click\n  switches to the free defender nearest the ball (on a pass in the air: the one nearest the landing point). With the **Auto-switch** setting (on by default), a\n  pass in the air or a carrier past the line switches each defensive human to the best unheld defender, with 0.5 s\n  hysteresis.\n- **Turnovers:** at an INT or a fumble recovery, the humans of the side that gained the ball take the carrier (the\n  first human) and the nearest teammates. The other side's humans take their nearest defenders.\n- **Kicks:** the kicking human runs the meter. The receiving human controls the returner.\n- **Hub sessions:** the same rules, keyed by seat (§16.4).\n- A ring under each held athlete in the player's color (local: `E.PLAYER_COLORS`; sessions: the seat color), and a\n  tag over him (\"P1\", or the hub name) during PRESNAP and for 1 s after each switch.\n\n### 9.5 The mouse on the field\n- Every athlete is drawn with `r.actor(..., { pick: athleteId })`. Each step, `r.pick(...input.pointer())` gives the\n  hovered athlete. Hovering a receiver while QB highlights his chip, and a left click (`input.clicked()`) throws to\n  him (Shift held: a Rocket Pass). Hovering a defender on defense outlines him, and a click switches to him.\n- Pre-snap on offense, a click on the QB or the ball hikes.\n- During the kick meter, a click anywhere presses it.\n- Clicking open ground does nothing in play. A click is never bound to b1, which avoids accidental hikes and dives.\n- The pointer hides after 2 s without mouse movement. `input.lastDevice` switches back to the keyboard on the next\n  key.\n\n---\n\n## 10. The over-the-top systems (`36-ots.js`)\n\n### 10.1 Hot Streak (\"BLAZING!\")\n- **Big plays** are: a TD, a catch of 20+ yd, a run of 15+ yd, a sack, an INT, a forced fumble, and a Big Hit that\n  tackles. Each athlete keeps a heat count. +1 per big play by him. It resets when the opponents make a big play\n  against him (a sack of that QB, a tackle for a loss on that carrier, a pick of that QB) or when they score.\n- At heat 3 he goes **BLAZING** for `T.hotPlays` (6 plays), or until a reset event happens:\n  - unlimited turbo, +12% speed;\n  - offense: tackle p −0.15 against him;\n  - defense: tackle p +0.15, Big Hit fumble ×2;\n  - a QB: aim error ×0.6 and fire-trail passes;\n  - visuals: fire particles at his feet and shoulders (`particles.fire` every 0.05 s, emissive), a flaming ball while\n    he has it, an orange light on him at night;\n  - audio: the music's `hot` layer comes in (`audio.layer('hot', 1, .5)`), the crowd chants, the announcer's\n    `hotStreakOn` lines.\n- A team can have 2 blazing athletes. The HUD shows a flame icon by his name and a heat pip count (1–3) on held\n  athletes' tags.\n\n### 10.2 The Fiasco Meter and the Fiasco Cards (the items)\n**The Fiasco Meter** is the title's mechanic: every disaster is an opportunity. Each team's meter (0–100) fills when\nthings go wrong for it:\n\n| Event | Fill |\n|---|---|\n| fumble lost | 35 |\n| INT thrown | 35 |\n| safety allowed | 40 |\n| sacked | 20 |\n| turnover on downs | 25 |\n| opponent's TD | 20 |\n| dropped pass | 10 |\n| Big Hit received | 10 |\n| late hit received | 5 |\n\nWhen it fills, the team **draws a Fiasco Card** at random from its pool (story: the cards you've unlocked;\nexhibition: all 8), up to 3 held. Then the meter resets. A HUD flourish plays: a card flips in, \"FIASCO BREAK!\".\nEach team also starts a game with cards: story, a loadout of up to 3 picked on the chapter screen; exhibition, 1\nrandom card each; hub rounds, 1 each, dealt from `AIHub.rng('cards')`.\n\n**Using a card:** on the play-call screen press b4 (△) to open the card row and pick one (or click a slot). One card\nper play. A card on offense needs an offensive card and so on; a card that doesn't fit the side is greyed out. Its\neffect lasts as its text says. Cards are data: `{ id, name, side: 'off' | 'def' | 'any' | 'kick', text, art,\napply(ctx) }`. `apply` registers bus modifiers for the play or for N plays. The CPU trigger is in §8.5.\n\n| Card | Side | Effect | CPU uses it when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ROCKET CLEATS | off | your carrier and receivers +20% speed this play | 3rd & 10+, or trailing in the last 2:00 |\n| GLUE GLOVES | off | your receivers can't drop the ball; contested catches +30% this play | a pass play on 3rd or 4th down |\n| BRICK WALL | off | your blockers can't be shed for 3.5 s | a pass play on 3rd & long |\n| HOT SAUCE | any | the held athlete (CPU: its best player) goes BLAZING for 3 plays | first chance in the 2nd half |\n| MEGA HIT | def | this play's first tackle is a Mega Hit: launch × 1.6, fumble chance 60%, the slow-mo cam | 3rd down defense, or the opponent in the red zone |\n| BUTTERFINGERS | def | opposing receivers' catch chance −35% this play | 3rd & long defense |\n| MASCOT MAYHEM | any | your mascot runs on as a 12th player for the play: he blocks the nearest defender on offense, rushes the passer on defense, with `noClip` through blockers. The announcers lose their minds. | trailing by 7+ |\n| SUPER FOOT | kick | the next kick: +15 yd, an accuracy band ×2.5, the power needle ×0.7 speed | a FG of 45+ yd, or a PAT in the last minute |\n\n### 10.3 Big Hits, Mega Hits, Dinged and the flying helmet\n- **Big Hit:** turbo + b1 (§7.4–7.5). Hit-stop 0.08 s, `game.shake(4)`, `game.hitFx` with an impact star, grass, mud\n  or snow bits, a camera punch-in to 1.25× for 0.35 s, slow motion (`timeScale` 0.4 for 0.3 s, honoring reduce\n  motion), a comic \"BOOM!\" / \"WHAM!\" / \"KER-RUNCH!\" burst over the hit, and the crowd \"OOOOH\".\n- **Mega Hit** (the card, or a 1-in-6 roll of a Blazing Big Hit): launch × 1.6, the victim spins in the air (PoseFX\n  spin), hit-stop 0.12, the slow-mo cam (`timeScale` 0.25 for 0.6 s), a white flash (`game.flash`, honoring the\n  flashes setting).\n- **Dinged:** the victim of a Mega Hit, or of 2 Big Hits in one drive, is Dinged for 2 plays: −10% speed and little\n  cartoon stars orbiting his helmet (3 tiny glints circling, emissive).\n- **The flying helmet:** on a Mega Hit (and 25% of Big Hits) the victim's helmet pops off as a separate bouncing\n  prop: a shape `helmet` in team colors, an `E.Body` with bounce 0.5. His hair shows until the next PRESNAP, when he\n  picks it up (cosmetic). The announcer says \"HIS HELMET IS IN A DIFFERENT AREA CODE!\"\n\n### 10.4 Late hits\nAfter the whistle, for 1.2 s, b1 near an opponent does a late hit (cosmetic knockdown). The refs throw their caps\n(Fiasco rules: no flags). A crowd reaction, the announcer's outrage lines, a stat. AI athletes late-hit too, by team\npersonality (the Mudhogs often, the Dynasty sneakily).\n\n### 10.5 Boss specials (story bosses; also in exhibition)\nEach special is used by the AI, or by a human holding that star (in exhibition or a hub round), who presses **lb and\nrb together** (L1 + R1, LB + RB, Q + E) within 0.1 s while its conditions hold. A single shoulder press waits those\n0.1 s before it becomes a lateral, so the chord never laterals by accident. For the Golden Spiral, press the chord,\nthen a receiver button within 1 s. A badge shows \"SPECIAL READY\" on the star's tag. NIGHT VISION is passive.\n\n| Star | Special | Rules |\n|---|---|---|\n| BIG BERTHA BOUDREAUX (Mudhogs RB #44) | **MUDSLIDE** | once per drive: for 1.2 s she lowers her shoulder; the first tackler in front is bowled over (down 1.2 s) unless it's a Big Hit from the front; mud spray FX |\n| LUMEN LOPEZ (Owls QB #1) | **NIGHT VISION** | always on: his reads see 1 extra yard of openness, and his aim error ×0.8 |\n| ZAP ZAPATA (Owls WR #11) | **AFTERBURNER** | once per play: a 1.0 s burst at ×1.35 speed that leaves neon light trails (`ghost` afterimages in cyan) |\n| AVALANCHE (Yetis DE #99) | **SNOWPLOW** | once per drive: his engagement drives the blocker straight back at 3× push for 1.5 s; a snow spray |\n| BROCK STERLING III (Dynasty QB #12) | **GOLDEN SPIRAL** | once per drive: a perfect pass, no aim error, it can't be intercepted (defenders may still deflect it with 0.15), gold sparkle trail |\n| THE MOUNTAIN, MALU FALEOLO (Dynasty LB #55) | **SEISMIC SACK** | once per half, when within 3 yd of the carrier: he leaps (0.6 s) and slams down; everyone within 2.5 yd is knocked down; a shockwave ring (`particles.ring`), shake 7, \"SEISMIC!\" |\nThe commentary has lines for each, and the HUD shows a banner with the star's portrait for 1.2 s when one fires.\n\n### 10.6 Celebrations\nAfter a TD the scorer celebrates. Locally the scorer's human picks one: b2 the Charleston, b3 the Body Roll, b4 the\nHelicopter (breakdance). Otherwise it's random by personality, from the list below. In hub sessions it's always\nrandom (no player-triggered emotes, §16.6).\n\n| Celebration | How it's animated |\n|---|---|\n| Spike | a throw-down attack spec, the ball bounces high |\n| Charleston, Body Roll | mocap `Dance Charleston` / `Dance Body Roll` |\n| Helicopter | mocap `Breakdance_Helicopter` |\n| Backflip | mocap `Backflip` |\n| Fist pump | mocap `Victory Fist Pump` |\n| Stands Dive | he leaps into the end zone stands: `body.jump`, the crowd lifts him, bodies bob |\n| Group Huddle Dance | 3 nearest teammates do `Dance_Loop` in a ring |\n\nTeammates near him do `Cheering_Two_Hands`. Defenders kneel (`pose: 'kneel'`) or put their hands on their helmets\n(PoseFX). Pyro cannons fire, confetti falls (`particles.bits` in team colors from z 160), the crowd goes wild.\n\n### 10.7 Mascots\nEach team has a mascot on the near sideline: a Humanoid in team colors with a big costume head drawn by the gear\nhook (§12.2). They react: dance on scores, slump on turnovers, and do a backflip on a Big Hit. They flip the coin, and\nrun on with Mascot Mayhem (`noClip`, a cartoon dust cloud, a squeaky sfx). Fumbles the Flaming Tumbleweed sets\nhimself on fire when the Fiascos score (fire particles), and trips over the power cable in chapter 3 (§15.1).\n\n### 10.8 Refs\nTwo refs on the field (an umpire 6 yd behind the defense, a back judge 20 yd deep) and a line judge on the near\nsideline. They're cosmetic Humanoids in striped shirts (the gear hook draws the stripes), with poses for signals:\nTOUCHDOWN (both arms up), FIRST DOWN (an arm pointing at the offense's goal), INCOMPLETE (arms crossing, a wave),\nFUMBLE / POSSESSION (pointing), and \"Sure, why not\" (a shrug) after a late hit. A play running through a ref knocks\nhim over comically (the announcer: \"AND THE REF GOES DOWN! HE'S FINE! HE'S... MOSTLY FINE!\").\n\n### 10.9 LIGHTS OUT (chapter 3's event; also a Dev toggle)\nFor 3 plays: `lights.ambient` 0.12, `darkness` 0.9, the light towers off. Every uniform's trim, the Owls' neon, the\nball, the yard lines and the Fiascos' glow paint (their trim turns `#7aff6a` with glow) stay bright as emissive (the\ngear hook draws trims with `px.glow` and queues them `emissive: true`). Camera flashes in the crowd sparkle.\nCommentary and the crowd play along, then the lights come back with a thunk and a flicker.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Presentation\n\n### 11.1 The camera director (`37-camera.js`)\nOur own smoothing (`E.smoothDamp` on x, y and zoom), `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update, `game.setZoom(z)`, and\n`cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)`. The player's own zoom (wheel, `-` `=`) multiplies ours.\n\n| Shot | When | Focus and zoom |\n|---|---|---|\n| PRESNAP | the formation | x = LOS + dir × 5 yd; y = the ball's y × 0.6 + MID_Y × 0.4; zoom 1.0 |\n| RUN | a carrier | the carrier + v × `T.camLead` (0.35 s); zoom 1.05 |\n| PASS_PRE | QB with the ball on a pass play | `cam.frame([qb, ...receivers within 30 yd], { margin: 40, zoom: [.62, 1] })` |\n| BALL_AIR | a pass in the air | between the ball's ground point and its landing point, zoomed to fit both (min .62) |\n| KICK | kickoffs and punts | follow the ball, zoom .75; on the catch, RUN on the returner |\n| KICKCAM | FG and PAT | cut (`{ fade: 0 }` style, 0.15 s dissolve) to the `kickcam` view behind the kicker; follow the ball to the posts; hold 1.2 s on the result; cut back |\n| PUNCH | a Big Hit or Mega Hit | zoom × 1.25 over 0.12 s, hold 0.25 s, ease back over 0.4 s |\n| CELEBRATE | after a TD | the scorer, zoom 1.35 |\n| CUTAWAY | after the celebration (50% of TDs) | pan up to the stands at the end zone for 1.2 s, zoom 1.2: \"the crowd goes wild\" |\n| REPLAY | §11.2 | `field`, `iso`, `reverse` views at 0.5× and 0.35× |\n| CUTSCENE | §15.1 | scripted tweens (`game.tween` on a camera target object) |\nViews are fixed per shot. Never animate the yaw continuously: each new yaw rebakes the floor.\n\n### 11.2 Instant replay and the highlight reel (`38-replay.js`)\n- **Recording:** from the snap to 1 s after the whistle, at 60 Hz (every 2nd step), record a frame per entity:\n  athletes `[x, y, z, facing, poseCode, poseU, flags]` (flags: carrying, hot, dinged, helmet off), the ball\n  `[x, y, z, state, spin]`, refs and the mascot. Record presentation events with their time: hit FX, particles\n  bursts, comic pops, sfx names, card banners. A 12 s ring buffer in `Float32Array`s.\n- **Playback** is a REPLAY sub-state of the match scene. The sim is frozen and untouched. Rigs are driven from the\n  frames (positions interpolated, velocity derived for the gait), the recorded events re-fire at their times, and\n  the audio plays the sfx (the commentary doesn't repeat; the crowd bed stays). Shots: 1) the `field` view following\n  the ball at 0.5× over the 3 s around the key moment; 2) the `iso` or `reverse` view at 0.35× for the impact. Then\n  back to the live view.\n- **The overlay:** a REPLAY badge with a blinking red dot, a VCR timecode, letterbox bars, \"✕ SKIP\" (b1 or a click\n  skips). The music ducks.\n- **Triggers:** a TD (when Replays is All big plays or Touchdowns only), a turnover, a Mega Hit, a Big Hit fumble, a\n  play of 40+ yd, a FG of 50+ yd, a Seismic Sack, a Mascot Mayhem play. Setting: Replays: All big plays (default) /\n  Touchdowns only / Off. In hub sessions: off.\n- **Highlight reel:** the 5 most exciting clips of the game, by an excitement score (event priority × yards), are\n  kept (8 s each). The results screen offers WATCH HIGHLIGHTS, which plays them back to back with captions\n  (\"Q2 3:12 — DOC DOBSON 48-YD TD RUN\").\n- **Invariant (tested):** the sim's state hash is the same before and after a replay.\n\n### 11.3 Commentary and on-field text (`39-commentary.js`)\n- **Two announcers:** CHET THUNDERBUNS (play-by-play: loud, excitable) and LOU \"THE TOE\" DIGGS (color: an ex-kicker,\n  folksy, deadpan).\n- **Events** have priorities (Appendix D): TD 10, turnover 9, Mega Hit 9, special 8, Big Hit 8, sack 8, long play 7,\n  Hot Streak 7, mascot 7, FG result 7, late hit 6, card 6, drop 5, first down 4, incomplete 3, weather 2, idle\n  chatter 1.\n- **Rules:** at most one line per 2.5 s. A higher priority interrupts a lower one. On events of priority ≥ 7, CHET\n  speaks and LOU answers 1.2 s later with chance 0.4. No line repeats within the last 8 for that event. Lines take\n  variables (`{player}`, `{team}`, `{opp}`, `{yards}`, `{score}`).\n- **The caption:** a non-modal box at the bottom left (keep clear of the prompt strip and the score bug). The\n  speaker's portrait (`rig.drawPortrait` of the announcer rig: a headset and a blazer) and name, the text typed at\n  45 chars/s with the babble voice (§13.4). It hides after its length / 15 + 1.5 s. The Commentary setting: On /\n  Subtitles only (no voice) / Off.\n- **On-field pops:** comic bursts in world space over the event: BOOM!, WHAM!, KER-RUNCH!, SACKED!, PICKED OFF!,\n  FUMBLE!, PANCAKE!, BROKEN TACKLE!, DOINK!, BLAZING!, FIASCO BREAK!, SEISMIC!, DROPPED!. `E.font.title` with an\n  outline over a jagged polygon burst (`px.poly`, 12 points with radii alternating 1 and 0.6). It scales from 0.4 to\n  1.1 to 1.0 (`outBack`) and lasts 0.9 s. Anchor it with `r.w(x, y, z + 40)`.\n- **Center text** for game events: TOUCHDOWN! (title scale 4 in the scoring team's colors with a shine sweep), FIRST\n  DOWN!, TURNOVER!, SAFETY!, IT'S GOOD! / NO GOOD!, TOUCHBACK, 4TH DOWN, RED ZONE, TWO-MINUTE WARNING (if quarters\n  are 2:00 or longer), LIGHTS OUT!\n\n### 11.4 The crowd (`26-crowd.js`)\n- **Fans** sit on the stand tiers: 2–3 per tile per tier (about 2,400 at the Mega Dome). Each is drawn like the SPORTS\n  kit's fans (`r.queue` with 2–3 `px.rect`s: shirt, head, an optional hat), with the team colors by section (home\n  sections, plus an away section by one end zone), and skin and hat variety from a seeded hash. 8% hold signs: a\n  6×4 rectangle with a tiny-font word (\"FIASCO!\", \"D!\", \"HOGS!\", \"HOOT!\", \"BRRR!\", \"$$$\"). 5% have foam fingers.\n- **Excitement E** (0–1): big events set it up, `T.crowdDecay` (0.15/s) brings it down. Above 0.6 the fans stand and\n  bob. On a TD the scoring side's fans jump (+5 z). Below 0.2 they sway.\n- **The Wave** rolls around the stands every 40–70 s when E is moderate and the home team leads: a sine wave of\n  stand-ups moving 10 tiles/s.\n- **Density** by stadium. Tumbleweed Field is 35% full until the story's ending, when it's 100%.\n- **Camera flashes** at night: random 1-px white dots with tiny lights, more on big plays.\n- **Audio:** the crowd bed and reactions (§13.3).\n\n### 11.5 FX (`40-fx.js`)\n- **Hits:** `game.hitFx(x, y, z, { power, angle })` with no damage number (leave `damage` out and check that none\n  shows), `particles.impact`, surface bits (grass `#4a8a3a`/`#2e5a22`, mud `#5a3a1a`, snow `#f0f4ff`), dust, and white\n  `particles.sparks` on helmet-to-helmet hits.\n- **Divots:** on Big Hits and dives, a persistent decal of torn turf at the spot, up to 40 per game (the oldest fade).\n- **Footprints** in snow and mud: small decals behind runners every 0.15 s, fading over 6 s, up to 200.\n- **Breath puffs** at Frostbite: `particles.smoke` at each head every 1.2 s, on the cosmetic RNG stream.\n- **Weather:** `game.weather = { kind: 'rain', amount: .6, wind: .2, lightning: .1 }` at the Hog Wallow;\n  `{ kind: 'snow', amount: .7, wind: .3 }` at Frostbite; set in the match's `enter` (weather ends with the scene).\n- **Celebrations:** confetti (`particles.bits` in team colors from z 160), fireworks in the sky\n  (`particles.explosion(..., { debris: false, shake: 0 })` and colored rings at z 300+ over the end zone), pyro columns\n  from the cannons (`particles.fire` + a light), streamers.\n- **Trails:** a Hot Streak's fire on the ball and athlete, Afterburner's neon ghosts (`r.actor(..., { ghost })`), the\n  Golden Spiral's gold glints.\n- **Lights** (`game.lights.enabled = true` in every stadium):\n  - Tumbleweed: dusk, `ambient` 0.62 warm, a low sun light from the west, string lights over the bleachers.\n  - Hog Wallow: overcast, 0.55, lightning flashes from the weather.\n  - Afterglow: night, 0.35, 4 light towers (radius 420, intensity 0.9) and colored neon lights.\n  - Frostbite: 0.5, the northern lights tint the sky, towers.\n  - Mega Dome: indoor 0.55, 6 spotlights sweeping on tweens, pyro lights.\n  - Try GPU lighting (`game.enableGPU({ map })`, ground views) for soft shadows from the stands. The canvas fallback\n    must look right too: set both ambients.\n- **Shadows:** `r.shadow` under every athlete, and under the ball with a radius and alpha that shrink with its\n  height. The ball's shadow is how players read a lob.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Art and animation\n\n### 12.1 The look target (from API.md: use every step)\n`res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true` (480×270 at 1080p, 427×240 at 1440p and 4K). Dressed HD characters 40–60 px tall\n(sizes 1.2–1.45; bosses 1.6–1.8). A backdrop behind the stands. A textured field and stands. 3–8 props on screen.\nEffects on every hit. Colors shaded with `E.tones`. Titles with `E.font.title`. Lights in every scene. Portraits in\nevery dialog. Compare screenshots with Metal Slug and Symphony of the Night, not NES games. `node tools/check.mjs`\ngives look notes: act on every one.\n\n### 12.2 Characters (`27-rigs.js`)\n**Rig recipes by position** (`new E.Humanoid({...})`, `weapon: null`, `sleeves: 'short'`, `outfit: 'shirt'`,\n`hat: { style: 'football', color, stripe, logo }` (E4)):\n\n| Position | build | size | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| OL, DL | bulky | 1.38–1.48 | `shoulderHalf` +0.6, `torsoW` +0.6, a lean of 0.1 |\n| LB | bulky or heroic | 1.3 | |\n| RB | heroic | 1.22 | stocky: `limbW` +0.3 |\n| WR, CB, S | heroic | 1.2 | lean: `limbW` −0.2 |\n| QB | heroic | 1.25 | |\n| K | heroic | 1.02 | the tiny kicker (a joke the announcer makes) |\n| Bosses: Bertha 1.6, The Mountain 1.7, Avalanche 1.8 | bulky | | Avalanche has a beard (gear hook) and `eyeGlow` off |\n| Refs | heroic | 1.15 | a cap (`hat: 'cap'`, white); stripes from the gear hook |\n| Coaches | bulky or heroic | 1.2 | a cap and a headset (gear hook), a polo |\n| Cheerleaders | heroic | 1.1 | `hair: 'ponytail'`, pom-poms (gear hook) |\n| Mascots | bulky | 1.3 | a costume head (gear hook), team colors |\n| Announcers (portraits only) | heroic | 1.2 | a headset, a blazer (`outfit: 'coat'`) |\n| Story cast (Mama Pearl, Sterling, ...) | per character | 1.2 | §18.1 |\n\n**The uniform from team colors:** `colors.cloth` is the jersey, `trim` the jersey trim (sleeve bands), `pants`,\n`belt`, `boot` the cleats, `glove` the gloves, the hat color the helmet. Away teams wear white jerseys with\nteam-color trim, and home teams wear their color. Skin tones (`#f0c8a8 #e0b090 #c89070 #a87050 #6a4028 #4a2c18`) and\nhair styles vary per player (data). Faces show: `face: { eyes: 'big' }` on story cast, default on athletes, and eye\nblack on athletes (gear hook).\n\n**The gear hook (E3)** draws, depth-sorted inside the rig:\n- **Shoulder pads:** a wide disc over each shoulder joint in the jersey color, with a darker rim. They sit over the\n  torso, so the silhouette reads as football at a glance.\n- **The jersey number:** 3×5 digits (`font: 'tiny'`, scale by `u`) in the trim color, on the chest when the torso\n  faces the camera (`dot(facing, view.fx/fy) > .3`), on the back when it faces away (< −.3), hidden edge-on. Placed at\n  `Sp(J.shC, ±torsoDepth, 0, -3)`.\n- **The carried ball:** at the carrying hand, a brown ellipse with white laces (below), at the hand's depth + 0.02.\n  The sim sets `rig.carry = 'R' | 'L' | null`.\n- **Eye black,** a headset on coaches, pom-poms on cheerleaders (discs at the hands, team colors), ref stripes (black\n  vertical lines over the torso).\n- **Mascot heads:** a big disc 2.2× the head with features. Fumbles is a twig ball (brown line scribbles) with googly\n  eyes. Hambone is a pink hog with a snout and ears. Hooty is an owl with neon eyes (glow). Abominable Al is a white\n  fur ball with horns. Goldie is a gold lion with a mane.\n- **Big Heads cheat:** `rig.o.headR × 1.9` live. Check it re-solves; if not, rebuild the rigs.\n- **The Fiascos' glow paint** in LIGHTS OUT: the trims drawn with `px.glow(g, 1)` and an emissive queue.\n\n### 12.3 Pose codes to animation (`FF.Rigs.drive(rig, athlete, dt)`)\nThe sim sets `pose` and `poseU` (Appendix C). The presentation maps each to rig inputs, from four sources: the\nengine's procedural rig (velocity gait, `pose`, `expr`, `air`, `down`, `stance`, `run`, `flip`, `land`), `E.MOVES`\nand attack specs, mocap clips (§12.4), and PoseFX (§12.5).\n\n| Pose | Drive |\n|---|---|\n| IDLE, RUN | `rig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing })`, the velocity gait; `sprint: true` with turbo |\n| STANCE3 (linemen) | mocap `Land_Three_Point` held at its last key (a hand on the ground), `mocapW` 1 |\n| STANCE2 (LB, WR, DB) | `pose: 'crouch'` blended 0.5, a lean of 0.25 |\n| QB_UC / QB_SG | `pose: 'crouch'` 0.35 with the hands forward (an attack spec held), or upright with the hands at the chest |\n| SNAP | a two-handed spec from low forward to between the legs, 0.25 s |\n| DROPBACK | the gait backward (verify the backpedal in a filmstrip; if it looks wrong, turn `facing` 25° off the motion) |\n| THROW | mocap `OverhandThrow`, `mocapMask: 'upper'` while moving (throwing on the run), time-scaled so the release lands on the sim's release frame |\n| HANDOFF give / take | specs: the arm extends / both arms form a pocket |\n| CARRY | the gait plus PoseFX `tuck` (the carrying hand to the chest side, the elbow in) |\n| CATCH_READY, CATCH | a two-handed spec reaching to the ball's height (`z1` from the arrival height); `air: true` with a jump |\n| DIVE, TACKLE_DIVE | PoseFX `tilt` forward 0→75° over 0.15 s, the arms forward (a two-handed `thrust`), `air: true`; on landing `down: 1` |\n| SPIN | the `facing` passed to `rig.update` turned through a full circle over the action (`facing + 2π·u`, eased); don't write `rig.spin` (the rig's own field for attack spins) |\n| STIFFARM | the `jab` move with the free hand, held 70% of the action |\n| HURDLE | mocap `Run_Jump_Over`, time-scaled to the jump |\n| BLOCK | mocap `Push_Loop` (`mocapMask: 'upper'`) + a lean of 0.2; feet from the gait |\n| SHED / SWIM | `E.MOVES.hook` (an arm over) |\n| BIGHIT | `E.MOVES.bash` + PoseFX `tilt` 25° |\n| TACKLED | mocap `Fall_On_Face` (tackled from behind) or `Hit_Knockback` (from the front), then DOWN |\n| FLY (a Big Hit victim) | PoseFX `tilt` back 70° + `roll` spin (Mega Hit), `air: true`, then DOWN |\n| DOWN | `down: 1` |\n| GETUP | mocap `Get_Up_Face_Down` or `Get_Up_From_Back`, by how he fell |\n| PILE | `down: 1` + PoseFX random `tilt`/`roll` and a z offset (stacked) |\n| KICK | `E.MOVES.kick` after a 3-step run-up |\n| HOLD | `pose: 'kneel'` + a hand low forward |\n| CELEBRATE(k) | §10.6 |\n| CHEER | mocap `Cheering_Two_Hands` (teammates), `pose: 'cheer'` (refs and coaches) |\n| DEJECTED | `pose: 'kneel'`, or PoseFX `handsOnHelmet` |\n| REF_TD, REF_FD, REF_INC, REF_SHRUG | specs: both arms up, an arm pointing, arms crossing back and forth, a shrug |\n| CHEERLEADER | mocap `Jumping Jacks` and `Cheering_Two_Hands`, alternating |\n| COACH_YELL | `expr: 'shout'`, `pose: 'hips'`, the headset |\nFaces act: `expr: 'shout'` on hits and celebrations, `'wince'` when hurt, `'smile'` when Blazing. Enemy specials\ntelegraph with a 0.25–0.35 s wind-up and a flash.\n\n### 12.4 Motion clips (the FIASCO set)\nCut once, from the engine repository (§3.2):\n```sh\nnode .cache/engine/tools/anim-set.mjs .cache/engine/src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js .cache/engine/src/mocap/sets/mesh2motion.js \\\n  .cache/engine/src/mocap/sets/cmu.js \\\n  --clips \"QUATERNIUS:OverhandThrow,QUATERNIUS:Push_Loop,QUATERNIUS:Dance_Loop,Dance Charleston,Dance Body Roll,Victory Fist Pump,Cheering_Two_Hands,Breakdance_Helicopter,Run_Jump_Over,Dive_Roll,Fall_On_Face,Get_Up_Face_Down,Get_Up_From_Back,QUATERNIUS:Hit_Knockback,MESH2MOTION:Land_Three_Point,Jumping Jacks,Front_Kick,MESH2MOTION:Backflip\" \\\n  --name FIASCO --credit \"Quaternius (CC0), Mesh2Motion (CC0), CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses)\" \\\n  --out vendor/anim/fiasco-anim.js\n```\nThis was run during planning. It writes 18 clips, 736 key poses, 222 KB. In the set the clips are named without the\n`SET:` prefix (`lib.clip('OverhandThrow')`). A headless probe confirmed that each drives an HD Humanoid (bulky, 1.3) on\nthe handoff engine: `Land_Three_Point` ends with the hand at z ≈ 1 (a three-point stance), and `OverhandThrow` raises\nthe hand to z ≈ 28 and comes through. Use:\n```js\nconst lib = Mocap.load(window.MOCAP.FIASCO);  Mocap.drive(rig, lib);           // once per rig\nrig.mocap = lib.sample(lib.clip('OverhandThrow'), t); rig.mocapW = w; rig.mocapMask = 'upper';  // each step, before rig.update\n```\nFade clips in and out with `mocapW` over 0.1 s. Never snap.\n\n### 12.5 PoseFX (new animation by joint post-processing)\nWrap each athlete rig's `solvePose` (the API allows it): call the original, then apply the active transforms to\n`rig.J` (all joints are in the rig's own frame: forward, right, up):\n- `tilt(angle)` rotates every joint about the hip center around the right axis (forward dives, backward falls).\n- `roll(angle)` rotates about the forward axis.\n- `lift(dz)` raises everything.\n- `tuck(side)` moves the hand and elbow of that side to fixed points by the chest (ball security).\n- `handsOnHelmet` puts both hands at the head's sides.\nTransforms blend with weights so they ease in and out. This is how Emberdeep makes its dodge roll (AI_GUIDE, Building\na big game).\n\n### 12.6 New shapes (`24-shapes.js`) and store props\nWrite these as `E.SHAPES` entries (docs/ASSETS.md format; 16 units = 1 m; face south). Look at each with\n`node .cache/engine/dist/handoff/tools/asset-sheet.mjs $PWD/src/24-shapes.js#<id>` and fix its lint notes:\n`pylon` (orange foam 2×2×7), `cone` (an orange traffic cone on a base), `dummy` (a red tackling dummy, r 4, h 22),\n`sled` (a blocking sled with two pads), `cooler` (an orange sports-drink cooler with a white lid and spigot), `cups`\n(a stacked paper cup tower), `teamBench` (a long bench, `params: { len }`, a team-color material slot), `lightTower` (a\nlattice tower h 200, a lamp bank in a glow material, `lights`), `oldScoreboard` (Tumbleweed's hand-flipped board;\ndigits drawn by code), `jumbotron` (a big dark screen on a pylon; the content drawn by code into its projected quad:\nthe score and \"MAKE SOME NOISE!\"), `downMarker` (an orange pole with a flip card; the down drawn by code),\n`chainPole`, `tvCamera` (a camera on a tripod), `podium`, `micStand`, `locker` (a metal locker 10×6×30 with vents and a\nteam-color door), `chalkboard` (on an easel; X's and O's by code), `trophy` (the Fiasco Cup: a gold lathe cup with\nhandles and a football on top), `pickupTruck`, `waterTower` (\"TUMBLEWEED JCT\" by code), `airboat`, `neonSign` (glow\nletters by code), `snowbank` (snowy rock lumps), `pyroCannon`, `blimp` (a flattened ball with fins, drawn at z 300+),\n`stage` (a ceremony stage with bunting), `cake` (a tiered victory cake), `bulldozer` (yellow, blade, tracks),\n`goldCart` (a gold golf cart), `grill` (a tailgate grill; smoke from particles), `helmet` (a loose football helmet:\na shell ball and facemask bars, mats per team), `inflatableHelmet` (the giant team-entrance tunnel helmet), `bassDrum`\n(held by band members through the gear hook).\n\nFrom the core library, no store needed: `bench`, `table`, `barrel`, `crate`, `lamppost`, `fence`, `pine`, `tree`,\n`bush`, `grass`, `rock`. `r.prop` built-ins: `banner`, `sign`, `lamp`, `streetlamp`, `awning`, `cactus`, `palm`,\n`lantern`, `trashcan`, `firedrum`, `vines`. Cut the few store entries into one set:\n```sh\nS=.cache/engine/dist/handoff/src/assets/sets\nnode .cache/engine/dist/handoff/tools/asset-set.mjs $S/kenney-survival.js $S/kenney-graveyard.js $S/kenney-pirate.js $S/kenney-nature.js $S/kenney-furniture.js \\\n  --ids kenney-survival:bucket,kenney-graveyard:hay-bale,kenney-pirate:flag,kenney-nature:tent-small-closed,kenney-furniture:speaker \\\n  --name fiasco-store --out vendor/assets/fiasco-store.js\n```\nThis was run during planning: 5 shapes, 10 materials, 1,799 tokens. Load it with\n`E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['fiasco-store'])`, which names the entries `fiasco-store:<id>`. The bucket is the sports-drink bucket dump. Use\n`asset-find.mjs` for anything else. The store sets are CC0: credit Kenney on the About screen.\n\n### 12.7 The football\nDrawn in `r.queue` at the ball's world position, or by the gear hook while carried. It is a prolate ellipse (a\n10-point polygon), 7×4 px at zoom 1, brown `#8a4a22` with an `E.tones` shade and highlight, a white lace stripe of 3\nstitches, and a white band at each tip. Its long axis follows the projected velocity\n(`r.w(p)` → `r.w(p + v·dt)`). In flight it spins: alternate the lace position each 1/30 s so it reads as a spiral.\nKicks tumble end over end (the axis rotates in screen space at 10 rad/s). Shadow below. A flame trail when Blazing, a\ngold trail on the Golden Spiral, white speed lines on Rocket Passes.\n\n### 12.8 Logos, field art and UI art\n- **Team logos** are string sprites (`E.sprite`), 16×16, with a 4–6 color palette per team: a flaming tumbleweed, a\n  hog head with tusks, neon owl eyes, a yeti footprint, a crown on a football, and a grey \"P\" on a whistle for the\n  Scrubs. Use them on the score bug (×1), menus (×3), the helmet ear dot (a 1–2 px dot of the main color), the\n  midfield paint (sampled as a bitmap at 1 sprite px = 0.5 yd) and the end-zone walls.\n- **The title logo:** `AMERICAN FOOTBALL` in `E.font.title` scale 3 (white, cream, orange, deep orange, extruded\n  purple), with `FIASCO` beneath in scale 5, colors fire yellow to orange to red, a crack line through it (`px.line`\n  in dark), and a cartoon football with motion lines bouncing off the \"O\". A short intro animation: the football hits\n  the word, which cracks and shakes.\n- **Play cards:** `E.ui.box` with a team-color gradient, a mini field strip (green with yard lines) where routes are\n  `px.line` with arrow heads in white and the team color, blocks as short T bars, and zones as dithered circles\n  (`px.polyDither`). The play name is font scale 2, plus a RUN/PASS/TRICK/KICK badge and the star badge for COACH\n  SAYS.","numLines":450,"startLine":850,"totalLines":2515}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011gHpUwRUqP6ScL6Uc999yE","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi1eTks2zcST1pLpA2Dj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014rjzS2LUBM2b1t21BWpHFm","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-070229__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","offset":1300,"limit":450},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17249,"cache_read_input_tokens":54399,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17249},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1931e3d-b97b-4f9d-8b9d-c7660f7740b5","uuid":"feae5663-06fa-4a8b-b048-6f05d569833a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:02:44.998Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi1eTYTfMsKKKLjt3j9g","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014rjzS2LUBM2b1t21BWpHFm":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-070229__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","offset":1300,"limit":450}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014rjzS2LUBM2b1t21BWpHFm","type":"tool_result","content":"1300\t- **Fiasco Cards:** a card frame in purple and gold with the card's art drawn by code (cleats with flames, glue\n1301\t  gloves, a brick wall, a hot sauce bottle, a big fist, butter, the mascot's head, a boot) and the name in big text.\n1302\t- Comic bursts, glyph chips (§9.3), the score bug, tabs and buttons all draw with engine primitives only (rule 2 of\n1303\t  API.md: no `ctx.arc`, `fillText` or canvas gradients on the game buffer).\n1304\t\n1305\t### 12.9 Checking the art\n1306\t`node .cache/engine/dist/handoff/tools/asset-sheet.mjs $PWD/src/24-shapes.js#<id>` for every shape. `node tools/filmstrip.mjs <page>#<deep link> --steps \"...\" --seed 1` for\n1307\tevery pose in §12.3 (the drill and dev deep links set them up). A swing must wind up, strike and settle back without\n1308\tjerks. Take screenshots only for the look (§21.6).\n1309\t\n1310\t---\n1311\t\n1312\t## 13. Audio (`20-audio-voice.js`, `21-audio-music.js`)\n1313\tAll sound is synthesized: the engine's chip synth (`audio.sfx`, `audio.music`, `audio.define`) plus our Web Audio\n1314\tnodes on engine buses (E6). There are no audio files. Every composition is original: write new melodies, and\n1315\tdon't transcribe known stadium tunes or fight songs.\n1316\t\n1317\t### 13.1 Music (`{ bpm, steps, tracks }`, named tracks for layers)\n1318\t| Song | For | Character |\n1319\t|---|---|---|\n1320\t| FIASCO ANTHEM | the title, the story hub | stadium rock, 132 bpm: a square lead, saw bass, a `k . s .` beat, a cheer-like hook |\n1321\t| LOCKER ROOM FUNK | menus, setup | 110 bpm, a slap-bass `pulse12` line, offbeat hats |\n1322\t| HUDDLE UP | the play-call screen | a drumline loop (drums only plus a low triangle ostinato), 120 bpm, 2 bars, rising tension |\n1323\t| DUST DEVIL STOMP | Tumbleweed Field | a western shuffle, triangle bass, a twangy pulse lead, 128 bpm |\n1324\t| SWAMP STOMP | The Hog Wallow | a blues shuffle, saw bass, a harmonica-like pulse with vibrato, 96 bpm |\n1325\t| NEON NIGHTDRIVE | Afterglow Arena | synthwave, saw arpeggios, a square lead, 118 bpm |\n1326\t| FROSTBITE FANFARE | Frostbite Field | bells (triangle and sine arpeggios), 100 bpm |\n1327\t| MEGA DOME ANTHEM | the Mega Dome | epic brass (3 squares in fifths), 140 bpm |\n1328\t| HALFTIME SHOW | halftime | a marching band: `k s s` drums, a brass melody |\n1329\t| ARCADE ATTRACT | the attract mode | a 150 bpm remix of the anthem |\n1330\t| FIASCO FOREVER | the credits | a slow ballad version of the anthem |\n1331\t| Stings | `audio.sting` | TD fanfare, FG good, first-down organ riff, defense chant riff, Fiasco Break \"wah-wah\", victory jingle, defeat jingle (a sad sliding saw) |\n1332\t\n1333\tIn matches the stadium song plays low under the crowd bed, with layers `hot` (more drums and a guitar-like saw, for a\n1334\tBlazing athlete), `redzone` (bass pulses inside the 20) and `final` (`audio.tempo` +8% in the last minute). Between\n1335\tplays, original stadium-organ riffs (square and triangle chords with vibrato) play as stings on first downs, and a\n1336\tdefense-chant riff plays on 3rd down.\n1337\t\n1338\t### 13.2 Sound effects (`audio.define` each; the engine warns on unknown names, and a test fails on such a warning)\n1339\thike (the QB's \"HUT\" voice + a snap thump), whistle, helmetClang (square 1800→1200 + a triangle ring), padsCrunch\n1340\t(lowpass noise 900→200 + sine 120→50), bigHit (crunch + clang + a boom sine 80→30), tackleThud, dive (whoosh),\n1341\tspinWhoosh, stiffArm (punch), hurdle (jump2), catchSlap (bandpass noise 1200, short), drop (thunk), throwWhoosh,\n1342\trocketPass (a saw sweep), kickThump (sine 150→60 + noise), doink (a triangle 1400 ring), airHorn (3 saw voices\n1343\t220/277/330, 0.6 s), cannon (boom), fireworks (explode + crackle bursts), confettiPop, coinFlip (blip arpeggio),\n1344\tcardFlip, fiascoBreak (a descending arpeggio + wah), hotOn (a fire whoosh + a rising arpeggio), pileThumps, refCapPop,\n1345\tmascotSqueak, lightsOut (a power-down sweep), lightsOn (a thunk + hum), menuMove, menuSelect, menuBack, meterTick,\n1346\tmeterSet. Crowd one-shots: roar, boo, oooh, groan, gasp, cheer swell (bandpassed noise shapes on the crowd bus).\n1347\t\n1348\t### 13.3 The crowd bed\n1349\tA long-lived looping noise source on the `crowd` bus, through a bandpass (500–1500 Hz), with gain\n1350\t`0.12 + 0.38 × E` (E from §11.4), plus a lower \"murmur\" layer. Reactions are the one-shots above. Chants are 6\n1351\tdetuned babble voices in rhythm with the team's chant syllables (\"FI-AS-CO!\", \"HOGS! HOGS! HOGS!\", \"HOOT-HOOT!\",\n1352\t\"BRRR-BRRR!\", \"DY-NAS-TY!\"), on the beat (`audio.onBeat`) when E > 0.4. Stadium size scales it: Tumbleweed is thin\n1353\tand quiet (until the ending), the Mega Dome thick.\n1354\t\n1355\t### 13.4 Voices: the babble synth (Animalese and Simlish)\n1356\t**The API:** `FF.voice.say(text, profile, { onSyllable })` plans syllables, schedules them on the `voices` bus and\n1357\treturns the plan. `FF.voice.letter(ch, profile)` is the E5 dialog hook, which groups typed letters into syllables\n1358\tlive. `FF.voice.stop(profile)`.\n1359\t\n1360\t**Two styles, one synth:**\n1361\t- **Animalese** (the story cast, refs, mascot noises): letter-driven. Each vowel group in the typed text is one\n1362\t  syllable, with its onset taken from the preceding consonant(s). Quick, high and cute.\n1363\t- **Simlish** (the announcers, coaches on the radio, the crowd chants): count the line's syllables, then speak that\n1364\t  many syllables drawn from a gibberish bank (`ba do zee ka fla nu ree bo shma gli va tor mip zub lo neh`), picked by\n1365\t  a hash of each real word so the same line sounds the same every time. More fluent, with less pitch jitter.\n1366\t\n1367\t**A syllable** = onset + nucleus:\n1368\t- **Onset** by consonant class: plosives (p b t d k g c q) are an 8 ms broadband noise click; fricatives (f s z v h\n1369\t  x, sh, th) are 25–40 ms of bandpassed or highpassed noise (s at 5 kHz, f at 3 kHz, h at 1.2 kHz); nasals (m n) are\n1370\t  30 ms of lowpassed voiced hum; liquids (l r w y) are a formant glide into the vowel.\n1371\t- **Nucleus:** an oscillator (the profile's wave) through two parallel bandpass filters at the vowel's formants\n1372\t  (a 800/1200, e 500/1900, i 320/2300, o 500/900, u 350/800 Hz, × the profile's formant scale; Q 6 and 10), with an\n1373\t  envelope of 6 ms attack, a sustain, and a 25 ms release. The duration is (1 / rate) × 0.6–1.0.\n1374\t- **Pitch** = base × 2^((jitter + contour) / 12). The jitter is ±2 semitones from a hash of the syllable, so it's\n1375\t  deterministic. The contour: questions rise +4 on the last 2 syllables, exclamations +3 and +30% volume, statements\n1376\t  fall −2 at the end, ALL-CAPS words +2 and louder.\n1377\t- The voice is monophonic per speaker: a new syllable cuts the previous one. A lowpass at 3.5 kHz on the bus softens\n1378\t  it.\n1379\t- Build the nodes on `game.audio.ctx` (null until the first key press or click, and always null headless: then plan\n1380\t  only). For `pulse` and `pulse12` waves, reuse the engine's PeriodicWave (`audio._pulse(.25)`, `audio._pulse(.125)`).\n1381\t  For noise, reuse `audio._noise`. Connect to the `voices` bus (E6).\n1382\t\n1383\t**Speaker profiles** (`{ base Hz, rate syl/s, wave, formant, breath, style }`):\n1384\t\n1385\t| Speaker | Profile |\n1386\t|---|---|\n1387\t| CHET THUNDERBUNS | 125, 13, saw, 0.95, 0.05, simlish, excited contours |\n1388\t| LOU DIGGS | 105, 9, triangle, 0.9, 0.08, simlish, falling contours |\n1389\t| COACH GUS BLUSTER | 140, 14, square, 1.0, 0.15, animalese, shouty |\n1390\t| MAMA PEARL | 210, 10, triangle, 1.15, 0.05, animalese, warm |\n1391\t| HAP HAZARD | 165, 12, saw, 1.05, 0.03, animalese |\n1392\t| BROCK STERLING III | 150, 11, pulse, 1.0, 0.02, animalese, smug (rise then fall) |\n1393\t| MONTGOMERY STERLING II | 115, 8, saw, 0.92, 0.06, animalese, pompous |\n1394\t| BIG BERTHA | 190, 9, square, 1.1, 0.1, animalese |\n1395\t| GATOR GUIDRY | 120, 10, saw, 0.95, 0.12, animalese |\n1396\t| LUMEN LOPEZ | 140, 11, pulse, 1.0, 0.02, animalese |\n1397\t| ZAP ZAPATA | 190, 16, pulse12, 1.1, 0.0, animalese |\n1398\t| DJ HOOT | 130, 12, pulse, 1.0, 0.04, animalese, vibrato |\n1399\t| COACH ICEBERG | 100, 6, sine, 0.85, 0.05, animalese, slow and solemn |\n1400\t| AVALANCHE | growls only: lowpass noise + a 70 Hz sine, no syllables |\n1401\t| THE MOUNTAIN | 90, 7, square, 0.8, 0.1, animalese |\n1402\t| refs | 150, 12, square, 1.0, 0.0, animalese, short |\n1403\t| QBs | the QB's own profile, cadence \"SET... HUT!\" (2–4 syllables + a strong plosive \"HUT\" on the snap) |\n1404\t| crowd chant | 6 voices, ±15 cents detuned, simlish |\n1405\tVoices are on whenever text is spoken: subtitles always show. Settings: Voices volume (the `voices` bus), Commentary\n1406\t(On / Subtitles only / Off). Headless, the synth schedules nothing and returns the plan, so tests can check that\n1407\tsyllables were planned.\n1408\t\n1409\t---\n1410\t\n1411\t## 14. Screens and UX (10-foot, controller-first)\n1412\t\n1413\t### 14.1 Rules for every screen\n1414\t- **Text size** on the 480×270 / 427×240 canvas: everything the player must read is `E.font` scale 2 (14 px glyphs,\n1415\t  56–84 px on a TV). Secondary text is scale 1 with a shadow. Titles are scale 3–5. `font: 'tiny'` only for jersey\n1416\t  numbers, signs and dev overlays.\n1417\t- **Few, big things:** at most 6–7 focusable items on a screen, one primary action highlighted, big cards, and\n1418\t  generous spacing. Focus shows as a pulsing border plus the `▸` marker. Results have at most 4 stat tiles, team\n1419\t  select 4 bars, records 6 rows a page. No tables of numbers outside the Dev mode.\n1420\t- **Navigation:** d-pad and stick with repeat (`input.repeat`); confirm and back; mouse hover moves the focus and a\n1421\t  click selects; the wheel scrolls lists. Every screen has a way back (○, Esc or the on-screen BACK).\n1422\t- **The prompt bar:** every screen's bottom edge shows its actions as chips for the current device (§9.3).\n1423\t- **Safe margins:** HUD and menus stay 8 px in from the edges (`T.safeMargin`, TV overscan) and clear of\n1424\t  `game.cornerArea()`.\n1425\t- **Contrast:** text over the field sits on dark translucent panels (`E.ui.box`, alpha 0.85) or has an outline.\n1426\t- **The HTML screens** (pause, settings, controls, help, dev) use the engine Shell with the 10-foot scale (E9: body\n1427\t  text ≥ 22 px at 1080p, buttons ≥ 56 px tall). Theme: `{ ink: '#f4f0e6', paper: 'rgba(14,16,34,.92)', accent:\n1428\t  '#ffb02e', muted: '#a9a6c0', danger: '#ff6a5a', line: 'rgba(255,176,46,.35)', font: 'system-ui, sans-serif',\n1429\t  titleFont: '\"Arial Black\", Impact, system-ui, sans-serif' }`: a sports-broadcast look.\n1430\t- **Every screen works with keyboard alone, pad alone, and mouse plus keyboard.** Tests drive them all three ways\n1431\t  (§21.3).\n1432\t\n1433\t### 14.2 Title\n1434\t- Tumbleweed Field at dusk in the broadcast view. Two lines of players face each other at midfield in stance, Fumbles\n1435\t  dances, the crowd sways. Every 6–8 s a scripted gag plays (Hap throws, a lineman catches it with his face; the\n1436\t  kicker whiffs; Fumbles catches fire).\n1437\t- The logo animates in (§12.8). \"PRESS START\" pulses with chips. Before any input, show both chips (Enter and ✕);\n1438\t  after that, the device's. The version label (`E.versionLabel()` + the game's version) is small at the bottom left.\n1439\t- 10 s idle starts the attract mode (§15.4). Touch-only devices: §14.12.\n1440\t\n1441\t### 14.3 Main menu\n1442\tSTORY: THE FIASCO CUP (Continue, New Game), EXHIBITION, PRACTICE, HALL OF FIASCO, OPTIONS (opens\n1443\t`game.menu('settings')`), ABOUT & CREDITS. A big vertical list on the left. On the right, a live vignette for the\n1444\tfocused item: the bracket board, two helmets clashing, a tackling dummy getting flattened, the trophy case, spinning\n1445\tgears, the announcers' booth. 10 s idle starts the attract mode.\n1446\t\n1447\t### 14.4 Exhibition setup (two steps)\n1448\t1. **CONTROLLERS & TEAMS.** Three columns: HOME (left), CPU (middle), AWAY (right). `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`:\n1449\t   Start on a free pad, or 2 on the keyboard (the right half), joins. Each player's device icon (a pad with P#, the\n1450\t   keyboard, or a keyboard half) starts in the middle. Left and right move it to a side. Up and down change that\n1451\t   side's team (the first human on a side chooses). The team panel shows the helmet (a portrait of a rig in that\n1452\t   helmet, turning slowly), the logo, the name, 4 rating bars, and the star player's portrait and special. ✕ readies.\n1453\t   A side with no human is the CPU, and P1 changes its team with △. When everyone is ready, go to step 2.\n1454\t2. **GAME OPTIONS.** Stadium (the home team's by default; arrows cycle with a preview), weather (Stadium / Clear /\n1455\t   Rain / Snow), quarter length, difficulty, then the big **PLAY BALL!** button.\n1456\tA coin toss starts the match. After the results: REMATCH, CHANGE TEAMS, MAIN MENU.\n1457\t\n1458\t### 14.5 The play-call screen (`53-playcall.js`)\n1459\t- **Layout:** a full-width panel when one side is human. With humans on both sides, split halves (home left, away\n1460\t  right), each player's choice hidden from the other: after picking, a panel turns its cards face down (\"READY\").\n1461\t  Co-op on one side: one panel with two cursors.\n1462\t- **The top bar:** the team logo, OFFENSE or DEFENSE, the down and distance, the ball spot, the play clock (big\n1463\t  digits, red under 4 s) and the team's Fiasco Meter.\n1464\t- **Tabs:** offense RUN | PASS | TRICK | SPECIAL (punt, field goal, fake punt, kneel). Defense BASE | PRESSURE |\n1465\t  SPECIAL (prevent, goal line, FG block). Kickoffs, PATs and returns show their own choices. L1/R1 or Q/E change the\n1466\t  tab; so does moving past the grid's edge, or a click on a tab.\n1467\t- **The grid:** 3×2 cards (§12.8). The focused card scales to 1.08. ✕ picks. □ flips (a FLIP badge, the routes\n1468\t  mirrored). The COACH SAYS card has a ★.\n1469\t- **The card row:** △ moves the focus to the 3 held Fiasco Cards, ✕ arms one (only one; greyed when it doesn't fit),\n1470\t  and ○ goes back to the grid.\n1471\t- **Co-op:** player 2's cursor picks his receiver (□ cycles).\n1472\t- **The timer:** when it runs out, the COACH SAYS play is picked.\n1473\t- **Behind:** the field, dimmed. The music is HUDDLE UP.\n1474\t\n1475\t### 14.6 The HUD (`41-hud.js`)\n1476\t- **The score bug** (bottom center, broadcast style, about 230×20): logo, abbreviation and score for each team, a\n1477\t  possession arrow, the quarter, the clock, the down and distance, and the ball spot. A thin Fiasco Meter bar under\n1478\t  each team, and flame icons for Blazing athletes. The scores flip on change.\n1479\t- **Player panels** for each local human, in their own corner (P1 top left, P2 top right, P3 bottom left above the\n1480\t  caption, P4 bottom right above the strip): the name tag, a big turbo bar (60×6), the held card count and SPECIAL\n1481\t  READY.\n1482\t- **The prompt strip** (§9.3), the caption (§11.3), center event text, the play clock in PRESNAP (from 5 s), and the\n1483\t  REPLAY overlay.\n1484\t- **In the world:** rings under held athletes; receiver chips with their glyphs; the pass target marker; the line of\n1485\t  scrimmage and first-down lines; the kick meter; tags; the down marker on the near sideline shows the down.\n1486\t- The HUD hides during replays (except the REPLAY overlay) and cutscenes.\n1487\t\n1488\t### 14.7 The kick meter\n1489\t- An arc meter (64×36 px) above the kicker in screen space. The power fill goes green, yellow, red. The accuracy\n1490\t  needle crosses a center band.\n1491\t- An aim line on the field: a dashed line from the ball along the aim, with the predicted landing marker for punts\n1492\t  and kickoffs. FGs show it in the Kick Cam with the posts in view.\n1493\t- Prompts step through ✕ KICK, ✕ SET POWER, ✕ SET AIM.\n1494\t\n1495\t### 14.8 Results\n1496\t- FINAL, both logos and scores huge, a winner banner.\n1497\t- PLAYER OF THE GAME: a portrait, his name and 2 key stats.\n1498\t- 4 stat tiles: total yards, turnovers, biggest hit (launch in yards), longest play.\n1499\t- Buttons: WATCH HIGHLIGHTS, then CONTINUE (story), or REMATCH / MAIN MENU (exhibition). In a hub session, the\n1500\t  results stay up under the hub's own results overlay.\n1501\t\n1502\t### 14.9 Hall of Fiasco (records)\n1503\t- `E.store('ff:records')`, 6 per page: longest TD, longest FG, biggest hit, most points in a game, most sacks in a\n1504\t  game, the fastest Fiasco Cup, and each chapter's stars.\n1505\t- Each row shows the team logo, the player, the value and the date.\n1506\t- Updated after exhibition and story games. Never written in hub sessions or the attract mode.\n1507\t\n1508\t### 14.10 About & Credits\n1509\tPages turned with ←/→ (L1/R1, clicks on the arrows), big text, scrolled with up/down when long:\n1510\t1. SOURCE INFORMATION: every field of TASK.md's list.\n1511\t2. TESTING FEEDBACK: the 4 answers.\n1512\t3. ENGINE FEEDBACK: the 6 answers.\n1513\t4. PROMPT FEEDBACK: the 4 answers.\n1514\t5. CREDITS: the engine my-3D2dge (MIT, michaelcrosato), the AI Hub client, motion clips (Quaternius CC0, Mesh2Motion\n1515\t   CC0, the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database), store props (Kenney CC0), and who built it and from what\n1516\t   (`PLAN.md`).\n1517\t6. ENGINE CHANGES: the summary from ENGINE-CHANGES.md.\n1518\t\n1519\tAll of it comes from `window.FF_ABOUT` (§23). Never type these texts in two places.\n1520\t\n1521\t### 14.11 Settings\n1522\tThe game's own (group `Game`, through `menu.settings`): Difficulty (Rookie / Pro / Fiasco; Pro), Quarter length\n1523\t(1:00 / 1:30 / 2:00 / 3:00 / 5:00; 2:00), Camera (Broadcast / Sky Cam / Blimp), Replays (All big plays / Touchdowns\n1524\tonly / Off), Commentary (On / Subtitles only / Off), Voices (slider, 0.8), Crowd (slider, 0.7), Auto-switch on\n1525\tdefense (On), Show routes (On: your receivers' route lines while you're the QB), Pass target marker (On), Late hits\n1526\t(On), Big Heads (Off; locked until the story is finished, or unlocked in the Dev mode).\n1527\tPlus the engine's: Display (full screen, pixel scaling, the screen filter: Clean / Scanlines / CRT), Sound, Comfort\n1528\t(reduce motion, screen shake, flashes), and **Controller: Button symbols (PlayStation / Xbox; PlayStation)** (E2).\n1529\tEverything applies live. The Controls screen (engine) rebinds every action per player.\n1530\t\n1531\t### 14.12 Touch-only devices\n1532\t- **Detection:** `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)')` matches, `(any-pointer: fine)` doesn't, `navigator.maxTouchPoints`\n1533\t  > 0, no gamepad is connected, and no key has been pressed.\n1534\t- **Then:** turn the touch controls off (`game.input.touchEnabled = false`, the Touch setting hidden, no scene\n1535\t  declares `touch` buttons). The title replaces PRESS START with **CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED** and a big\n1536\t  **SETTINGS** button (a tap calls `game.menu('settings')`). The corner's menu button stays. The attract mode runs\n1537\t  after 10 s as usual, and a tap goes back to the title.\n1538\t- **Unlock** the game the moment a gamepad connects (`gamepadconnected`, or `input.lastDevice === 'gamepad'`) or a key\n1539\t  is pressed.\n1540\t- In a hub session on such a device, the card says the same, and that seat is played by the AI until a controller or\n1541\t  keyboard input arrives. Don't call `AIHub.fail`, because from a host it would void the round.\n1542\t\n1543\t### 14.13 The pause menu\n1544\tResume (engine), then the game's items: Dev Mode, Restart (exhibition and practice). Then the engine's Settings,\n1545\tControls, Full screen, Help, and \"Quit to menu\" (made by `menu.quit: 'menu'`; don't add a quit item of your own).\n1546\tIn story mode, Quit to menu returns to the story board. Help comes from our Shell's `screens.help`: the\n1547\tcontrols by context in short sections (Offense, Ball carrier, Defense, Kicking, Menus), each line a key and what it\n1548\tdoes, for the device last used. Cutscenes aren't pausable: hold ○ or Esc for 1 s (a fill ring) to skip one.\n1549\t\n1550\t---\n1551\t\n1552\t## 15. Modes\n1553\t\n1554\t### 15.1 Story: THE FIASCO CUP (`54-scene-story.js`, `14-data-story.js`)\n1555\t**The hub** is the bracket board, a corkboard in the Fiascos' locker room. It holds 5 chapter cards, each locked,\n1556\topen or done with 0–3 stars. Below them sit the Fiasco Card collection and the **loadout**: up to 3 cards picked for\n1557\tthe next chapter. CONTINUE plays the next chapter. A chapter runs: intro cutscene → gameplay (a scenario) → halftime\n1558\tcutscene (chapters 2 and 5) → outro cutscene on a win, or the loss screen → reward → autosave\n1559\t(`game.saves.autosave()`, with the place, the chapter and the time played). Main menu CONTINUE loads\n1560\t`game.saves.latest()`. Wire the slots once at boot:\n1561\t`game.saves.write = () => ({ data: { story: story.state(), done, stars, collection, loadout, unlocked, playTime },\n1562\tplace: 'THE FIASCO CUP', level: nextChapter })` and `game.saves.read = s => restoreStory(s.data)`, with\n1563\t`menu: { saves: { version: 1 } }` so a future version can `migrate`. Records and settings live in `E.store`, not in\n1564\tthe slots.\n1565\t\n1566\t| # | Chapter | Stadium | Opponent | Scenario | Win condition | Shows | Reward |\n1567\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1568\t| 0 | PROLOGUE | Tumbleweed, night | — | a cutscene | — | the stakes | — |\n1569\t| 1 | TRYOUTS | Tumbleweed, dusk | the Scrubs | drills D1–D9 (§15.2) | finish D1–D8; D9 scored | every control | HOT SAUCE, SUPER FOOT |\n1570\t| 2 | THE MUD BOWL | Hog Wallow (rain, mud) | Mudhogs | a full game, 4 × 1:30 | win | run defense, tackling, fumbles, weather, halftime choice | MEGA HIT |\n1571\t| 3 | LIGHTS OUT | Afterglow (night) | Nite Owls | from the start of Q3, 1:30 quarters, trailing 7–21, Owls kick off | win | passing, pass defense, Hot Streak, the LIGHTS OUT event at the 4th play of Q3 | ROCKET CLEATS, GLUE GLOVES |\n1572\t| 4 | FROSTBITE | Frostbite (snow, ice) | Yetis | Q4 with 1:30 left, trailing 17–20, your ball 1st & 20 at your 20 | win (a FG ties: sudden death OT) | the two-minute drill, sidelines, the clock, laterals, ice, Snowplow | MASCOT MAYHEM |\n1573\t| 5 | THE FIASCO BOWL | Mega Dome | Dynasty | a full game, 4 × 2:00 | win | everything, both bosses, the halftime show | the trophy, the ending, the credits, Big Heads |\n1574\t\n1575\t- The collection starts with BRICK WALL and BUTTERFINGERS.\n1576\t- **Difficulty** per chapter: ch2 Rookie+, ch3 Pro−, ch4 Pro, ch5 Pro+ (a blend of the §8.4 columns), shifted one\n1577\t  step by the Difficulty setting.\n1578\t- **Stars:** 1 for the win, +1 for the bonus (ch2: force 2 fumbles; ch3: win by 7+; ch4: score with 20+ s left; ch5:\n1579\t  hold the Dynasty to 14 or fewer), +1 for no turnovers.\n1580\t- **Loss:** a \"THAT'S A FIASCO!\" screen with the coach's loss line and RETRY, RETRY EASIER (one step lower for this\n1581\t  chapter) or STORY MENU.\n1582\t\n1583\t**Cutscenes.** A cutscene is `{ set, stadium, cast: [{ who, at: [x, y], facing, pose }], camera: { view, focus,\n1584\tzoom }, node }`. Its node is an `E.Story` node list. `event` steps stage it: move an actor (a tween), set a pose or\n1585\tmocap clip, play an sfx, FX, a camera tween, a wait. The dialog is an `E.Dialog` with a live portrait of the speaker's\n1586\trig and `voice: ch => FF.voice.letter(ch, profile)` (E5). Hold ○ or Esc to skip to the end, keeping flags and\n1587\trewards. Sets are small TileMaps in the `field` view, built in code:\n1588\t- **LOCKER ROOM:** a row of lockers, benches, the chalkboard, the cooler and a team banner, a flickering lamp\n1589\t  (light). It is recolored for each team.\n1590\t- **OFFICE:** Mama Pearl's Pickle Barn: pickle barrels, a desk, a framed photo of the 1974 team, a lamp.\n1591\t- **PRESS ROOM:** the podium with mics, a backdrop wall painted with logos, chairs, TV cameras, camera flashes\n1592\t  (`game.flash` at 0.1 strength, honoring the flashes setting).\n1593\t- **FIELD:** the match stadium itself at midfield, with the trophy stage for the ending.\n1594\tThe lines are in §18.6.\n1595\t\n1596\t### 15.2 Tutorial and practice drills (`55-scene-drills.js`, `15-data-drills.js`)\n1597\t| Drill | Setup | Goal | Teaches |\n1598\t|---|---|---|---|\n1599\t| D1 RUN THE CONES | Hap at the 20, 5 cones zigzagging, a finish gate 15 yd past the last cone | pass within 1.5 yd of each cone in order and cross the gate within 9 s | move, turbo |\n1600\t| D2 HIKE & HANDOFF | offense, 3 dummies, HB DIVE called | hike, the RB takes the ball, gain 10 yd | hike, handoff, running |\n1601\t| D3 MOVES | the RB in a lane against 3 scrubs (a standing tackler, a frontal tackler, a diver), the goal line at 25 yd | beat each with the right move, then dive into the end zone | spin, stiff arm, hurdle, dive |\n1602\t| D4 PASSING | QB, 3 receivers on SLANTS, 2 slow defenders | complete 2 passes, then a Rocket Pass to the covered man | receiver buttons, Rocket Pass |\n1603\t| D5 CATCHING | you are Wheels (WR); the coach lobs high balls | catch 2 of 3 with a jump | jump catch, the target marker |\n1604\t| D6 DEFENSE | defense against a scrub runner, then a scrub QB | switch to the nearest defender and tackle; Big Hit to force a fumble and recover it; intercept a pass with a jump | switch, tackle, Big Hit, fumble recovery, INT |\n1605\t| D7 KICKING | a 37-yd FG, then a kickoff | make the FG; kick off 50+ yd | the meter, the Kick Cam |\n1606\t| D8 PLAY CALL | the play-call screen, HOT SAUCE given | pick a PASS play, flip it, use HOT SAUCE, run it Blazing | play calling, cards, Hot Streak |\n1607\t| D9 SCRIMMAGE | one drive against the Scrubs from your 30 | score a TD in 4 downs | all of it together |\n1608\t- Each drill opens with a big instruction card (2 lines with chips) and Coach Bluster's line, and shows a progress\n1609\t  counter. Success gives a star burst and praise. A failure restarts at once (b2 also restarts). After 2 failures,\n1610\t  \"□ SKIP DRILL\" appears.\n1611\t- Every drill has a `bot(ctx)` that completes it through intents. The tests and the attract mode use it.\n1612\t- **PRACTICE** (main menu) offers a grid of the 9 drills, any of them replayable, with best times kept.\n1613\t\n1614\t### 15.3 Exhibition\n1615\tSetup (§14.4), the coin toss, the match, the results, then REMATCH, CHANGE TEAMS or MAIN MENU. 1–4 local humans in\n1616\tany split, or CPU vs CPU (no humans: a \"spectate\" exhibition; P1 can press Start to take over a side).\n1617\t\n1618\t### 15.4 The attract mode (`56-scene-attract.js`, `17-data-attract.js`)\n1619\t- **Trigger:** 10 s with no input on the title, the main menu or the exhibition setup, with no Shell screen open.\n1620\t  Input means: any action pressed by any player, a click, the pointer moving more than 6 px, the wheel, a touch, or a\n1621\t  gamepad axis past the dead zone.\n1622\t- **Exit:** any input goes back to the screen it came from with a quick fade, and that input is consumed.\n1623\t- **Content:** a looping director, about 100 s a cycle. Each segment is a scenario with forced play calls and the\n1624\t  cheats it needs (TASK.md allows the attract AI to cheat freely):\n1625\t\n1626\t| # | Segment | Where | Shows | Cheats |\n1627\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1628\t| 1 | Logo sting, 3 s | — | the title logo cracking | — |\n1629\t| 2 | Kickoff return, 8 s | Tumbleweed, dusk; Fiascos vs Mudhogs | the meter, a return with a spin and a stiff arm | returner god mode |\n1630\t| 3 | Play call, 3 s | same | the play-call screen, both cursors picking DEEP POST and COVER 2, HOT SAUCE armed | — |\n1631\t| 4 | Blazing TD, 10 s | same | a deep pass, a jump catch, flames, the TD, a dance, the replay (field and iso) | forceCatch, noTackle |\n1632\t| 5 | Mud fumble, 8 s | Hog Wallow, rain | Bertha's MUDSLIDE, a Big Hit fumble, a pile-up | forceFumble |\n1633\t| 6 | Kick Cam FG, 6 s | Hog Wallow | a 52-yd FG, DOINK off the upright and in | forced accuracy |\n1634\t| 7 | Pick six, 7 s | Afterglow, night | Zap's AFTERBURNER, an INT returned for a TD, a late hit, a ref flattened | forceINT |\n1635\t| 8 | Mascot Mayhem, 6 s | Afterglow | Hooty runs on and sacks the QB | — |\n1636\t| 9 | Lights out, 5 s | Afterglow | glowing uniforms in the dark | — |\n1637\t| 10 | Snowplow, 6 s | Frostbite, snow | Avalanche's SNOWPLOW sack, divots, breath puffs | — |\n1638\t| 11 | Story snippet, 6 s | the press room | Brock's \"pickles\" line with his babble voice | — |\n1639\t| 12 | Halftime show, 5 s | Mega Dome | cheerleaders, the band, pyro, the blimp | — |\n1640\t| 13 | Seismic Sack, 5 s | Mega Dome | The Mountain's slam and shockwave | — |\n1641\t| 14 | Champions, 5 s | Mega Dome | the trophy stage, confetti, the bucket dump | — |\n1642\t| 15 | Hall of Fiasco, 5 s | — | the records | — |\n1643\t| 16 | Title, 5 s | — | the title, then the loop again | — |\n1644\t\n1645\t- **The overlay:** DEMO PLAY at the top, PRESS ANY BUTTON pulsing at the bottom, the ARCADE ATTRACT music,\n1646\t  commentary on with voices at 60%. Nothing is saved and no records are written.\n1647\t- Both teams are played by the normal brains. The director only forces play calls, the listed outcomes and the story\n1648\t  snippet's camera. The attract mode runs the same on touch-only devices.\n1649\t\n1650\t---\n1651\t\n1652\t## 16. Multiplayer (`80-net.js`)\n1653\t\n1654\t### 16.1 How TASK.md's multiplayer line is met\n1655\t- **The game on its own:** full local multiplayer for 1–4 on one machine (pads and a shared keyboard), in exhibition.\n1656\t  Story and practice are single player.\n1657\t- **In AI Hub:** sessions of the AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in **shared-world** mode for 1–4 players, each on their\n1658\t  own device. One player plays against the CPU; 2–4 play head to head or 2-on-1 and 2-on-2 with CPU teammates. The\n1659\t  hub's relay only exists inside a session, so outside one the game is unaffected (`AIHub.active` is false) and plays\n1660\t  as above.\n1661\t\n1662\t### 16.2 The page head\n1663\t`session.js` goes right after `<meta charset>`, unmodified and first:\n1664\t```html\n1665\t<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n1666\t<script>/*! aihub-session 1.4.0 … (the whole file, unmodified) */</script>\n1667\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n1668\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n1669\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n1670\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n1671\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"6\">\n1672\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Outscore the other side over one half of arcade football\">\n1673\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Stick/WASD run · ✕/Space hike, dive, tackle · ✕○□△ or 1-4 throw · R2/Shift turbo\">\n1674\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\"><meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\"><meta name=\"hub:source\" content=\"American football\">\n1675\t```\n1676\t\n1677\t### 16.3 A session, step by step (when `AIHub.active`)\n1678\t1. Boot skips the title, attract mode and menus. The boot steps build everything.\n1679\t2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. If it returns `null`, play\n1680\t   as on its own.\n1681\t3. **The setup** comes from `AIHub.rng('setup')`, the same on every copy: a stadium (one of the 5), two different\n1682\t   teams (never the Scrubs), the stadium's weather. Seats map to sides: 0 home, 1 away, 2 home, 3 away. With one\n1683\t   player, seat 0 is home against a CPU away team.\n1684\t4. **The card scene** (`57-scene-hubcard.js`):\n1685\t   - It shows the two helmets facing off over the stadium backdrop, the team logos, the players by side in their seat\n1686\t     colors with ready ticks (`card.ready`), \"HOW TO WIN: OUTSCORE THEM OVER ONE HALF\", this device's controls as\n1687\t     chips, and the countdown (`card.left`; PAUSED while `card.paused`).\n1688\t   - **READY** (Enter, ✕ or a tap) calls `AIHub.answer('ready')`. **MORE TIME** (△, T or a tap) calls\n1689\t     `AIHub.answer('more')`; pressing again takes it back.\n1690\t   - It has no menus or choices, and nothing on it changes the round. Any randomness comes from `AIHub.rng('card')`.\n1691\t   - Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the match is built, without a key press.\n1692\t5. On `start`, show a 3-2-1 countdown over the opening kickoff setup while `AIHub.time() < 0`. Kick off at ≥ 0.\n1693\t6. **The match** is one half: 2 quarters of 1:30. A play takes about 17 s of real time for about 16 s of game clock\n1694\t   (live play plus the runoff), plus about 8 s per TD celebration, so plan on 1.3 real seconds per clock second. When\n1695\t   `info.limitMs` is short, use `quarter = clamp((limitMs / 1000 − 60) / 2 / 1.3, 45, 90)` seconds (a 300 s limit\n1696\t   gives 90; a 200 s limit gives 54). No overtime: a tie is a tie.\n1697\t7. At the end of Q2, or on `time-up` (within `graceMs`), the host calls\n1698\t   `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, stats, awards })` and sends a last reliable snapshot with `over: 1`. Every copy\n1699\t   shows the final score (§14.8) until `end`.\n1700\t8. On `end`: take no more input, send nothing, `audio.music(null)` and mute. `reason` picks the message: finished,\n1701\t   ROUND CANCELLED (void, host-left) or YOU SAT THIS ONE OUT (sat-out).\n1702\t\n1703\t### 16.4 Roles\n1704\t- **Host** (`info.isHost`): runs the sim, the AI and the rules for every seat. Its own seat, if any, plays locally\n1705\t  with no prediction needed.\n1706\t- **Others:** predict their own held athlete, render snapshots, and run their local UI (the play call and the kick\n1707\t  meter).\n1708\t- **Server** (`info.server`): a host with no player of its own. It runs the same host code with every seat remote,\n1709\t  draws nothing (`draw` fills the screen once and returns), plays no audio, and owns no camera or input.\n1710\t- **Control** (§9.4) is keyed by seat. The host owns the assignment and broadcasts changes as events.\n1711\t\n1712\t### 16.5 Messages, rates, prediction (formats in Appendix B)\n1713\t- Client → host: `i`, its state and inputs, unreliable at 15 Hz. `call`, its play call, reliable. `kick`, its kick\n1714\t  meter result, reliable.\n1715\t- Host → all: `s`, the snapshot, unreliable at **14 Hz**, **at most 1,100 bytes** (under the hub's ~1,200\n1716\t  fragmentation size). `e`, events, reliable, at most 4/s: phase changes with the plays, control changes, scores,\n1717\t  stats, card draws, `over`. One `send()` to everyone counts as one message.\n1718\t- **The budget:** each copy sends at most 18 messages/s. The host sends 14 + ≤ 4. A client sends 15 + about 1\n1719\t  reliable (`call` or `kick`). The hub allows 20/s with bursts of 40, and the client drops the rest. `FF.net.budget` is\n1720\t  a token bucket at 18/s that refuses sends past it: it drops the oldest queued unreliable message, never a reliable\n1721\t  one, and delays the next snapshot rather than skipping an event. Tests check this.\n1722\t- **Client prediction** (the arena example's rule: \"your controls never wait for the network\"):\n1723\t  - The client moves its held athlete locally with the same movement code, plus a soft push from the snapshot's\n1724\t    nearby athletes. It sends its position, velocity, facing, intent bits and an **action log**: the last 8 actions\n1725\t    with sequence numbers. The host runs each new sequence number once, so losing a packet loses nothing.\n1726\t  - The host accepts the reported position unless the athlete is `locked` (tackled, engaged, in an action the host\n1727\t    started, a dead ball) or it is farther than top speed × elapsed + 8 units from the host's last position.\n1728\t    Otherwise it keeps its own and flags `corr`.\n1729\t  - The client snaps to the host when the difference is over 2 yd, or `corr` or `locked` is set. While locked it\n1730\t    stops predicting.\n1731\t- **Interpolation:** others and the ball render 100 ms behind the newest snapshot, interpolated, extrapolated up to\n1732\t  150 ms when late.\n1733\t- **The play call:** each client opens its play-call UI on the host's `e: phase PLAYCALL` and sends `call`. The host\n1734\t  waits for every human side or its own timer, then sends `e: phase PRESNAP` with both plays.\n1735\t- **The kick meter** runs on the kicking human's copy. Its `{ power, acc, aim }` goes as `kick` and the host executes\n1736\t  it.\n1737\t- **Leaving:** on `leave { id }`, that seat's athletes go back to the AI (a CPU tag over them) and the match goes on.\n1738\t  When the host leaves, the hub voids the round and the clients show ROUND CANCELLED on `end`.\n1739\t\n1740\t### 16.6 Session rules (the protocol's, and what they mean here)\n1741\t- No title, menus, attract mode or story. No quit or restart (`menu.quit: false`, no Restart item, no Dev Mode item).\n1742\t- **No world freeze:** `AIHub.active` is known from the page's first line, so in a session the game is constructed\n1743\t  with `new E.Game({ autoPause: false, ... })` and the match scene is `pausable: false`. The match scene reads the\n1744\t  `pause` action itself: Options (or Esc) opens a small local canvas overlay with SETTINGS\n1745\t  (`game.menu('settings', { pause: false })`, E8) and CONTROLS. While it is open, that player's athlete is driven by\n1746\t  the AI.\n1747\t- **Create + Options is the hub's:** each step, while Pad8 (Create) is held on any pad, call\n1748\t  `input.suppress('pause')` and `input.suppress('start')`.\n1749\t- **No player-to-player communication:** celebrations are random, there are no taunts, and names come only from the"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e1931e3d-b97b-4f9d-8b9d-c7660f7740b5","uuid":"12b57e85-4ee2-4859-a603-decbb7435f96","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:02:45.168Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-070229__xb697dc/work/PLAN.md","content":"- **Fiasco Cards:** a card frame in purple and gold with the card's art drawn by code (cleats with flames, glue\n  gloves, a brick wall, a hot sauce bottle, a big fist, butter, the mascot's head, a boot) and the name in big text.\n- Comic bursts, glyph chips (§9.3), the score bug, tabs and buttons all draw with engine primitives only (rule 2 of\n  API.md: no `ctx.arc`, `fillText` or canvas gradients on the game buffer).\n\n### 12.9 Checking the art\n`node .cache/engine/dist/handoff/tools/asset-sheet.mjs $PWD/src/24-shapes.js#<id>` for every shape. `node tools/filmstrip.mjs <page>#<deep link> --steps \"...\" --seed 1` for\nevery pose in §12.3 (the drill and dev deep links set them up). A swing must wind up, strike and settle back without\njerks. Take screenshots only for the look (§21.6).\n\n---\n\n## 13. Audio (`20-audio-voice.js`, `21-audio-music.js`)\nAll sound is synthesized: the engine's chip synth (`audio.sfx`, `audio.music`, `audio.define`) plus our Web Audio\nnodes on engine buses (E6). There are no audio files. Every composition is original: write new melodies, and\ndon't transcribe known stadium tunes or fight songs.\n\n### 13.1 Music (`{ bpm, steps, tracks }`, named tracks for layers)\n| Song | For | Character |\n|---|---|---|\n| FIASCO ANTHEM | the title, the story hub | stadium rock, 132 bpm: a square lead, saw bass, a `k . s .` beat, a cheer-like hook |\n| LOCKER ROOM FUNK | menus, setup | 110 bpm, a slap-bass `pulse12` line, offbeat hats |\n| HUDDLE UP | the play-call screen | a drumline loop (drums only plus a low triangle ostinato), 120 bpm, 2 bars, rising tension |\n| DUST DEVIL STOMP | Tumbleweed Field | a western shuffle, triangle bass, a twangy pulse lead, 128 bpm |\n| SWAMP STOMP | The Hog Wallow | a blues shuffle, saw bass, a harmonica-like pulse with vibrato, 96 bpm |\n| NEON NIGHTDRIVE | Afterglow Arena | synthwave, saw arpeggios, a square lead, 118 bpm |\n| FROSTBITE FANFARE | Frostbite Field | bells (triangle and sine arpeggios), 100 bpm |\n| MEGA DOME ANTHEM | the Mega Dome | epic brass (3 squares in fifths), 140 bpm |\n| HALFTIME SHOW | halftime | a marching band: `k s s` drums, a brass melody |\n| ARCADE ATTRACT | the attract mode | a 150 bpm remix of the anthem |\n| FIASCO FOREVER | the credits | a slow ballad version of the anthem |\n| Stings | `audio.sting` | TD fanfare, FG good, first-down organ riff, defense chant riff, Fiasco Break \"wah-wah\", victory jingle, defeat jingle (a sad sliding saw) |\n\nIn matches the stadium song plays low under the crowd bed, with layers `hot` (more drums and a guitar-like saw, for a\nBlazing athlete), `redzone` (bass pulses inside the 20) and `final` (`audio.tempo` +8% in the last minute). Between\nplays, original stadium-organ riffs (square and triangle chords with vibrato) play as stings on first downs, and a\ndefense-chant riff plays on 3rd down.\n\n### 13.2 Sound effects (`audio.define` each; the engine warns on unknown names, and a test fails on such a warning)\nhike (the QB's \"HUT\" voice + a snap thump), whistle, helmetClang (square 1800→1200 + a triangle ring), padsCrunch\n(lowpass noise 900→200 + sine 120→50), bigHit (crunch + clang + a boom sine 80→30), tackleThud, dive (whoosh),\nspinWhoosh, stiffArm (punch), hurdle (jump2), catchSlap (bandpass noise 1200, short), drop (thunk), throwWhoosh,\nrocketPass (a saw sweep), kickThump (sine 150→60 + noise), doink (a triangle 1400 ring), airHorn (3 saw voices\n220/277/330, 0.6 s), cannon (boom), fireworks (explode + crackle bursts), confettiPop, coinFlip (blip arpeggio),\ncardFlip, fiascoBreak (a descending arpeggio + wah), hotOn (a fire whoosh + a rising arpeggio), pileThumps, refCapPop,\nmascotSqueak, lightsOut (a power-down sweep), lightsOn (a thunk + hum), menuMove, menuSelect, menuBack, meterTick,\nmeterSet. Crowd one-shots: roar, boo, oooh, groan, gasp, cheer swell (bandpassed noise shapes on the crowd bus).\n\n### 13.3 The crowd bed\nA long-lived looping noise source on the `crowd` bus, through a bandpass (500–1500 Hz), with gain\n`0.12 + 0.38 × E` (E from §11.4), plus a lower \"murmur\" layer. Reactions are the one-shots above. Chants are 6\ndetuned babble voices in rhythm with the team's chant syllables (\"FI-AS-CO!\", \"HOGS! HOGS! HOGS!\", \"HOOT-HOOT!\",\n\"BRRR-BRRR!\", \"DY-NAS-TY!\"), on the beat (`audio.onBeat`) when E > 0.4. Stadium size scales it: Tumbleweed is thin\nand quiet (until the ending), the Mega Dome thick.\n\n### 13.4 Voices: the babble synth (Animalese and Simlish)\n**The API:** `FF.voice.say(text, profile, { onSyllable })` plans syllables, schedules them on the `voices` bus and\nreturns the plan. `FF.voice.letter(ch, profile)` is the E5 dialog hook, which groups typed letters into syllables\nlive. `FF.voice.stop(profile)`.\n\n**Two styles, one synth:**\n- **Animalese** (the story cast, refs, mascot noises): letter-driven. Each vowel group in the typed text is one\n  syllable, with its onset taken from the preceding consonant(s). Quick, high and cute.\n- **Simlish** (the announcers, coaches on the radio, the crowd chants): count the line's syllables, then speak that\n  many syllables drawn from a gibberish bank (`ba do zee ka fla nu ree bo shma gli va tor mip zub lo neh`), picked by\n  a hash of each real word so the same line sounds the same every time. More fluent, with less pitch jitter.\n\n**A syllable** = onset + nucleus:\n- **Onset** by consonant class: plosives (p b t d k g c q) are an 8 ms broadband noise click; fricatives (f s z v h\n  x, sh, th) are 25–40 ms of bandpassed or highpassed noise (s at 5 kHz, f at 3 kHz, h at 1.2 kHz); nasals (m n) are\n  30 ms of lowpassed voiced hum; liquids (l r w y) are a formant glide into the vowel.\n- **Nucleus:** an oscillator (the profile's wave) through two parallel bandpass filters at the vowel's formants\n  (a 800/1200, e 500/1900, i 320/2300, o 500/900, u 350/800 Hz, × the profile's formant scale; Q 6 and 10), with an\n  envelope of 6 ms attack, a sustain, and a 25 ms release. The duration is (1 / rate) × 0.6–1.0.\n- **Pitch** = base × 2^((jitter + contour) / 12). The jitter is ±2 semitones from a hash of the syllable, so it's\n  deterministic. The contour: questions rise +4 on the last 2 syllables, exclamations +3 and +30% volume, statements\n  fall −2 at the end, ALL-CAPS words +2 and louder.\n- The voice is monophonic per speaker: a new syllable cuts the previous one. A lowpass at 3.5 kHz on the bus softens\n  it.\n- Build the nodes on `game.audio.ctx` (null until the first key press or click, and always null headless: then plan\n  only). For `pulse` and `pulse12` waves, reuse the engine's PeriodicWave (`audio._pulse(.25)`, `audio._pulse(.125)`).\n  For noise, reuse `audio._noise`. Connect to the `voices` bus (E6).\n\n**Speaker profiles** (`{ base Hz, rate syl/s, wave, formant, breath, style }`):\n\n| Speaker | Profile |\n|---|---|\n| CHET THUNDERBUNS | 125, 13, saw, 0.95, 0.05, simlish, excited contours |\n| LOU DIGGS | 105, 9, triangle, 0.9, 0.08, simlish, falling contours |\n| COACH GUS BLUSTER | 140, 14, square, 1.0, 0.15, animalese, shouty |\n| MAMA PEARL | 210, 10, triangle, 1.15, 0.05, animalese, warm |\n| HAP HAZARD | 165, 12, saw, 1.05, 0.03, animalese |\n| BROCK STERLING III | 150, 11, pulse, 1.0, 0.02, animalese, smug (rise then fall) |\n| MONTGOMERY STERLING II | 115, 8, saw, 0.92, 0.06, animalese, pompous |\n| BIG BERTHA | 190, 9, square, 1.1, 0.1, animalese |\n| GATOR GUIDRY | 120, 10, saw, 0.95, 0.12, animalese |\n| LUMEN LOPEZ | 140, 11, pulse, 1.0, 0.02, animalese |\n| ZAP ZAPATA | 190, 16, pulse12, 1.1, 0.0, animalese |\n| DJ HOOT | 130, 12, pulse, 1.0, 0.04, animalese, vibrato |\n| COACH ICEBERG | 100, 6, sine, 0.85, 0.05, animalese, slow and solemn |\n| AVALANCHE | growls only: lowpass noise + a 70 Hz sine, no syllables |\n| THE MOUNTAIN | 90, 7, square, 0.8, 0.1, animalese |\n| refs | 150, 12, square, 1.0, 0.0, animalese, short |\n| QBs | the QB's own profile, cadence \"SET... HUT!\" (2–4 syllables + a strong plosive \"HUT\" on the snap) |\n| crowd chant | 6 voices, ±15 cents detuned, simlish |\nVoices are on whenever text is spoken: subtitles always show. Settings: Voices volume (the `voices` bus), Commentary\n(On / Subtitles only / Off). Headless, the synth schedules nothing and returns the plan, so tests can check that\nsyllables were planned.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Screens and UX (10-foot, controller-first)\n\n### 14.1 Rules for every screen\n- **Text size** on the 480×270 / 427×240 canvas: everything the player must read is `E.font` scale 2 (14 px glyphs,\n  56–84 px on a TV). Secondary text is scale 1 with a shadow. Titles are scale 3–5. `font: 'tiny'` only for jersey\n  numbers, signs and dev overlays.\n- **Few, big things:** at most 6–7 focusable items on a screen, one primary action highlighted, big cards, and\n  generous spacing. Focus shows as a pulsing border plus the `▸` marker. Results have at most 4 stat tiles, team\n  select 4 bars, records 6 rows a page. No tables of numbers outside the Dev mode.\n- **Navigation:** d-pad and stick with repeat (`input.repeat`); confirm and back; mouse hover moves the focus and a\n  click selects; the wheel scrolls lists. Every screen has a way back (○, Esc or the on-screen BACK).\n- **The prompt bar:** every screen's bottom edge shows its actions as chips for the current device (§9.3).\n- **Safe margins:** HUD and menus stay 8 px in from the edges (`T.safeMargin`, TV overscan) and clear of\n  `game.cornerArea()`.\n- **Contrast:** text over the field sits on dark translucent panels (`E.ui.box`, alpha 0.85) or has an outline.\n- **The HTML screens** (pause, settings, controls, help, dev) use the engine Shell with the 10-foot scale (E9: body\n  text ≥ 22 px at 1080p, buttons ≥ 56 px tall). Theme: `{ ink: '#f4f0e6', paper: 'rgba(14,16,34,.92)', accent:\n  '#ffb02e', muted: '#a9a6c0', danger: '#ff6a5a', line: 'rgba(255,176,46,.35)', font: 'system-ui, sans-serif',\n  titleFont: '\"Arial Black\", Impact, system-ui, sans-serif' }`: a sports-broadcast look.\n- **Every screen works with keyboard alone, pad alone, and mouse plus keyboard.** Tests drive them all three ways\n  (§21.3).\n\n### 14.2 Title\n- Tumbleweed Field at dusk in the broadcast view. Two lines of players face each other at midfield in stance, Fumbles\n  dances, the crowd sways. Every 6–8 s a scripted gag plays (Hap throws, a lineman catches it with his face; the\n  kicker whiffs; Fumbles catches fire).\n- The logo animates in (§12.8). \"PRESS START\" pulses with chips. Before any input, show both chips (Enter and ✕);\n  after that, the device's. The version label (`E.versionLabel()` + the game's version) is small at the bottom left.\n- 10 s idle starts the attract mode (§15.4). Touch-only devices: §14.12.\n\n### 14.3 Main menu\nSTORY: THE FIASCO CUP (Continue, New Game), EXHIBITION, PRACTICE, HALL OF FIASCO, OPTIONS (opens\n`game.menu('settings')`), ABOUT & CREDITS. A big vertical list on the left. On the right, a live vignette for the\nfocused item: the bracket board, two helmets clashing, a tackling dummy getting flattened, the trophy case, spinning\ngears, the announcers' booth. 10 s idle starts the attract mode.\n\n### 14.4 Exhibition setup (two steps)\n1. **CONTROLLERS & TEAMS.** Three columns: HOME (left), CPU (middle), AWAY (right). `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`:\n   Start on a free pad, or 2 on the keyboard (the right half), joins. Each player's device icon (a pad with P#, the\n   keyboard, or a keyboard half) starts in the middle. Left and right move it to a side. Up and down change that\n   side's team (the first human on a side chooses). The team panel shows the helmet (a portrait of a rig in that\n   helmet, turning slowly), the logo, the name, 4 rating bars, and the star player's portrait and special. ✕ readies.\n   A side with no human is the CPU, and P1 changes its team with △. When everyone is ready, go to step 2.\n2. **GAME OPTIONS.** Stadium (the home team's by default; arrows cycle with a preview), weather (Stadium / Clear /\n   Rain / Snow), quarter length, difficulty, then the big **PLAY BALL!** button.\nA coin toss starts the match. After the results: REMATCH, CHANGE TEAMS, MAIN MENU.\n\n### 14.5 The play-call screen (`53-playcall.js`)\n- **Layout:** a full-width panel when one side is human. With humans on both sides, split halves (home left, away\n  right), each player's choice hidden from the other: after picking, a panel turns its cards face down (\"READY\").\n  Co-op on one side: one panel with two cursors.\n- **The top bar:** the team logo, OFFENSE or DEFENSE, the down and distance, the ball spot, the play clock (big\n  digits, red under 4 s) and the team's Fiasco Meter.\n- **Tabs:** offense RUN | PASS | TRICK | SPECIAL (punt, field goal, fake punt, kneel). Defense BASE | PRESSURE |\n  SPECIAL (prevent, goal line, FG block). Kickoffs, PATs and returns show their own choices. L1/R1 or Q/E change the\n  tab; so does moving past the grid's edge, or a click on a tab.\n- **The grid:** 3×2 cards (§12.8). The focused card scales to 1.08. ✕ picks. □ flips (a FLIP badge, the routes\n  mirrored). The COACH SAYS card has a ★.\n- **The card row:** △ moves the focus to the 3 held Fiasco Cards, ✕ arms one (only one; greyed when it doesn't fit),\n  and ○ goes back to the grid.\n- **Co-op:** player 2's cursor picks his receiver (□ cycles).\n- **The timer:** when it runs out, the COACH SAYS play is picked.\n- **Behind:** the field, dimmed. The music is HUDDLE UP.\n\n### 14.6 The HUD (`41-hud.js`)\n- **The score bug** (bottom center, broadcast style, about 230×20): logo, abbreviation and score for each team, a\n  possession arrow, the quarter, the clock, the down and distance, and the ball spot. A thin Fiasco Meter bar under\n  each team, and flame icons for Blazing athletes. The scores flip on change.\n- **Player panels** for each local human, in their own corner (P1 top left, P2 top right, P3 bottom left above the\n  caption, P4 bottom right above the strip): the name tag, a big turbo bar (60×6), the held card count and SPECIAL\n  READY.\n- **The prompt strip** (§9.3), the caption (§11.3), center event text, the play clock in PRESNAP (from 5 s), and the\n  REPLAY overlay.\n- **In the world:** rings under held athletes; receiver chips with their glyphs; the pass target marker; the line of\n  scrimmage and first-down lines; the kick meter; tags; the down marker on the near sideline shows the down.\n- The HUD hides during replays (except the REPLAY overlay) and cutscenes.\n\n### 14.7 The kick meter\n- An arc meter (64×36 px) above the kicker in screen space. The power fill goes green, yellow, red. The accuracy\n  needle crosses a center band.\n- An aim line on the field: a dashed line from the ball along the aim, with the predicted landing marker for punts\n  and kickoffs. FGs show it in the Kick Cam with the posts in view.\n- Prompts step through ✕ KICK, ✕ SET POWER, ✕ SET AIM.\n\n### 14.8 Results\n- FINAL, both logos and scores huge, a winner banner.\n- PLAYER OF THE GAME: a portrait, his name and 2 key stats.\n- 4 stat tiles: total yards, turnovers, biggest hit (launch in yards), longest play.\n- Buttons: WATCH HIGHLIGHTS, then CONTINUE (story), or REMATCH / MAIN MENU (exhibition). In a hub session, the\n  results stay up under the hub's own results overlay.\n\n### 14.9 Hall of Fiasco (records)\n- `E.store('ff:records')`, 6 per page: longest TD, longest FG, biggest hit, most points in a game, most sacks in a\n  game, the fastest Fiasco Cup, and each chapter's stars.\n- Each row shows the team logo, the player, the value and the date.\n- Updated after exhibition and story games. Never written in hub sessions or the attract mode.\n\n### 14.10 About & Credits\nPages turned with ←/→ (L1/R1, clicks on the arrows), big text, scrolled with up/down when long:\n1. SOURCE INFORMATION: every field of TASK.md's list.\n2. TESTING FEEDBACK: the 4 answers.\n3. ENGINE FEEDBACK: the 6 answers.\n4. PROMPT FEEDBACK: the 4 answers.\n5. CREDITS: the engine my-3D2dge (MIT, michaelcrosato), the AI Hub client, motion clips (Quaternius CC0, Mesh2Motion\n   CC0, the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database), store props (Kenney CC0), and who built it and from what\n   (`PLAN.md`).\n6. ENGINE CHANGES: the summary from ENGINE-CHANGES.md.\n\nAll of it comes from `window.FF_ABOUT` (§23). Never type these texts in two places.\n\n### 14.11 Settings\nThe game's own (group `Game`, through `menu.settings`): Difficulty (Rookie / Pro / Fiasco; Pro), Quarter length\n(1:00 / 1:30 / 2:00 / 3:00 / 5:00; 2:00), Camera (Broadcast / Sky Cam / Blimp), Replays (All big plays / Touchdowns\nonly / Off), Commentary (On / Subtitles only / Off), Voices (slider, 0.8), Crowd (slider, 0.7), Auto-switch on\ndefense (On), Show routes (On: your receivers' route lines while you're the QB), Pass target marker (On), Late hits\n(On), Big Heads (Off; locked until the story is finished, or unlocked in the Dev mode).\nPlus the engine's: Display (full screen, pixel scaling, the screen filter: Clean / Scanlines / CRT), Sound, Comfort\n(reduce motion, screen shake, flashes), and **Controller: Button symbols (PlayStation / Xbox; PlayStation)** (E2).\nEverything applies live. The Controls screen (engine) rebinds every action per player.\n\n### 14.12 Touch-only devices\n- **Detection:** `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)')` matches, `(any-pointer: fine)` doesn't, `navigator.maxTouchPoints`\n  > 0, no gamepad is connected, and no key has been pressed.\n- **Then:** turn the touch controls off (`game.input.touchEnabled = false`, the Touch setting hidden, no scene\n  declares `touch` buttons). The title replaces PRESS START with **CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED** and a big\n  **SETTINGS** button (a tap calls `game.menu('settings')`). The corner's menu button stays. The attract mode runs\n  after 10 s as usual, and a tap goes back to the title.\n- **Unlock** the game the moment a gamepad connects (`gamepadconnected`, or `input.lastDevice === 'gamepad'`) or a key\n  is pressed.\n- In a hub session on such a device, the card says the same, and that seat is played by the AI until a controller or\n  keyboard input arrives. Don't call `AIHub.fail`, because from a host it would void the round.\n\n### 14.13 The pause menu\nResume (engine), then the game's items: Dev Mode, Restart (exhibition and practice). Then the engine's Settings,\nControls, Full screen, Help, and \"Quit to menu\" (made by `menu.quit: 'menu'`; don't add a quit item of your own).\nIn story mode, Quit to menu returns to the story board. Help comes from our Shell's `screens.help`: the\ncontrols by context in short sections (Offense, Ball carrier, Defense, Kicking, Menus), each line a key and what it\ndoes, for the device last used. Cutscenes aren't pausable: hold ○ or Esc for 1 s (a fill ring) to skip one.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Modes\n\n### 15.1 Story: THE FIASCO CUP (`54-scene-story.js`, `14-data-story.js`)\n**The hub** is the bracket board, a corkboard in the Fiascos' locker room. It holds 5 chapter cards, each locked,\nopen or done with 0–3 stars. Below them sit the Fiasco Card collection and the **loadout**: up to 3 cards picked for\nthe next chapter. CONTINUE plays the next chapter. A chapter runs: intro cutscene → gameplay (a scenario) → halftime\ncutscene (chapters 2 and 5) → outro cutscene on a win, or the loss screen → reward → autosave\n(`game.saves.autosave()`, with the place, the chapter and the time played). Main menu CONTINUE loads\n`game.saves.latest()`. Wire the slots once at boot:\n`game.saves.write = () => ({ data: { story: story.state(), done, stars, collection, loadout, unlocked, playTime },\nplace: 'THE FIASCO CUP', level: nextChapter })` and `game.saves.read = s => restoreStory(s.data)`, with\n`menu: { saves: { version: 1 } }` so a future version can `migrate`. Records and settings live in `E.store`, not in\nthe slots.\n\n| # | Chapter | Stadium | Opponent | Scenario | Win condition | Shows | Reward |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | PROLOGUE | Tumbleweed, night | — | a cutscene | — | the stakes | — |\n| 1 | TRYOUTS | Tumbleweed, dusk | the Scrubs | drills D1–D9 (§15.2) | finish D1–D8; D9 scored | every control | HOT SAUCE, SUPER FOOT |\n| 2 | THE MUD BOWL | Hog Wallow (rain, mud) | Mudhogs | a full game, 4 × 1:30 | win | run defense, tackling, fumbles, weather, halftime choice | MEGA HIT |\n| 3 | LIGHTS OUT | Afterglow (night) | Nite Owls | from the start of Q3, 1:30 quarters, trailing 7–21, Owls kick off | win | passing, pass defense, Hot Streak, the LIGHTS OUT event at the 4th play of Q3 | ROCKET CLEATS, GLUE GLOVES |\n| 4 | FROSTBITE | Frostbite (snow, ice) | Yetis | Q4 with 1:30 left, trailing 17–20, your ball 1st & 20 at your 20 | win (a FG ties: sudden death OT) | the two-minute drill, sidelines, the clock, laterals, ice, Snowplow | MASCOT MAYHEM |\n| 5 | THE FIASCO BOWL | Mega Dome | Dynasty | a full game, 4 × 2:00 | win | everything, both bosses, the halftime show | the trophy, the ending, the credits, Big Heads |\n\n- The collection starts with BRICK WALL and BUTTERFINGERS.\n- **Difficulty** per chapter: ch2 Rookie+, ch3 Pro−, ch4 Pro, ch5 Pro+ (a blend of the §8.4 columns), shifted one\n  step by the Difficulty setting.\n- **Stars:** 1 for the win, +1 for the bonus (ch2: force 2 fumbles; ch3: win by 7+; ch4: score with 20+ s left; ch5:\n  hold the Dynasty to 14 or fewer), +1 for no turnovers.\n- **Loss:** a \"THAT'S A FIASCO!\" screen with the coach's loss line and RETRY, RETRY EASIER (one step lower for this\n  chapter) or STORY MENU.\n\n**Cutscenes.** A cutscene is `{ set, stadium, cast: [{ who, at: [x, y], facing, pose }], camera: { view, focus,\nzoom }, node }`. Its node is an `E.Story` node list. `event` steps stage it: move an actor (a tween), set a pose or\nmocap clip, play an sfx, FX, a camera tween, a wait. The dialog is an `E.Dialog` with a live portrait of the speaker's\nrig and `voice: ch => FF.voice.letter(ch, profile)` (E5). Hold ○ or Esc to skip to the end, keeping flags and\nrewards. Sets are small TileMaps in the `field` view, built in code:\n- **LOCKER ROOM:** a row of lockers, benches, the chalkboard, the cooler and a team banner, a flickering lamp\n  (light). It is recolored for each team.\n- **OFFICE:** Mama Pearl's Pickle Barn: pickle barrels, a desk, a framed photo of the 1974 team, a lamp.\n- **PRESS ROOM:** the podium with mics, a backdrop wall painted with logos, chairs, TV cameras, camera flashes\n  (`game.flash` at 0.1 strength, honoring the flashes setting).\n- **FIELD:** the match stadium itself at midfield, with the trophy stage for the ending.\nThe lines are in §18.6.\n\n### 15.2 Tutorial and practice drills (`55-scene-drills.js`, `15-data-drills.js`)\n| Drill | Setup | Goal | Teaches |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| D1 RUN THE CONES | Hap at the 20, 5 cones zigzagging, a finish gate 15 yd past the last cone | pass within 1.5 yd of each cone in order and cross the gate within 9 s | move, turbo |\n| D2 HIKE & HANDOFF | offense, 3 dummies, HB DIVE called | hike, the RB takes the ball, gain 10 yd | hike, handoff, running |\n| D3 MOVES | the RB in a lane against 3 scrubs (a standing tackler, a frontal tackler, a diver), the goal line at 25 yd | beat each with the right move, then dive into the end zone | spin, stiff arm, hurdle, dive |\n| D4 PASSING | QB, 3 receivers on SLANTS, 2 slow defenders | complete 2 passes, then a Rocket Pass to the covered man | receiver buttons, Rocket Pass |\n| D5 CATCHING | you are Wheels (WR); the coach lobs high balls | catch 2 of 3 with a jump | jump catch, the target marker |\n| D6 DEFENSE | defense against a scrub runner, then a scrub QB | switch to the nearest defender and tackle; Big Hit to force a fumble and recover it; intercept a pass with a jump | switch, tackle, Big Hit, fumble recovery, INT |\n| D7 KICKING | a 37-yd FG, then a kickoff | make the FG; kick off 50+ yd | the meter, the Kick Cam |\n| D8 PLAY CALL | the play-call screen, HOT SAUCE given | pick a PASS play, flip it, use HOT SAUCE, run it Blazing | play calling, cards, Hot Streak |\n| D9 SCRIMMAGE | one drive against the Scrubs from your 30 | score a TD in 4 downs | all of it together |\n- Each drill opens with a big instruction card (2 lines with chips) and Coach Bluster's line, and shows a progress\n  counter. Success gives a star burst and praise. A failure restarts at once (b2 also restarts). After 2 failures,\n  \"□ SKIP DRILL\" appears.\n- Every drill has a `bot(ctx)` that completes it through intents. The tests and the attract mode use it.\n- **PRACTICE** (main menu) offers a grid of the 9 drills, any of them replayable, with best times kept.\n\n### 15.3 Exhibition\nSetup (§14.4), the coin toss, the match, the results, then REMATCH, CHANGE TEAMS or MAIN MENU. 1–4 local humans in\nany split, or CPU vs CPU (no humans: a \"spectate\" exhibition; P1 can press Start to take over a side).\n\n### 15.4 The attract mode (`56-scene-attract.js`, `17-data-attract.js`)\n- **Trigger:** 10 s with no input on the title, the main menu or the exhibition setup, with no Shell screen open.\n  Input means: any action pressed by any player, a click, the pointer moving more than 6 px, the wheel, a touch, or a\n  gamepad axis past the dead zone.\n- **Exit:** any input goes back to the screen it came from with a quick fade, and that input is consumed.\n- **Content:** a looping director, about 100 s a cycle. Each segment is a scenario with forced play calls and the\n  cheats it needs (TASK.md allows the attract AI to cheat freely):\n\n| # | Segment | Where | Shows | Cheats |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Logo sting, 3 s | — | the title logo cracking | — |\n| 2 | Kickoff return, 8 s | Tumbleweed, dusk; Fiascos vs Mudhogs | the meter, a return with a spin and a stiff arm | returner god mode |\n| 3 | Play call, 3 s | same | the play-call screen, both cursors picking DEEP POST and COVER 2, HOT SAUCE armed | — |\n| 4 | Blazing TD, 10 s | same | a deep pass, a jump catch, flames, the TD, a dance, the replay (field and iso) | forceCatch, noTackle |\n| 5 | Mud fumble, 8 s | Hog Wallow, rain | Bertha's MUDSLIDE, a Big Hit fumble, a pile-up | forceFumble |\n| 6 | Kick Cam FG, 6 s | Hog Wallow | a 52-yd FG, DOINK off the upright and in | forced accuracy |\n| 7 | Pick six, 7 s | Afterglow, night | Zap's AFTERBURNER, an INT returned for a TD, a late hit, a ref flattened | forceINT |\n| 8 | Mascot Mayhem, 6 s | Afterglow | Hooty runs on and sacks the QB | — |\n| 9 | Lights out, 5 s | Afterglow | glowing uniforms in the dark | — |\n| 10 | Snowplow, 6 s | Frostbite, snow | Avalanche's SNOWPLOW sack, divots, breath puffs | — |\n| 11 | Story snippet, 6 s | the press room | Brock's \"pickles\" line with his babble voice | — |\n| 12 | Halftime show, 5 s | Mega Dome | cheerleaders, the band, pyro, the blimp | — |\n| 13 | Seismic Sack, 5 s | Mega Dome | The Mountain's slam and shockwave | — |\n| 14 | Champions, 5 s | Mega Dome | the trophy stage, confetti, the bucket dump | — |\n| 15 | Hall of Fiasco, 5 s | — | the records | — |\n| 16 | Title, 5 s | — | the title, then the loop again | — |\n\n- **The overlay:** DEMO PLAY at the top, PRESS ANY BUTTON pulsing at the bottom, the ARCADE ATTRACT music,\n  commentary on with voices at 60%. Nothing is saved and no records are written.\n- Both teams are played by the normal brains. The director only forces play calls, the listed outcomes and the story\n  snippet's camera. The attract mode runs the same on touch-only devices.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Multiplayer (`80-net.js`)\n\n### 16.1 How TASK.md's multiplayer line is met\n- **The game on its own:** full local multiplayer for 1–4 on one machine (pads and a shared keyboard), in exhibition.\n  Story and practice are single player.\n- **In AI Hub:** sessions of the AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in **shared-world** mode for 1–4 players, each on their\n  own device. One player plays against the CPU; 2–4 play head to head or 2-on-1 and 2-on-2 with CPU teammates. The\n  hub's relay only exists inside a session, so outside one the game is unaffected (`AIHub.active` is false) and plays\n  as above.\n\n### 16.2 The page head\n`session.js` goes right after `<meta charset>`, unmodified and first:\n```html\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>/*! aihub-session 1.4.0 … (the whole file, unmodified) */</script>\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"6\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Outscore the other side over one half of arcade football\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Stick/WASD run · ✕/Space hike, dive, tackle · ✕○□△ or 1-4 throw · R2/Shift turbo\">\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\"><meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\"><meta name=\"hub:source\" content=\"American football\">\n```\n\n### 16.3 A session, step by step (when `AIHub.active`)\n1. Boot skips the title, attract mode and menus. The boot steps build everything.\n2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. If it returns `null`, play\n   as on its own.\n3. **The setup** comes from `AIHub.rng('setup')`, the same on every copy: a stadium (one of the 5), two different\n   teams (never the Scrubs), the stadium's weather. Seats map to sides: 0 home, 1 away, 2 home, 3 away. With one\n   player, seat 0 is home against a CPU away team.\n4. **The card scene** (`57-scene-hubcard.js`):\n   - It shows the two helmets facing off over the stadium backdrop, the team logos, the players by side in their seat\n     colors with ready ticks (`card.ready`), \"HOW TO WIN: OUTSCORE THEM OVER ONE HALF\", this device's controls as\n     chips, and the countdown (`card.left`; PAUSED while `card.paused`).\n   - **READY** (Enter, ✕ or a tap) calls `AIHub.answer('ready')`. **MORE TIME** (△, T or a tap) calls\n     `AIHub.answer('more')`; pressing again takes it back.\n   - It has no menus or choices, and nothing on it changes the round. Any randomness comes from `AIHub.rng('card')`.\n   - Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the match is built, without a key press.\n5. On `start`, show a 3-2-1 countdown over the opening kickoff setup while `AIHub.time() < 0`. Kick off at ≥ 0.\n6. **The match** is one half: 2 quarters of 1:30. A play takes about 17 s of real time for about 16 s of game clock\n   (live play plus the runoff), plus about 8 s per TD celebration, so plan on 1.3 real seconds per clock second. When\n   `info.limitMs` is short, use `quarter = clamp((limitMs / 1000 − 60) / 2 / 1.3, 45, 90)` seconds (a 300 s limit\n   gives 90; a 200 s limit gives 54). No overtime: a tie is a tie.\n7. At the end of Q2, or on `time-up` (within `graceMs`), the host calls\n   `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, stats, awards })` and sends a last reliable snapshot with `over: 1`. Every copy\n   shows the final score (§14.8) until `end`.\n8. On `end`: take no more input, send nothing, `audio.music(null)` and mute. `reason` picks the message: finished,\n   ROUND CANCELLED (void, host-left) or YOU SAT THIS ONE OUT (sat-out).\n\n### 16.4 Roles\n- **Host** (`info.isHost`): runs the sim, the AI and the rules for every seat. Its own seat, if any, plays locally\n  with no prediction needed.\n- **Others:** predict their own held athlete, render snapshots, and run their local UI (the play call and the kick\n  meter).\n- **Server** (`info.server`): a host with no player of its own. It runs the same host code with every seat remote,\n  draws nothing (`draw` fills the screen once and returns), plays no audio, and owns no camera or input.\n- **Control** (§9.4) is keyed by seat. The host owns the assignment and broadcasts changes as events.\n\n### 16.5 Messages, rates, prediction (formats in Appendix B)\n- Client → host: `i`, its state and inputs, unreliable at 15 Hz. `call`, its play call, reliable. `kick`, its kick\n  meter result, reliable.\n- Host → all: `s`, the snapshot, unreliable at **14 Hz**, **at most 1,100 bytes** (under the hub's ~1,200\n  fragmentation size). `e`, events, reliable, at most 4/s: phase changes with the plays, control changes, scores,\n  stats, card draws, `over`. One `send()` to everyone counts as one message.\n- **The budget:** each copy sends at most 18 messages/s. The host sends 14 + ≤ 4. A client sends 15 + about 1\n  reliable (`call` or `kick`). The hub allows 20/s with bursts of 40, and the client drops the rest. `FF.net.budget` is\n  a token bucket at 18/s that refuses sends past it: it drops the oldest queued unreliable message, never a reliable\n  one, and delays the next snapshot rather than skipping an event. Tests check this.\n- **Client prediction** (the arena example's rule: \"your controls never wait for the network\"):\n  - The client moves its held athlete locally with the same movement code, plus a soft push from the snapshot's\n    nearby athletes. It sends its position, velocity, facing, intent bits and an **action log**: the last 8 actions\n    with sequence numbers. The host runs each new sequence number once, so losing a packet loses nothing.\n  - The host accepts the reported position unless the athlete is `locked` (tackled, engaged, in an action the host\n    started, a dead ball) or it is farther than top speed × elapsed + 8 units from the host's last position.\n    Otherwise it keeps its own and flags `corr`.\n  - The client snaps to the host when the difference is over 2 yd, or `corr` or `locked` is set. While locked it\n    stops predicting.\n- **Interpolation:** others and the ball render 100 ms behind the newest snapshot, interpolated, extrapolated up to\n  150 ms when late.\n- **The play call:** each client opens its play-call UI on the host's `e: phase PLAYCALL` and sends `call`. The host\n  waits for every human side or its own timer, then sends `e: phase PRESNAP` with both plays.\n- **The kick meter** runs on the kicking human's copy. Its `{ power, acc, aim }` goes as `kick` and the host executes\n  it.\n- **Leaving:** on `leave { id }`, that seat's athletes go back to the AI (a CPU tag over them) and the match goes on.\n  When the host leaves, the hub voids the round and the clients show ROUND CANCELLED on `end`.\n\n### 16.6 Session rules (the protocol's, and what they mean here)\n- No title, menus, attract mode or story. No quit or restart (`menu.quit: false`, no Restart item, no Dev Mode item).\n- **No world freeze:** `AIHub.active` is known from the page's first line, so in a session the game is constructed\n  with `new E.Game({ autoPause: false, ... })` and the match scene is `pausable: false`. The match scene reads the\n  `pause` action itself: Options (or Esc) opens a small local canvas overlay with SETTINGS\n  (`game.menu('settings', { pause: false })`, E8) and CONTROLS. While it is open, that player's athlete is driven by\n  the AI.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's:** each step, while Pad8 (Create) is held on any pad, call\n  `input.suppress('pause')` and `input.suppress('start')`.\n- **No player-to-player communication:** celebrations are random, there are no taunts, and names come only from the","numLines":450,"startLine":1300,"totalLines":2515}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014rjzS2LUBM2b1t21BWpHFm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
